"""Data-loss prevention: credential blocking and PII redaction.

Two jobs, with different severities:

* **Block.** Credential-looking patterns (API keys, private keys, tokens,
  JWTs, DB connection strings) in tool arguments cause the executor to DENY
  the call before any policy evaluation or execution. Secrets must never
  leave the boundary.
* **Redact.** PII patterns (SSN, credit-card numbers with a Luhn check,
  emails, phone numbers) are replaced with ``[REDACTED:<rule>]`` in anything
  written to the action log or the approval queue. PII in logs is a breach
  waiting to happen; the decision is logged, the value is not.

A conservative entropy tripwire blocks high-entropy tokens that appear next
to credential keywords (``password``, ``api_key``, ...) even when they do
not match a known format.

ENCODING-AWARE MATCHING (F2, extended F8, F428, F429, F430). The credential patterns above
are syntactic, so an attacker can smuggle a credential past them with one
encoding step (hex, base64, base32, URL-encoding, JSFuck, text escapes,
JS string expressions).
``find_credential``
therefore decodes likely encodings BEFORE pattern matching, bounded to 2
layers: each layer finds candidate encoded runs, decodes only those that
validate (base64/base32 round-trip / even-length hex / percent-decoding
that changes the text / strict static JSFuck evaluation / strict
static JS-expression evaluation / strict
backslash-escape, numeric-HTML-entity, and UTF-16-null decodes / NFKC +
zero-width confusable normalization) AND decode to
mostly-printable ASCII, then re-runs
the credential rules on the decoded variants. Innocent base64-looking text
(random tokens, hashes) decodes to binary and is rejected by the printable
check, so it is never blocked; text whose DECODED form matches a credential
regex is blocked because the decoded content genuinely contains a
credential-shaped string. The JSFuck decoder is a strict bounded
evaluator, never a JS engine: undecodable runs are ignored, and
Function() (arbitrary code) always fails closed. The text-escape decoders
(F429) are likewise strict static transforms -- malformed, surrogate, or
out-of-range sequences decode to nothing, never to a guess. The
JS-expression decoder (F430) is a strict bounded recursive-descent
parser for quoted '+' concatenation, template literals (F457:
${...} interpolation over string-literal payloads only),
String.fromCharCode, .concat()/.repeat(), (F434) array-literal
+ .join(sep), (F437) .reduce(...) array-assembly plus identity .map(...)
/ constant-true .filter(...) passthroughs, (F473) array-receiver
.concat(...) returning a fresh array for the chain, and (F443)
Array(N).fill(x) construction plus .filter(Boolean) truthiness
filtering -- never eval; undecodable runs are ignored and bare
literals are not rewritten.

FORM VARIANTS (F8). Every candidate string (the original text and each
decoded variant) is also checked whitespace-stripped and reversed: those
are deterministic, precise transforms of a KNOWN-FORMAT credential, and a
match on any form blocks. The joined buffer of ALL string params is
scanned too (chunked-across-params), with regex rules only — never the
entropy tripwire, which stays on the original text (see below).

Deliberate boundary: the entropy tripwire runs on the ORIGINAL text only.
Running a heuristic tripwire on decoded variants would amplify its
false-positive surface (any printable decode containing a keyword plus a
long token); known-format credential rules on decoded text are precise.
An unknown-format credential smuggled via encoding next to a keyword is a
documented residual, not a silent bypass — the known-format defeat (F2)
is closed. Custom substitution ciphers (arbitrary bijections, F8 residual)
are NOT deterministically catchable and remain a documented residual.

Field-level policy (see ``scan_tool_args``): some fields are audit-relevant
and stay readable (email recipients, URLs, file paths); payloads and file
contents are summarized to ``{bytes, sha256}`` so secret material never
reaches the log at all.
"""

import base64
import hashlib
import json
import math
import re
import unicodedata
from urllib.parse import parse_qsl, quote, unquote, unquote_plus, urlparse, urlsplit

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- patterns

_CREDENTIAL_RULES = [
    # (rule_id, compiled regex) -- a match BLOCKS the call.
    ("dlp-aws-access-key", re.compile(r"AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}")),
    ("dlp-github-token",
     re.compile(r"(?:ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}|github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,})")),
    ("dlp-stripe-live-key", re.compile(r"sk_live_[A-Za-z0-9]{16,}")),
    ("dlp-stripe-test-key", re.compile(r"sk_test_[A-Za-z0-9]{16,}")),
    ("dlp-private-key",
     re.compile(r"-----BEGIN [A-Z0-9 ]*PRIVATE KEY-----")),
    ("dlp-jwt",
     re.compile(r"eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}")),
    ("dlp-slack-token", re.compile(r"xox[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{10,}")),
    ("dlp-google-api-key", re.compile(r"AIza[0-9A-Za-z_-]{35}")),
    ("dlp-connection-string",
     re.compile(r"(?i)(?:postgres(?:ql)?|mysql|mongodb(?:\+srv)?|redis)://[^/\s]*@[^/\s]+")),
]

# F91 (Wave 25): the PII patterns below use digit-only boundaries
# (``(?<![0-9])`` / ``(?![0-9])``) instead of ``\b``. The AGT
# redaction-evasion class — affixing a word character to a sensitive
# value (``123-45-6789_old``, ``old_123-45-6789``) — defeated ``\b``
# and leaked the value into logs/approvals in cleartext. Digits and
# dashes adjacent to the pattern still suppress the match (a longer
# numeric run is not an SSN/phone/card); letters and underscores are
# treated as affixes and the sensitive core is still redacted, which
# is the fail-closed direction for log redaction.
_PII_RULES = [
    # (rule_id, compiled regex) -- a match is REDACTED in logs/approvals.
    ("pii-ssn", re.compile(r"(?<![0-9])\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}(?![0-9])")),
    ("pii-email",
     re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}")),
    ("pii-phone",
     re.compile(r"(?<![0-9])(?:\+?1[-.\s]?)?\(?\d{3}\)?[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}(?![0-9])")),
]

_CARD_RE = re.compile(r"(?<![0-9])(?:\d[ -]*?){13,19}(?![0-9])")

# F16: the entropy tripwire's keyword list is matched in a NORMALIZED
# space — lowercase, leet-speak folded, and every whitespace RUN replaced
# by a \x00 boundary marker (no whitespace survives, but word edges do,
# so "deploy password" still has a boundary while "private key" and
# "privatekey" both match). Synonyms ("credentials"), spaced forms
# ("private key"), and leet ("p4ssw0rd" -> "password") all trip it. The
# window / token / entropy half still runs on the ORIGINAL text (match
# positions are mapped back), so the precise-credential regexes and the
# entropy behavior are untouched.
# F91 (Wave 25): the keyword boundaries are letter-only
# (``(?<![A-Za-z])`` / ``(?![A-Za-z])``) rather than ``\b``. The
# affix-evasion class (``password_old``, ``old_password``,
# ``api_key_backup``) defeated ``\b`` and let unknown-format secrets
# next to affixed keywords slip past the tripwire. Letters adjacent
# to a keyword still suppress the match — ``monkey`` / ``keyboard``
# must not trip — but ``_``/``-``/digits are treated as affixes
# (rotation-naming conventions like ``key2``), which is the
# fail-closed direction for a heuristic tripwire.
_TRIP_KEYWORDS = re.compile(
    r"(?<![A-Za-z])(password|passwd|passcode|pwd|secret|credentials?|creds|token|"
    r"api[_\-\x00]?key|private[_\-\x00]?key|key|access|auth|login|"
    r"aws_secret|client_secret)(?![A-Za-z])")

_LEET_FOLD = {
    "0": "o", "1": "l", "3": "e", "4": "a", "5": "s",
    "7": "t", "8": "b", "@": "a", "$": "s", "!": "i",
}


def _normalize_tripwire(text):
    """Normalize *text* for keyword matching.

    Returns (normalized, positions): lowercase, leet-speak folded, each
    whitespace run replaced by one \\x00 boundary marker;
    ``positions[i]`` is the index in the ORIGINAL text of
    ``normalized[i]``, so a keyword match in normalized space maps back
    to the true window for the token/entropy half.
    """
    norm, positions = [], []
    in_ws = False
    for i, ch in enumerate(text):
        if ch.isspace():
            if not in_ws:
                norm.append("\x00")
                positions.append(i)
                in_ws = True
            continue
        in_ws = False
        low = ch.lower()
        norm.append(_LEET_FOLD.get(low, low))
        positions.append(i)
    return "".join(norm), positions


def _tripwire_hit(text):
    """Entropy tripwire: a long high-entropy token sitting near a
    credential keyword (in normalized space) is an unknown key format."""
    norm, positions = _normalize_tripwire(text)
    for m in _TRIP_KEYWORDS.finditer(norm):
        ws = max(0, m.start() - 10)
        we = min(len(norm), m.end() + 80)
        window = text[positions[ws]:positions[we - 1] + 1]
        for token in _HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_RE.findall(window):
            if _shannon_entropy(token) > HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_MIN_ENTROPY:
                return True
    return False


# F99 (Wave 27): parameters shared by the keyword-adjacent entropy
# tripwire (``_tripwire_hit``, DENY effect) and the keyword-free framed
# secret tripwire (``has_high_entropy_token``, QUEUE effect via the
# policy engine). 20 chars / entropy > 4.3 is the FP-analyzed boundary
# inherited from F2/F8: hex digests (~3.7), UUIDs (~3.4) and random
# base64-of-binary fixtures (~4.26) stay under it, while unknown-format
# framed secrets (the F99 probe token: 4.375) trip it. Without a
# credential keyword nearby the signal can only justify human review,
# never a block.
#
# F106 (Wave 27): the signal covers the api_call ENDPOINT URL too
# (query values + path segments, per-segment so adjacent low-entropy
# segments never join into a phantom run through '/'/'=' — both in the
# token alphabet). F105 (Wave 27) is the accepted encoding residual:
# hex/base32 alphabets cap entropy below 4.3, so decode-and-rescan
# cannot separate honest from adversarial hex — measured 2026-09-22: an
# honest SHA-256 digest decodes to per-byte entropy 4.875, an
# adversarial 32-byte secret to 4.938 (same distribution; no threshold
# separates them). Queueing decoded-hex hits would FP-collapse on
# honest digests, which the closure's FP analysis requires to EXECUTE.
# Low-alphabet encodings of unknown-format secrets are therefore a
# documented residual of this rule, not a silent bypass.
HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_MIN_LEN = 20
HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_MIN_ENTROPY = 4.3
_HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9_+/=-]{%d,}"
                                    % HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_MIN_LEN)


def has_high_entropy_token(text):
    """Keyword-free framed-secret tripwire (F99).

    True when *text* contains a token-like run of at least
    ``HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_MIN_LEN`` chars whose Shannon entropy exceeds
    ``HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_MIN_ENTROPY``. This is the unknown-format /
    paraphrase exfil channel DLP cannot name (no credential keyword to
    anchor on), so callers must use it for require_approval (queue)
    decisions only — never deny. Scans the given text only; callers
    must not join cross-field buffers (the F2/F8 boundary: the entropy
    tripwire stays on the original text). Low-alphabet encodings
    (hex/base32) of secrets never trip by construction (alphabet cap
    4.0 < 4.3) — the documented F105 residual, not a miss.
    """
    if not text:
        return False
    for token in _HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_RE.findall(text):
        if _shannon_entropy(token) > HIGH_ENTROPY_TOKEN_MIN_ENTROPY:
            return True
    return False


def params_have_high_entropy_token(params):
    """F99: True when any string inside a (possibly nested) params
    mapping carries a high-entropy token-like run.

    The params-side half of the executor's ``high_entropy_payload``
    signal — see ``api_call_high_entropy_payload`` for the full
    composition (params + endpoint URL), which is what the executor
    and the simulator's replay recomputation must call. Single source
    of truth for the params traversal; do not reimplement the
    traversal elsewhere (a divergent copy would silently desync
    replay).
    """
    stack = [params]
    while stack:
        v = stack.pop()
        if isinstance(v, str):
            if has_high_entropy_token(v):
                return True
        elif isinstance(v, dict):
            stack.extend(v.values())
        elif isinstance(v, (list, tuple)):
            stack.extend(v)
    return False


def endpoint_url_has_high_entropy_token(endpoint):
    """F106: True when the endpoint URL's query string or path carries
    a high-entropy token-like run.

    The URL is a first-class exfil channel to the allowlisted host (the
    F94 allowlist matches hostname only), and DLP's ``find_credential``
    needs a known pattern or keyword adjacency — an unknown-format
    token in a query string or path matches neither. Scanned per query
    VALUE (``k=v`` pairs split on ``&``/``;``; keys are not content,
    the params-side convention) and per path SEGMENT (split on ``/``),
    each both raw and percent-decoded: splitting first keeps adjacent
    low-entropy segments from joining into a phantom run through
    ``/``/``=`` (both in the token alphabet) — e.g. a git-SHA path
    ``/repos/x/commits/<sha>`` stays clean — while decoding catches
    URL-encoded tokens. Fragments are not scanned (never sent to the
    server); userinfo is out of scope (noted residual).
    """
    if not endpoint or not isinstance(endpoint, str):
        return False
    try:
        parts = urlparse(endpoint)
    except Exception:
        return False
    for pair in re.split(r"[&;]", parts.query or ""):
        if not pair:
            continue
        value = pair.split("=", 1)[1] if "=" in pair else pair
        if (has_high_entropy_token(value)
                or has_high_entropy_token(unquote_plus(value))):
            return True
    for seg in (parts.path or "").split("/"):
        if not seg:
            continue
        if (has_high_entropy_token(seg)
                or has_high_entropy_token(unquote(seg))):
            return True
    return False


def api_call_high_entropy_payload(args):
    """F99/F106: the single source of truth for the executor's
    ``high_entropy_payload`` policy-context key (and the simulator's
    deterministic recomputation).

    True when the api_call's params (any nested string value) OR its
    endpoint URL (query values / path segments) carries a high-entropy
    token-like run. Do not reimplement this composition elsewhere —
    a divergent copy would silently desync replay.
    """
    args = args or {}
    return (params_have_high_entropy_token(args.get("params"))
            or endpoint_url_has_high_entropy_token(args.get("endpoint")))


def _luhn_ok(digits):
    total = 0
    for i, ch in enumerate(reversed(digits)):
        d = ord(ch) - 48
        if i % 2 == 1:
            d *= 2
            if d > 9:
                d -= 9
        total += d
    return total % 10 == 0


def _shannon_entropy(text):
    if not text:
        return 0.0
    counts = {}
    for ch in text:
        counts[ch] = counts.get(ch, 0) + 1
    n = len(text)
    return -sum((c / n) * math.log2(c / n) for c in counts.values())


# ------------------------------------------------- encoding-aware matching
# F2/F428/F429: decode likely encodings BEFORE credential pattern matching,
# bounded to 2 layers. Every candidate must VALIDATE (strict decode +
# round-trip for base64, even-length for hex, strict static evaluation for
# JSFuck, strict static decode for text escapes) and decode
# to mostly-printable ASCII;
# otherwise it is not a hidden credential, it is a random token/hash, and it
# is ignored. This keeps innocent base64-looking text unblocked.

_B64_RUN = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9+/\-_]{24,}={0,2}")
_HEX_RUN = re.compile(r"(?:[0-9a-fA-F]{2}){16,}")
_B32_RUN = re.compile(r"[A-Z2-7]{24,}={0,6}")
# F445: short base64/base64url and base32 runs (8-23 payload chars). The
# 24-char floor above is an FP guard against word-like runs; short runs
# are attempted ONLY when the strict decode round-trips AND the decoded
# text is credential-shaped (the guard made precise -- see
# _b64_short_variants / _b32_short_variants). The negative
# lookbehind/lookahead keep these from matching interior slices of
# longer runs (already handled by the long path); padding '=' is not a
# boundary char, so a padded short run still matches.
_B64_SHORT_RUN = re.compile(
    r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9+/\-_])[A-Za-z0-9+/\-_]{8,23}={0,2}"
    r"(?![A-Za-z0-9+/\-_])")
_B32_SHORT_RUN = re.compile(
    r"(?<![A-Z2-7])[A-Z2-7]{8,23}={0,6}(?![A-Z2-7])")
# F445: floor on short-run decode attempts. 8 payload chars decode to 6
# bytes (b64) / 5 bytes (b32); no credential rule can match that little
# (the shortest rule needs 15 chars: xox[baprs]- plus 10), so the floor
# only gates attempts, never blocking decisions -- same discipline as
# the F428 JSFuck threshold.
_B64_SHORT_FLOOR = 8
# F428: JSFuck is a JS-execution encoding over the alphabet []()!+- (unary
# minus included). A run of 64+ alphabet chars is essentially never
# innocent text; genuine single-char JSFuck is shorter but a single
# decoded char can never match a credential rule, so the threshold only
# gates decode ATTEMPTS, never blocking decisions.
_JSFUCK_RUN = re.compile(r"[\[\]()!+\-]{64,}")
# F429: text-escape runs. Backslash escapes (\uXXXX / \UXXXXXXXX / \xXX
# / octal / CSS \HHHHHH<space>) need only 1+ escapes before a decode is
# attempted (F432: a single escaped char decodes to the raw credential in
# any real JSON/JS/CSS parser). The ASCII-printable gate and the
# credential-rule match still protect precision: a lone escape that
# decodes to non-credential text never blocks.
# F433: CSS escapes may also terminate space-free -- per CSS Syntax L3
# 4.3.7 the escape ends at the first non-hex code point, which is NOT
# consumed (\78o -> 'x' + 'o'), and a single following whitespace
# (space, tab, LF, FF, CR, or CRLF) is consumed as part of the escape.
# The bare [0-9a-fA-F]{1,6} alternative (after the whitespace-terminated
# form) lets space-free runs be found; the strict decoders below
# validate them. NOTE: a non-whitespace terminator (e.g. '!') is NOT
# consumed, so it survives decoding and breaks consecutiveness -- only
# backslash- and whitespace-terminated escapes form runs.
_ESC_RUN = re.compile(
    r"(?:\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|U[0-9a-fA-F]{8}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}"
    r"|[0-9a-fA-F]{1,6}(?:\r\n|[ \t\n\f])"
    r"|[0-9a-fA-F]{1,6}|[0-7]{1,3}))+")
# F429: numeric HTML entities (&#x..; / &#..;), 1+ (F432: a single
# entity decodes to the raw credential in any real HTML parser).
# F436: the trailing semicolon is OPTIONAL -- a real HTML parser
# (html.unescape) recovers the legacy no-semicolon form (&#65), so a
# no-semicolon entity inside a base64 run poisons the run match while
# the parser still recovers the credential. Greedy digit consumption
# (6 hex / 7 decimal max) mirrors html.unescape; 7+ hex or 8+ decimal
# digits are always out of range, so the cap is semantically identical.
_ENTITY_RUN = re.compile(r"(?:&#[xX][0-9a-fA-F]{1,6};?|&#[0-9]{1,7};?)+")
# F429: UTF-16 null-interleaved runs (ASCII bytes separated by NULs).
_UTF16N_RUN = re.compile(r"(?:[\x20-\x7e]\x00){4,}|(?:\x00[\x20-\x7e]){4,}")
# F429: zero-width / invisible joiners stripped by the confusable
# normalizer (whole-string variant, alongside NFKC).
_ZERO_WIDTH_RUN = re.compile("[\u200b\u200c\u200d\ufeff]")
# F431: bidi-override controls (U+202A-U+202E, U+2066-U+2069). An
# attacker interleaves RLO (U+202E) so the VISUAL rendering shows the
# credential while the byte sequence does not match. The bidi
# normalizer strips every control and reverses right-to-left-governed
# runs (RLO and RLE) to visual order; LRO/LRE/isolates keep logical
# order (FSI's first-strong is content-dependent -- the strip variant
# still catches logical-order credentials inside them). Bounded,
# static, never executes.
_BIDI_RLO = "\u202e"
_BIDI_LRO = "\u202d"
_BIDI_RLE = "\u202b"
_BIDI_REVERSING = frozenset((_BIDI_RLO, _BIDI_RLE))  # RTL display order
_BIDI_EMBED = frozenset(("\u202a",))                   # LRE (LTR: no reverse)
_BIDI_ISOLATE = frozenset(("\u2066", "\u2067", "\u2068"))  # LRI, RLI, FSI
_BIDI_POP = frozenset(("\u202c", "\u2069"))            # PDF, PDI
_BIDI_ALL = (_BIDI_REVERSING | frozenset((_BIDI_LRO,)) | _BIDI_EMBED
             | _BIDI_ISOLATE | _BIDI_POP)
_MAX_BIDI_CONTROLS = 128  # bound on processed bidi controls per text
# F431: Cyrillic homoglyphs that do NOT NFKC-fold to ASCII (U+043E
# stays U+043E under NFKC). Bounded static map of verified visual
# homoglyphs, applied after NFKC in the confusable normalizer. Every
# entry is pinned by evals/w344_f431close_probes.py.
_CYRILLIC_HOMOGLYPHS = {
    "\u0430": "a",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER A
    "\u0435": "e",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER IE
    "\u043e": "o",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER O (F431 verified miss)
    "\u0440": "p",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER ER
    "\u0441": "c",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER ES
    "\u0445": "x",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER HA
    "\u0456": "i",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER BYELORUSSIAN-UKRAINIAN I
    "\u0458": "j",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER JE
    "\u0455": "s",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER DZE
    "\u043c": "m",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER EM
    "\u043a": "k",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER KA
    "\u043d": "H",  # CYRILLIC SMALL LETTER EN (looks like capital H)
    "\u0410": "A",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER A
    "\u0412": "B",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER VE
    "\u0421": "C",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER ES
    "\u0415": "E",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER IE
    "\u041d": "H",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER EN
    "\u041a": "K",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER KA
    "\u041c": "M",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER EM
    "\u041e": "O",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER O
    "\u0420": "P",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER ER
    "\u0422": "T",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER TE
    "\u0425": "X",  # CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER HA
}
_CYRILLIC_FOLD = {ord(k): v for k, v in _CYRILLIC_HOMOGLYPHS.items()}
# F435: Greek + Cherokee homoglyphs that do NOT NFKC-fold to ASCII
# (U+03BF omicron stays U+03BF under NFKC, same for the others).
# Bounded static map of verified visual homoglyphs, applied after
# NFKC in the confusable normalizer alongside the Cyrillic map.
# The four verified misses (small alpha/omicron/rho, Cherokee O)
# anchor the class; the capital siblings and the Cherokee small
# letter are the same visual-homoglyph class (Unicode confusables).
# Every entry is pinned by evals/w345_f435close_probes.py.
_GREEK_CHEROKEE_HOMOGLYPHS = {
    "\u03b1": "a",  # GREEK SMALL LETTER ALPHA (F435 verified miss)
    "\u03bf": "o",  # GREEK SMALL LETTER OMICRON (F435 verified miss)
    "\u03c1": "p",  # GREEK SMALL LETTER RHO (F435 verified miss)
    "\u13a3": "o",  # CHEROKEE LETTER O (F435 verified miss)
    "\u0391": "A",  # GREEK CAPITAL LETTER ALPHA (same visual class)
    "\u039f": "O",  # GREEK CAPITAL LETTER OMICRON (same visual class)
    "\u03a1": "P",  # GREEK CAPITAL LETTER RHO (same visual class)
    "\uab73": "o",  # CHEROKEE SMALL LETTER O (same letter as U+13A3)
}
_GREEK_CHEROKEE_FOLD = {ord(k): v for k, v in _GREEK_CHEROKEE_HOMOGLYPHS.items()}
_WS_RUN = re.compile(r"\s+")
_MAX_DECODE_BYTES = 4096  # bound on any single decoded blob
_MAX_JSFUCK_BYTES = 100000  # bound on a JSFuck run (artifact: 34635)
_MAX_CANDIDATES = 8       # bound on decode attempts per layer
# F436: bound on entities / escape runs decoded by a whole-text pass.
# One whole-text variant is a single decode attempt, so the item bound
# is a DoS guard, not an attempt budget: 64 stacked fragments still
# compose in 2 layers; beyond 64 the per-run path still tries.
_MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS = 64
# F512: bound on TOTAL _JS_EXPR_START match attempts per whole-text
# scan (decode-producing or fail-closed). The F452 closure counted
# only decode-producing attempts against _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS, which
# fixes the F452 starvation class but removed the DoS guard on
# fail-closed attempts: a hostile input with thousands of
# obfuscation-shaped-but-undecodable matches (e.g. '"a".concat(' *
# 2000, 6000 anchor matches) forces one deep recursive
# _try_js_expr_decode per match -- measured 13.45s on a 22KB input,
# red on the P5 ReDoS pins (5s threshold). The total-attempt cap
# restores the guard; it is 8x the decode budget (512) so the F452
# battery's pinned scenario (200 fail-closed statements = 400 matches
# ahead of a real expression) still decodes with margin, and the
# F452 filing scenario (64 matches) with wide margin. Honest
# residual: 512+ fail-closed matches ahead of a real expression
# reintroduce the F452 starvation shape
# (strictly beyond the documented F444 ~130+ decodable-junk
# residual); each attempt stays internally bounded (literal/run/ops
# budgets), so the cap bounds aggregate work, not just item count.
_MAX_WHOLETEXT_ATTEMPTS = 512
# F439: bound on base64 decode rounds in the decode-until-fixpoint pass.
# A credential wrapped in up to 3 base64 layers blocks; depth-4+ stays a
# documented non-goal (bounded decode budget, not a finding).
_MAX_B64_ROUNDS = 3


def _printable_ascii(data, min_ratio=0.9):
    if not data:
        return False
    good = sum(1 for b in data if 32 <= b < 127 or b in (9, 10, 13))
    return good / len(data) >= min_ratio


def _try_b64_decode(run, min_payload=24):
    """Strict base64/base64url decode with round-trip validation.

    Returns the decoded ASCII string, or None when the run is not really
    base64 (wrong alphabet after normalization, bad padding, no round-trip,
    or decodes to binary rather than text).

    min_payload is the floor on payload chars (default 24: the F2/F8-era
    FP guard against word-like runs). The F445 short path passes 8 --
    the remaining guards (strict decode, exact round-trip,
    mostly-printable ASCII) are unchanged; the credential-shape gate in
    _b64_short_variants decides which short decodes become variants.
    """
    norm = run.replace("-", "+").replace("_", "/").rstrip("=")
    if len(norm) < min_payload:
        return None
    norm += "=" * (-len(norm) % 4)
    try:
        raw = base64.b64decode(norm, validate=True)
    except Exception:
        return None
    if len(raw) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        return None
    # Round-trip: re-encoding must reproduce the candidate (modulo padding).
    # Without this, arbitrary word-char runs "decode" to garbage bytes.
    if base64.b64encode(raw).decode("ascii").rstrip("=") != run.replace(
            "-", "+").replace("_", "/").rstrip("="):
        return None
    if not _printable_ascii(raw):
        return None
    # _printable_ascii is a 0.9-ratio heuristic, so up to 10% of the
    # bytes may still be non-ASCII: guard the final decode (wave-52
    # RT19: innocent base64-looking traffic crashed the scanner here).
    try:
        return raw.decode("ascii")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        return None


_B64_ATOB_WS = " \t\n\f\r"  # ASCII whitespace the engine strips (F611)
_B64_ATOB_ALPHA = frozenset(
    "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/",
)  # strict atob alphabet: base64url chars throw (F611)


def _js_f611_atob_decode(lit):
    """Decode an atob() argument, node-exact (v24.20.0, F611).

    The engine's forgiving-base64 decode: strip ASCII whitespace,
    then strictly validate (standard base64 alphabet only -- '-'
    and '_' throw InvalidCharacterError; '=' only as 1-2 trailing
    padding chars; length % 4 != 1). The decoded bytes become the
    binary string (each byte a code point, latin-1) -- no
    printable-ASCII gate: the engine returns binary strings and
    only credential-shaped assemblies can block downstream, so a
    fragment alone is harmless. Returns the decoded str, or None
    when the engine throws (fail closed). Bounded by
    _MAX_DECODE_BYTES. Never evals.
    """
    s = "".join(c for c in lit if c not in _B64_ATOB_WS)
    if not s:
        return ""  # node: atob('') / atob('   ') -> ''
    if len(s) % 4 == 1:
        return None  # node throws InvalidCharacterError
    body = s.rstrip("=")
    pad = len(s) - len(body)
    if pad > 2 or "=" in body:
        return None  # misplaced padding: node throws
    for c in body:
        if c not in _B64_ATOB_ALPHA:
            return None  # non-alphabet char: node throws
    if not body:
        return None  # node: atob('==') / atob('=') throws
    s = body + "=" * (-len(body) % 4)
    try:
        raw = base64.b64decode(s, validate=True)
    except Exception:
        return None
    if len(raw) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        return None
    return raw.decode("latin-1")  # binary string, engine-exact


def _js_parse_f611_atob(text, k):
    """Parse `atob('LIT')` at text[k] (F611); returns (decoded, next).

    The call is the engine's base64 decode over a single string
    literal argument (ws-tolerant around the paren and arg, both
    quote styles). Decoded via _js_f611_atob_decode (node-exact,
    v24.20.0); an undecodable argument fails closed (the engine
    throws). Extra arguments are SKIPPED node-exactly -- the engine
    ignores them (verified v24.20.0: atob('eG94','x') decodes the
    first argument); the skip is paren-balanced and string-aware,
    so a paren inside an extra arg's string cannot desync it.
    Template literals anywhere in the skipped span fail closed.
    The fragment value flows into the caller's composition
    (additive concatenation, var bindings, WeakMap values). Never
    evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + len("atob"))
    if k >= n or text[k] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("atob without paren")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if k >= n or text[k] not in "'\"":
        raise _JSExprFail("atob arg not a string literal")
    lit, k = _js_parse_string(text, k)
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k < n and text[k] == ",":
        # Node ignores extra arguments: skip balanced to the
        # call's closing paren (string-aware; template fails
        # closed). A `]`/`}` at depth 0 is malformed (node throws
        # SyntaxError) -- fail closed.
        q = None
        pdepth = 0
        m = k
        while m < n:
            c = text[m]
            if q is not None:
                if c == "\\":
                    m += 2
                    continue
                if c == q:
                    q = None
            elif c in "'\"":
                q = c
            elif c == "`":
                raise _JSExprFail("atob extra arg has template")
            elif c in "([{":
                pdepth += 1
            elif c in ")]}":
                if pdepth == 0:
                    if c != ")":
                        raise _JSExprFail("atob args malformed")
                    break
                pdepth -= 1
            m += 1
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("atob args unterminated")
        if q is not None or pdepth != 0:
            raise _JSExprFail("atob args malformed")
        k = m
    if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("atob without close paren")
    decoded = _js_f611_atob_decode(lit)
    if decoded is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("atob arg not strict base64")
    return decoded, k + 1


def _b64_fixpoint_variants(text):
    """Bounded base64 decode-until-fixpoint variants (F439).

    For each base64/base64url run in *text*, decode repeatedly (at most
    _MAX_B64_ROUNDS rounds) and yield one variant per round with the run
    replaced by that round's decoded output. Decoding stops early (fail
    closed) when a round is not strict base64 (bad padding, no
    round-trip, binary output) or makes no progress, so this always
    terminates: valid decodes strictly shrink the run. These variants are
    checked directly by the strict scan and are NOT fed back into the
    layer pipeline, so the total base64 decode depth across the scan
    stays <= _MAX_B64_ROUNDS.

    F453: whitespace-tolerant spans (_iter_b64_ws_spans) seed the
    fixpoint too, as their whitespace-stripped whole-span candidate.
    A ws-b64 outer layer decoding to a non-credential-shaped inner
    payload (e.g. an inner contiguous base64 run) previously
    terminated -- the F449 _b64_ws_variants gate only emits
    credential-shaped decodes -- while base64.b64decode recovers the
    credential exactly through both layers. Seeding the fixpoint with
    the stripped candidate feeds the outer layer through the existing
    stack path: round 1 decodes the ws outer, round 2+ the inner
    stack. Each round strips whitespace from its input first
    (base64.b64decode ignores whitespace, so this is ground truth --
    a no-op for the round-1 seed of contiguous runs; rounds 2+ may
    now strip whitespace from decoded intermediates regardless of
    seed origin, a strict superset toward stdlib ground truth) --
    which also composes ws-b64(ws-b64) two-whitespace-layer stacks.
    Short seeds keep the F445 credential-shape-gated short retry, so
    the short-run gating discipline is unchanged. Contiguous runs are
    seeded first, so base budget behavior for them is identical; the
    ws seeds only fill leftover _MAX_CANDIDATES budget. No new
    regexes; bounded; static; never executes.
    """
    seen = set()
    seeds = [(m.start(), m.end(), m.group(0))
             for m in _B64_RUN.finditer(text)]
    for start, end, words in _iter_b64_ws_spans(text):
        seeds.append((start, end,
                      "".join(text[s:e] for s, e in words)))
    # F456: junk-tolerant spans seed the fixpoint too, as their
    # junk-stripped whole-span candidate -- a junk-fragmented (or
    # mixed whitespace+junk fragmented) outer layer over an inner
    # stack composes exactly like the F453 ws-outer case: round 1
    # strips junk via the seed, rounds 2+ strip whitespace from
    # decoded intermediates. Pure-whitespace spans are skipped by
    # _iter_b64_junk_spans (F449 owns them), so no seed duplicates
    # and the _MAX_CANDIDATES budget is unperturbed.
    for start, end, words in _iter_b64_junk_spans(text):
        seeds.append((start, end,
                      "".join(text[s:e] for s, e in words)))
    for start, end, run in seeds[:_MAX_CANDIDATES]:
        prev = run
        for _ in range(_MAX_B64_ROUNDS):
            # F453: whitespace-tolerant round input -- see docstring.
            prev_stripped = "".join(prev.split())
            decoded = _try_b64_decode(prev_stripped)
            if decoded is None:
                # F445: the innermost run of a short-credential stack can
                # be under 24 payload chars -- attempt the
                # credential-shape-gated short decode rather than
                # failing closed on the floor. The gate is identical to
                # the pipeline short path: only a credential-shaped
                # decode becomes a variant. (On a 24+ run this retry is
                # a no-op -- the floor is the only difference.)
                gated = _try_b64_decode(prev_stripped,
                                        min_payload=_B64_SHORT_FLOOR)
                if (gated is None
                        or _match_credential_forms(gated) is None):
                    break
                decoded = gated
            if decoded == prev_stripped:
                break
            variant = text[:start] + decoded + text[end:]
            if variant not in seen:
                seen.add(variant)
                yield variant
            prev = decoded


# F449/F450: manual (regex-free) scanners for the whitespace-tolerant
# base64 and short-hex run shapes. The brief for these closures forbids
# new regexes (ReDoS surface), so both scans are linear manual walks --
# each character is visited O(1) times -- over module-level alphabet
# sets. Ground truth for both is the stdlib decoder (base64.b64decode
# ignores whitespace; bytes.fromhex rejects odd lengths).
_B64_ALPHABET = frozenset(
    "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/-_")
_HEX_ALPHABET = frozenset("0123456789abcdefABCDEF")
# F450: short hex runs are 10-15 byte-pairs (20-30 hex chars). The
# 16-pair _HEX_RUN floor is the FP guard; the credential-shape gate in
# _gated_encoded_variants is the guard made precise (the shortest
# credential rule needs 15 chars, so only 15-pair runs can pass it --
# the floor gates attempts, never blocking decisions, same discipline
# as the F445 short base64 floor).
_HEX_SHORT_MIN_PAIRS = 10
_HEX_SHORT_MAX_PAIRS = 15
# F449: a whitespace-tolerant run is a maximal span of base64-alphabet
# chars and whitespace containing >= 1 whitespace gap and >=
# _B64_SHORT_FLOOR alphabet chars. Maximal-span (not chunk-floor)
# semantics: base64.b64decode ignores whitespace wherever it falls, so
# an 8-char chunk floor would leave a fragmentation residual -- any
# random whitespace insertion has a good chance of leaving a <8-char
# fragment (fuzzer-verified). Non-whitespace interior junk breaks the
# span (fail closed), exactly like the contiguous-run gate; the
# whitespace-stripped candidate still has to pass strict decode
# (exact round-trip) plus the credential-shape gate, so the FP
# discipline is identical to the long path.
# Bound on the scan's total span (DoS guard); the stripped payload is
# additionally capped by _try_b64_decode's _MAX_DECODE_BYTES check.
_B64_WS_MAX_SPAN = 4 * _MAX_DECODE_BYTES
# F451: hex with embedded whitespace. These 6 ASCII whitespace chars
# are EXACTLY what bytes.fromhex ignores -- str.isspace() is broader
# (\x1c-\x1f, \x85, \xa0, \u2003, \u2028, \u2029 are REJECTED by
# fromhex, verified empirically), so treating them as gaps would emit
# variants a real parser rejects (a fidelity false positive). Gaps are
# therefore this set only, not str.isspace().
_HEX_WS = frozenset(" \t\n\r\x0b\x0c")
# F451: alpha floor for a whitespace-tolerant hex span, in hex chars.
# The F450 short-hex floor (20 chars = 10 byte-pairs): single words
# that could be credential-shaped are already owned by the _HEX_RUN
# (32+) / _iter_hex_short_runs (20-30 even) paths, so only >= 2-word
# sub-runs are new; a span under 20 alphabet chars cannot emit under
# the credential-shape gate (decoded text needs >= 15 chars).
_HEX_WS_ALPHA_FLOOR = 20
# Bound on the scan's total span (DoS guard); the stripped payload is
# additionally capped by _try_hex_decode's _MAX_DECODE_BYTES check.
_HEX_WS_MAX_SPAN = 4 * _MAX_DECODE_BYTES
# F456: the exact ASCII whitespace subset of junk gaps. base64.b64decode
# (validate=False) discards whitespace, but on str input it RAISES on
# non-ASCII (verified: ValueError on '\u00e9'/ZWSP), so only ASCII
# whitespace is a junk gap here -- fail closed on the rest. Kept as a
# named set so _iter_b64_junk_spans can tell F449-owned pure-whitespace
# spans apart from spans with real (non-whitespace) junk.
_B64_JUNK_WS = frozenset(" \t\n\r\x0b\x0c")
# F456: the strict stdlib base64 alphabet (no base64url '-'/'_'). The
# junk walker uses this, not _B64_ALPHABET, so that inserted '-'/'_'
# are junk gaps: base64.b64decode(validate=False) discards them
# (verified at every position), while a genuine base64url parser
# treats them as data. Both interpretations are attempted -- the
# contiguous _B64_RUN path (which keeps '-'/'_' as word chars and
# normalizes to '+/') still catches genuine base64url, and the junk
# path catches '-'/'_'-fragmented stdlib base64. A stripped blob only
# becomes a variant on strict decode + the credential-shape gate, so
# the duality cannot false-positive: the wrong interpretation just
# fails the gate.
_B64_STRICT_ALPHABET = frozenset(
    "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/")


def _is_b64_junk_gap(ch):
    """F456: junk-gap predicate for base64 spans.

    True for exactly the characters base64.b64decode(validate=False)
    discards on str input (verified empirically): ASCII whitespace and
    every other ASCII char outside the strict base64 alphabet --
    including '-'/'_' (verified discarded at every position; genuine
    base64url is still owned by the contiguous _B64_RUN path, which
    normalizes them -- see _B64_STRICT_ALPHABET) -- except '='.
    '=' is a span BREAKER, not a gap: a single interior '=' is
    discarded at some positions but TRUNCATES the decode at others
    (verified: '=' at pos%4==3 and '==' mid-string truncate, and
    'SGVsbG8=!V29ybGQ=' decodes to b'Hello' only), so stripping it
    would emit variants a real parser does not produce. Non-ASCII
    chars break too (b64decode(str) raises on non-ASCII -- fail
    closed). No parity sensitivity: junk discard is
    position-independent (verified: '!' inserted at every one of 28
    positions decodes identically), unlike the F451 hex
    byte-boundary parity. Static, never executes.
    """
    return (ch in _B64_JUNK_WS
            or (ord(ch) < 128 and ch not in _B64_STRICT_ALPHABET
                and ch != "="))


def _iter_hex_short_runs(text):
    """Yield (start, end) spans of maximal hex runs with an even length
    in [_HEX_SHORT_MIN_PAIRS*2, _HEX_SHORT_MAX_PAIRS*2].

    Maximal-run semantics give the boundary guards for free: a span
    inside a longer hex run is never yielded (the long path owns 32+
    runs), and odd-length runs yield nothing at all -- bytes.fromhex
    rejects odd lengths too, so ground truth is identical (fail closed).
    Linear, no regexes."""
    n = len(text)
    i = 0
    lo = 2 * _HEX_SHORT_MIN_PAIRS
    hi = 2 * _HEX_SHORT_MAX_PAIRS
    while i < n:
        if text[i] in _HEX_ALPHABET:
            j = i + 1
            while j < n and text[j] in _HEX_ALPHABET:
                j += 1
            length = j - i
            if lo <= length <= hi and length % 2 == 0:
                yield (i, j)
            i = j
        else:
            i += 1


def _iter_b64_ws_spans(text):
    """Yield (start, end, words) for maximal base64 spans with whitespace.

    A span is a maximal run of base64-alphabet words joined by
    whitespace-only gaps, containing at least one gap and at least
    _B64_SHORT_FLOOR alphabet chars. Words are maximal alphabet runs
    of any length >= 1. ``=`` is a span/word SEPARATOR, not a word
    character: in strict base64 ``=`` occurs only as trailing padding,
    so an interior/fused ``=`` (``id=<b64>``, padding mid-string) can
    never be part of a valid decode -- splitting there lets the
    strict decoder plus the exact round-trip check decide, and the
    decoder's ``% 4`` normalization re-adds stripped trailing padding
    itself. Shared walker: _iter_ws_spans (F451 refactor -- compose,
    don't fork). No regexes. Static, never executes."""
    return _iter_ws_spans(text, _B64_ALPHABET, str.isspace,
                           _B64_WS_MAX_SPAN, _B64_SHORT_FLOOR, False)


def _iter_b64_junk_spans(text):
    """Yield (start, end, words) for maximal base64 spans with junk gaps.

    A span is a maximal run of strict-alphabet base64 words
    (_B64_STRICT_ALPHABET -- '-'/'_' are gaps here, not word chars;
    genuine base64url stays owned by the contiguous _B64_RUN path)
    joined by junk gaps (chars base64.b64decode(validate=False)
    discards on str input -- see _is_b64_junk_gap), containing at
    least one NON-whitespace junk gap and at least _B64_SHORT_FLOOR
    alphabet chars. Words are maximal alphabet runs of any length >=
    1. Pure-whitespace spans are SKIPPED: they are owned by the F449
    path (_iter_b64_ws_spans / _b64_ws_variants), so yielding them
    here would duplicate fixpoint seeds and perturb the shared
    _MAX_CANDIDATES budget. '=' breaks spans (verified truncation
    semantics -- stripping it would emit variants a real parser does
    not produce); non-ASCII breaks spans (fail closed). Shared
    walker: _iter_ws_spans (compose, don't fork). No regexes.
    Static, never executes."""
    for start, end, words in _iter_ws_spans(
            text, _B64_STRICT_ALPHABET, _is_b64_junk_gap,
            _B64_WS_MAX_SPAN, _B64_SHORT_FLOOR, False):
        if any(ch not in _B64_JUNK_WS
               for (_, we), (ns, _) in zip(words, words[1:])
               for ch in text[we:ns]):
            yield (start, end, words)


def _iter_hex_ws_spans(text):
    """Yield (start, end, words) for maximal hex spans with whitespace.

    A span is a maximal run of hex-alphabet words joined by gaps of
    _HEX_WS chars only (exactly the chars bytes.fromhex ignores --
    fidelity probe: str.isspace() is broader and would false-positive),
    containing at least one gap and at least _HEX_WS_ALPHA_FLOOR
    alphabet chars. Words are maximal hex-alphabet runs of any length
    >= 1. Non-gap, non-hex interior chars break the span (fail closed),
    exactly like the contiguous-run gate.

    Byte-boundary parity (fidelity-critical): bytes.fromhex skips
    whitespace only at byte boundaries -- whitespace between the two
    nibbles of a byte is REJECTED (verified: fromhex('a bcd') raises
    at the gap, fromhex('ab cd') decodes). A gap therefore continues
    the span only when the hex-digit count so far is even; an
    odd-parity gap breaks the span (fail closed), exactly like ground
    truth. Consequence: every interior word of a yielded span has
    even length, so every sub-word-run's internal gaps are also at
    even sub-run-relative parity -- stripping any enumerated sub-run
    is ground-truth identical to fromhex on the spaced sub-run; odd
    stripped lengths raise ValueError in ground truth and decode to
    None in the scanner (fail closed).

    Shared walker: _iter_ws_spans (F451 refactor -- compose, don't
    fork). No regexes. Static, never executes."""
    return _iter_ws_spans(text, _HEX_ALPHABET, _HEX_WS.__contains__,
                           _HEX_WS_MAX_SPAN, _HEX_WS_ALPHA_FLOOR, True)


def _iter_ws_spans(text, alphabet, is_gap, max_span, alpha_floor,
                   parity_sensitive):
    """Shared whitespace-tolerant span walker (F449 base64, F451 hex) --
    compose, don't fork.

    Yields (start, end, words) for maximal spans of *alphabet* words
    joined by gaps (chars where ``is_gap`` holds), containing at least
    one gap and at least *alpha_floor* alphabet chars, with total span
    at most *max_span*. Words are maximal alphabet runs of any length
    >= 1. Non-gap, non-alphabet interior chars break the span (fail
    closed). When *parity_sensitive*, a gap continues the span only at
    even digit parity (bytes.fromhex skips whitespace only at byte
    boundaries); otherwise any gap continues it. Maximal spans keep
    the walk linear (the scan jumps past each span). No regexes.
    Static, never executes."""
    n = len(text)
    i = 0
    while i < n:
        if text[i] in alphabet:
            words = []
            pos = i
            digits = 0  # alphabet chars in the span so far
            while True:
                j = pos
                while j < n and text[j] in alphabet:
                    j += 1
                words.append((pos, j))
                digits += j - pos
                g = j
                while g < n and is_gap(text[g]):
                    g += 1
                if (g == j or g >= n or text[g] not in alphabet
                        or (parity_sensitive and digits % 2)):
                    end = j
                    break
                pos = g
            if len(words) > 1 and end - i <= max_span:
                alpha = sum(e - s for s, e in words)
                if alpha >= alpha_floor:
                    yield (i, end, words)
            i = end
        else:
            i += 1


def _gated_encoded_variants(text, matches, decode):
    """Shared strict-decode + credential-shape gate (F445 base64/base32
    short runs, F449 whitespace-tolerant base64 sub-runs, F450 short
    hex) -- compose, don't fork.

    *matches* is an iterable of (start, end, blob) with blob the exact
    string the strict decoder sees; *decode* is the strict decoder
    (_try_b64_decode / _try_b32_decode / _try_hex_decode). The
    per-shape floors live in the match producers (min_payload for
    base64/base32, the pair band in _iter_hex_short_runs, the alpha
    floor in _iter_b64_ws_spans). A blob that fails strict decode is
    ignored; a blob that DOES decode becomes a variant ONLY when the
    decoded text is credential-shaped (_match_credential_forms) -- the
    old length floors' FP guard made precise. Innocent decodes never
    enter the variant pipeline, so they cannot block and cannot starve
    the _MAX_CANDIDATES budget. At most _MAX_CANDIDATES matches are
    attempted per call (decode attempts, not just emissions, count --
    same as the F445 originals). Bounded, static, never executes.
    """
    attempted = 0
    for start, end, blob in matches:
        if attempted >= _MAX_CANDIDATES:
            break
        attempted += 1
        decoded = decode(blob)
        if decoded is None:
            continue
        if _match_credential_forms(decoded) is None:
            continue
        variant = text[:start] + decoded + text[end:]
        if variant != text:
            yield variant


def _b64_short_variants(text):
    """F445: short base64/base64url runs (8-23 payload chars) attempted
    only when the decoded candidate is credential-shaped.

    For each short run: strict-decode with the lowered floor
    (_B64_SHORT_FLOOR); a run that fails strict decode, round-trip, or
    the printable-ASCII gate is ignored. A run that DOES decode is a
    genuine encoding -- but it becomes a variant ONLY when the decoded
    text matches a credential rule. That credential-shape gate is the
    24-char floor's FP guard made precise: innocent short decodes
    (junk text, word fragments, UUIDs) never enter the variant
    pipeline, so they cannot block and cannot starve the
    _MAX_CANDIDATES budget either. One decode layer only -- stacked
    short runs compose via the 2-layer pipeline (an outer long run
    decodes in layer 1, the inner short run in layer 2). Bounded,
    static, never executes.
    """
    matches = ((m.start(), m.end(), m.group(0))
               for m in _B64_SHORT_RUN.finditer(text))
    yield from _gated_encoded_variants(
        text, matches,
        lambda blob: _try_b64_decode(blob, min_payload=_B64_SHORT_FLOOR))


def _b32_short_variants(text):
    """F445: short base32 runs (8-23 payload chars), same contract as
    _b64_short_variants -- strict decode plus the credential-shape gate
    before a variant is emitted. Same mechanism class as the base64
    gate, closed together."""
    matches = ((m.start(), m.end(), m.group(0))
               for m in _B32_SHORT_RUN.finditer(text))
    yield from _gated_encoded_variants(
        text, matches,
        lambda blob: _try_b32_decode(blob, min_payload=_B64_SHORT_FLOOR))


def _hex_short_variants(text):
    """F450: short hex runs (10-15 byte-pairs) attempted only when the
    decoded candidate is credential-shaped.

    Same mechanism class as F445 (a minimum-length FP guard on an
    encoded run) but the hex code path (_HEX_RUN + _try_hex_decode),
    which a base64-only fix would not cover -- hence filed distinctly.
    Strict decode is bytes.fromhex with the printable-ASCII gate; the
    credential-shape gate is the 16-pair floor's FP guard made precise
    (composed via _gated_encoded_variants, not forked). One decode
    layer; stacks compose via the 2-layer pipeline. Bounded, static,
    never executes.

    Honest residual (documented, not a finding): hex of sub-15-char
    text cannot be blocked -- the shortest credential rule needs 15
    chars (xox[baprs]- + 10), so such text is not credential-shaped
    under any deterministic form. That is the F2 unknown-format
    residual class, not a bypass of a known format.
    """
    matches = ((s, e, text[s:e]) for s, e in _iter_hex_short_runs(text))
    yield from _gated_encoded_variants(text, matches, _try_hex_decode)


def _b64_ws_variants(text):
    """F449: base64/base64url runs with embedded whitespace, attempted
    only when the whitespace-stripped candidate strict-decodes to
    credential-shaped text.

    base64.b64decode ignores whitespace, so a credential's base64 with
    spaces/newlines inserted decodes exactly in a real parser while
    _B64_RUN (24 contiguous alphabet chars) matches nothing -- a
    contiguity miss, distinct from F445's length gate (this payload is
    over the length gate and still misses). Each maximal
    whitespace-tolerant span (_iter_b64_ws_spans) is searched over its
    sub-word-runs, longest first: the whitespace-stripped sub-run goes
    through the existing strict-decode path (_try_b64_decode: strict
    alphabet, exact round-trip, printable-ASCII); only a
    credential-shaped decode becomes a variant, so the FP discipline
    is identical to the long path (only credential-shaped decodes
    enter the pipeline). Single-word sub-runs are skipped (the
    contiguous _B64_RUN/_B64_SHORT_RUN paths own them). Padding
    mid-string fails strict decode (fail closed); non-whitespace
    interior junk breaks the span (fail closed). The sub-run
    enumeration is routed through _gated_encoded_variants, so decode
    attempts obey the same per-call _MAX_CANDIDATES budget as every
    other encoded-run path. Linear scan, no new regexes; bounded
    span; static, never executes.

    Honest residual (documented, not a finding): at most
    _MAX_CANDIDATES sub-runs are attempted per layer, longest first,
    so a small fragmented payload buried in a very word-rich span can
    miss -- the same bounded-budget class as F444's documented
    ~130-junk residual.
    """
    def _matches():
        for start, end, words in _iter_b64_ws_spans(text):
            n_words = len(words)
            for length in range(n_words, 1, -1):  # longest first
                for i in range(n_words - length + 1):
                    blob = "".join(text[s:e]
                                   for s, e in words[i:i + length])
                    yield (start, end, blob)
    yield from _gated_encoded_variants(
        text, _matches(),
        lambda blob: _try_b64_decode(blob, min_payload=_B64_SHORT_FLOOR))


def _b64_junk_variants(text):
    """F456: base64/base64url runs with embedded non-whitespace junk,
    attempted only when the junk-stripped candidate strict-decodes to
    credential-shaped text.

    base64.b64decode(validate=False) discards EVERY ASCII character
    outside the base64 alphabet before decoding -- not just whitespace
    -- so the base64 of a credential with '\\x00' / '!' / '.'
    inserted decodes exactly in a real parser (verified:
    b64decode(junked)==credential at every insertion position) while
    _B64_RUN (24 contiguous alphabet chars) matches nothing and the
    F449 _iter_b64_ws_spans requires whitespace-only gaps, so any
    non-whitespace junk breaks the span. Distinct from F449
    (whitespace-only spans): different character class, different gap
    predicate -- same filing precedent as F451-vs-F449. Each maximal
    junk-tolerant span (_iter_b64_junk_spans) is searched over its
    sub-word-runs, longest first: the junk-stripped sub-run goes
    through the existing strict-decode path (_try_b64_decode: strict
    alphabet, exact round-trip, printable-ASCII); only a
    credential-shaped decode becomes a variant, so the FP discipline
    is identical to the long path (only credential-shaped decodes
    enter the pipeline). Single-word sub-runs are skipped (the
    contiguous _B64_RUN/_B64_SHORT_RUN paths own them). '=' breaks
    spans (verified: stripping it would emit variants a real parser
    does not produce -- interior '=' truncates at pos%4==3 and '=='
    mid-string truncates); non-ASCII breaks spans (fail closed --
    b64decode(str) raises). '-'/'_' are junk gaps here (stdlib
    discards them; verified): a genuine base64url payload fragmented
    at '-'/'_' strips to a wrong decode that fails the
    credential-shape gate, while the contiguous _B64_RUN path
    (unchanged) still catches the true base64url decode -- both
    interpretations are attempted, neither false-positives. The
    sub-run enumeration is routed through
    _gated_encoded_variants, so decode attempts obey the same
    per-call _MAX_CANDIDATES budget as every other encoded-run path.
    Linear scan, no new regexes; bounded span; static, never executes.

    Honest residual (documented, not a finding): junk spans whose
    gaps include '=' (padding mid-string) do not assemble -- the '='
    breaker is ground-truth exact for 'SGVsbG8=!V29ybGQ=' (real
    parser truncates to the first chunk), but a single interior '='
    at a quantum boundary IS discarded by the real parser while the
    scanner breaks there (fail closed). Same bounded-budget class as
    F449's documented residual.
    """
    def _matches():
        for start, end, words in _iter_b64_junk_spans(text):
            n_words = len(words)
            for length in range(n_words, 1, -1):  # longest first
                for i in range(n_words - length + 1):
                    blob = "".join(text[s:e]
                                   for s, e in words[i:i + length])
                    yield (start, end, blob)
    yield from _gated_encoded_variants(
        text, _matches(),
        lambda blob: _try_b64_decode(blob, min_payload=_B64_SHORT_FLOOR))


def _hex_ws_variants(text):
    """F451: hex runs with embedded whitespace, attempted only when
    the whitespace-stripped candidate strict-decodes to
    credential-shaped text.

    bytes.fromhex ignores whitespace, so the hex of a (long,
    over-the-F450-length-gate) credential with spaces/newlines
    inserted decodes exactly in a real parser while _HEX_RUN (16
    contiguous byte-pairs) matches nothing -- a contiguity miss,
    distinct from F450's length gate and F449's base64 code path
    (_HEX_RUN + _try_hex_decode vs _B64_RUN + _try_b64_decode; a
    base64-only F449 fix would not cover it). Gaps continue a span
    only at byte boundaries (even hex-digit parity); a gap at odd
    parity breaks the span, exactly like bytes.fromhex (verified:
    fromhex('a bcd') raises, fromhex('ab cd') decodes) -- the scanner
    never emits a variant a real parser would reject. Each maximal
    whitespace-tolerant span (_iter_hex_ws_spans) is searched over
    its sub-word-runs, longest first: the whitespace-stripped sub-run
    goes through the existing strict-decode path (_try_hex_decode:
    bytes.fromhex + printable-ASCII); only a credential-shaped decode
    becomes a variant, so the FP discipline is identical to the long
    path (only credential-shaped decodes enter the pipeline).
    Single-word sub-runs are skipped (the contiguous _HEX_RUN /
    _iter_hex_short_runs paths own every single word that could pass
    the shape gate). Odd stripped lengths fail closed (ValueError in
    both ground truth and the scanner). Non-_HEX_WS interior junk
    breaks the span (fail closed). The sub-run enumeration is routed
    through _gated_encoded_variants, so decode attempts obey the same
    per-call _MAX_CANDIDATES budget as every other encoded-run path.
    Linear scan, no new regexes; bounded span; static, never executes.

    Honest residual (documented, not a finding): at most
    _MAX_CANDIDATES sub-runs are attempted per layer, longest first,
    so a small fragmented payload buried in a very word-rich span can
    miss -- the same bounded-budget class as F444's documented
    ~130-junk residual and F449's ws-b64 residual.
    """
    def _matches():
        for start, end, words in _iter_hex_ws_spans(text):
            n_words = len(words)
            for length in range(n_words, 1, -1):  # longest first
                for i in range(n_words - length + 1):
                    blob = "".join(text[s:e]
                                   for s, e in words[i:i + length])
                    yield (start, end, blob)
    yield from _gated_encoded_variants(text, _matches(), _try_hex_decode)


def _try_hex_decode(run):
    """Hex decode guarded by the printable-ASCII check."""
    if len(run) > 2 * _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        return None
    try:
        raw = bytes.fromhex(run)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if not _printable_ascii(raw):
        return None
    try:
        return raw.decode("ascii")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        return None


def _try_b32_decode(run, min_payload=24):
    """Strict base32 decode with round-trip validation (F8).

    Same contract as the base64 path: the run must be a genuine base32
    encoding (strict alphabet, valid padding, exact round-trip) that
    decodes to mostly-printable ASCII. Without the round-trip check,
    arbitrary uppercase/digit runs would "decode" to garbage bytes.

    min_payload is the floor on payload chars (default 24); the F445
    short path passes 8, with the credential-shape gate in
    _b32_short_variants deciding which short decodes become variants.
    """
    norm = run.rstrip("=")
    if len(norm) < min_payload:
        return None
    try:
        raw = base64.b32decode(norm + "=" * (-len(norm) % 8),
                               casefold=False)
    except Exception:
        return None
    if len(raw) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        return None
    if base64.b32encode(raw).decode("ascii").rstrip("=") != norm:
        return None
    if not _printable_ascii(raw):
        return None
    try:
        return raw.decode("ascii")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        return None


# --------------------------------------------------- F428: JSFuck decoding
# JSFuck is a JS-execution encoding: arbitrary JS written with only the
# characters []()!+- (unary minus included). Salt Labs vs Manus (2026-10-03)
# showed it used for prompt-injection in the wild; the wave-341 attack lane
# showed a JSFuck-encoded slack token missing every DLP seam while the
# plain/base64/hex/base32 forms all block.
#
# A static decoder is safe here because it never executes: it is a strict,
# bounded recursive-descent evaluator for the JSFuck construction set that
# real encoders emit (literals ![]/!![]/+/-, string coercion, indexing,
# the "constructor"/"toString" member ladder, and calls of the safe
# primitive constructors String/Number/Boolean). Anything outside that set
# — notably Function() (arbitrary code) — fails closed: the run is not a
# decodable payload and is ignored, exactly like a base64 run that fails
# round-trip validation. The decoded text must additionally be
# mostly-printable ASCII and differ from the input before it becomes a
# variant, so the false-positive surface stays where it already was: the
# credential regexes themselves. A run that merely LOOKS like JSFuck but
# decodes to innocent text (or fails to decode) never blocks.


class _JSFuckFail(Exception):
    """Strict-decode failure: the run is not decodable JSFuck."""


_JSFuck_UNDEF = ("undef", None)

# V8 Function.prototype.toString forms for the callable surface the
# decoder supports. The artifact was node-verified (V8); a non-V8 form
# simply fails closed (no variant), it never mis-decodes.
_JSFUCK_FUNC_SOURCE = {
    "filter": "function filter() { [native code] }",
    "flat": "function flat() { [native code] }",
    "map": "function map() { [native code] }",
    "fill": "function fill() { [native code] }",
    "find": "function find() { [native code] }",
    "includes": "function includes() { [native code] }",
    "indexOf": "function indexOf() { [native code] }",
    "join": "function join() { [native code] }",
    "sort": "function sort() { [native code] }",
    "reverse": "function reverse() { [native code] }",
    "slice": "function slice() { [native code] }",
    "entries": "function entries() { [native code] }",
    "keys": "function keys() { [native code] }",
    "values": "function values() { [native code] }",
    "String": "function String() { [native code] }",
    "Number": "function Number() { [native code] }",
    "Boolean": "function Boolean() { [native code] }",
    "Array": "function Array() { [native code] }",
    "Function": "function Function() { [native code] }",
}

_JSFUCK_ARR_MEMBERS = set(_JSFUCK_FUNC_SOURCE) | {"constructor", "length"}


def _jsfuck_truthy(v):
    k = v[0]
    if k == "undef":
        return False
    if k == "bool":
        return v[1]
    if k == "num":
        return v[1] != 0 and v[1] == v[1]  # nonzero and not NaN
    if k == "str":
        return len(v[1]) > 0
    return True  # arrays and functions are truthy


def _jsfuck_primitive(v):
    k = v[0]
    if k in ("undef", "bool", "num", "str"):
        return v
    if k == "arr":
        # Array.prototype.toString: join(",").
        return ("str", ",".join(_jsfuck_to_string(e) for e in v[1]))
    if k == "func":
        try:
            return ("str", _JSFUCK_FUNC_SOURCE[v[1]])
        except KeyError:
            raise _JSFuckFail("unknown function %r" % (v[1],))
    raise _JSFuckFail("uncoercible %r" % (k,))


def _jsfuck_to_number(v):
    v = _jsfuck_primitive(v)
    k = v[0]
    if k == "undef":
        return float("nan")
    if k == "bool":
        return 1.0 if v[1] else 0.0
    if k == "num":
        return v[1]
    s = v[1].strip()
    if s == "":
        return 0.0
    if s in ("Infinity", "+Infinity"):
        return float("inf")
    if s == "-Infinity":
        return float("-inf")
    try:
        if (s[:2].lower() == "0x" and len(s) > 2
                and all(c in "0123456789abcdefABCDEF" for c in s[2:])):
            return float(int(s, 16))
        return float(s)
    except ValueError:
        return float("nan")


def _jsfuck_to_string(v):
    v = _jsfuck_primitive(v)
    k = v[0]
    if k == "undef":
        return "undefined"
    if k == "bool":
        return "true" if v[1] else "false"
    if k == "num":
        n = v[1]
        if n != n:
            return "NaN"
        if n == float("inf"):
            return "Infinity"
        if n == float("-inf"):
            return "-Infinity"
        if n == int(n) and abs(n) < 1e21:
            return str(int(n))
        return repr(n)
    return v[1]


def _jsfuck_num_to_string_radix(n, radix):
    """Number.prototype.toString(radix), integer n, radix 2..36."""
    if n != n:
        return "NaN"
    if n == float("inf"):
        return "Infinity"
    if n == float("-inf"):
        return "-Infinity"
    digits = "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz"
    neg = n < 0
    n = int(abs(n))
    if n == 0:
        return "0"
    out = ""
    while n:
        out = digits[n % radix] + out
        n //= radix
    return ("-" if neg else "") + out


class _JSFuckParser:
    """Strict recursive-descent evaluator for the JSFuck subset.

    Grammar (fully parenthesized, encoder-emitted):
      expr    := unary (("+" | "-") unary)*
      unary   := ("!" | "+" | "-")* postfix
      postfix := primary (("[" expr "]") | call)*
      primary := "[]" | "(" expr ")"
      call    := "(" (expr ("," expr)*)? ")"
    """

    def __init__(self, text, max_steps=2000000, max_depth=600):
        self.t = text
        self.n = len(text)
        self.i = 0
        self.steps = 0
        self.max_steps = max_steps
        self.max_depth = max_depth

    def _step(self):
        self.steps += 1
        if self.steps > self.max_steps:
            raise _JSFuckFail("step budget")

    def peek(self):
        return self.t[self.i] if self.i < self.n else ""

    def parse(self, depth=0):
        if depth > self.max_depth:
            raise _JSFuckFail("depth")
        v = self._parse_add(depth + 1)
        if self.i != self.n:
            raise _JSFuckFail("trailing input")
        return v

    def _parse_add(self, depth):
        self._step()
        v = self._parse_unary(depth)
        while self.peek() in ("+", "-"):
            op = self.t[self.i]
            self.i += 1
            rhs = self._parse_unary(depth)
            v = self._apply_add(v, rhs, op)
        return v

    def _parse_unary(self, depth):
        self._step()
        ch = self.peek()
        if ch == "!":
            self.i += 1
            return ("bool", not _jsfuck_truthy(self._parse_unary(depth)))
        if ch == "+":
            self.i += 1
            return ("num", _jsfuck_to_number(self._parse_unary(depth)))
        if ch == "-":
            self.i += 1
            return ("num", -_jsfuck_to_number(self._parse_unary(depth)))
        return self._parse_postfix(depth)

    def _parse_postfix(self, depth):
        self._step()
        v = self._parse_primary(depth)
        while True:
            if self.peek() == "[":
                self.i += 1
                idx = self._parse_add(depth + 1)
                if self.peek() != "]":
                    raise _JSFuckFail("unclosed [")
                self.i += 1
                v = self._index(v, idx)
            elif self.peek() == "(" and v[0] in ("func", "method"):
                v = self._call(v, depth + 1)
            else:
                return v

    def _parse_primary(self, depth):
        self._step()
        ch = self.peek()
        if ch == "[":
            # Array literal: [] or [expr (, expr)*]. Elements are needed
            # for correct ToPrimitive (join) and indexed access.
            self.i += 1
            elems = []
            if self.peek() != "]":
                elems.append(self._parse_add(depth + 1))
                while self.peek() == ",":
                    self.i += 1
                    elems.append(self._parse_add(depth + 1))
            if self.peek() != "]":
                raise _JSFuckFail("unclosed array")
            self.i += 1
            return ("arr", elems)
        if ch == "(":
            self.i += 1
            if self.peek() == ")":
                raise _JSFuckFail("empty parens")
            v = self._parse_add(depth + 1)
            if self.peek() != ")":
                raise _JSFuckFail("unclosed (")
            self.i += 1
            return v
        raise _JSFuckFail("unexpected char")

    def _apply_add(self, a, b, op):
        pa, pb = _jsfuck_primitive(a), _jsfuck_primitive(b)
        if pa[0] == "str" or pb[0] == "str":
            if op == "-":
                return ("num", float("nan"))
            return ("str", _jsfuck_to_string(pa) + _jsfuck_to_string(pb))
        na, nb = _jsfuck_to_number(pa), _jsfuck_to_number(pb)
        return ("num", na + nb if op == "+" else na - nb)

    def _index(self, v, idx):
        k = v[0]
        key = _jsfuck_to_string(_jsfuck_primitive(idx))
        if k == "str":
            try:
                n = int(key)
            except ValueError:
                n = None
            if n is not None and 0 <= n < len(v[1]):
                return ("str", v[1][n])
            if key == "length":
                return ("num", float(len(v[1])))
            if key == "constructor":
                return ("func", "String")
            return _JSFuck_UNDEF
        if k == "arr":
            if key in _JSFUCK_ARR_MEMBERS:
                if key == "length":
                    return ("num", float(len(v[1])))
                if key == "constructor":
                    return ("func", "Array")
                return ("func", key)
            try:
                n = int(key)
            except ValueError:
                return _JSFuck_UNDEF
            if 0 <= n < len(v[1]):
                return v[1][n]
            return _JSFuck_UNDEF
        if k == "num":
            if key == "constructor":
                return ("func", "Number")
            if key == "toString":
                return ("method", (v, "num_toString"))
            return _JSFuck_UNDEF
        if k == "bool":
            if key == "constructor":
                return ("func", "Boolean")
            return _JSFuck_UNDEF
        if k == "func":
            if key == "length":
                return ("num", 1.0)
            if key == "name":
                return ("str", v[1])
            if key == "constructor":
                return ("func", "Function")
            return _JSFuck_UNDEF
        return _JSFuck_UNDEF

    def _call(self, v, depth):
        self.i += 1  # consume '('
        args = []
        if self.peek() != ")":
            args.append(self._parse_add(depth + 1))
            while self.peek() == ",":
                self.i += 1
                args.append(self._parse_add(depth + 1))
        if self.peek() != ")":
            raise _JSFuckFail("unclosed call")
        self.i += 1
        if v[0] == "method":
            obj, mname = v[1]
            if mname == "num_toString":
                n = _jsfuck_to_number(obj)
                radix = 10
                if args:
                    r = _jsfuck_to_number(args[0])
                    if r != int(r) or not 2 <= r <= 36:
                        raise _JSFuckFail("bad radix")
                    radix = int(r)
                return ("str", _jsfuck_num_to_string_radix(n, radix))
            raise _JSFuckFail("unsafe method")
        name = v[1]
        if name == "String":
            a = args[0] if args else _JSFuck_UNDEF
            return ("str", _jsfuck_to_string(_jsfuck_primitive(a)))
        if name == "Number":
            a = args[0] if args else _JSFuck_UNDEF
            return ("num", _jsfuck_to_number(a))
        if name == "Boolean":
            a = args[0] if args else _JSFuck_UNDEF
            return ("bool", _jsfuck_truthy(a))
        if name == "Array":
            return ("arr", list(args))
        # Function() (arbitrary code) and anything else: refuse.
        raise _JSFuckFail("unsafe call")


def _try_jsfuck_decode(run):
    """Strict static JSFuck decode of one alphabet run.

    Returns the decoded ASCII string, or None when the run is not
    decodable JSFuck (strict-eval failure), decodes to non-text, or is
    unchanged. Never executes; bounded by run length, step budget, and
    recursion depth.
    """
    if len(run) > _MAX_JSFUCK_BYTES:
        return None
    try:
        value = _JSFuckParser(run).parse()
        decoded = _jsfuck_to_string(_jsfuck_primitive(value))
    except _JSFuckFail:
        return None
    except RecursionError:
        return None
    if not decoded or decoded == run:
        return None
    if len(decoded) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        return None
    try:
        raw = decoded.encode("ascii")
    except UnicodeEncodeError:
        return None
    if not _printable_ascii(raw):
        return None
    return decoded


_HEX_DIGITS = frozenset("0123456789abcdefABCDEF")
_OCT_DIGITS = frozenset("01234567")
_BIN_DIGITS = frozenset("01")  # F472: ToNumber 0b radix
_DEC_DIGITS = frozenset("0123456789")  # F472: strict ToNumber decimal scan
# F436: the semicolon is optional (legacy lenient form, mirroring
# html.unescape); the strict form is matched first by alternation
# order in _ENTITY_RUN, so &#65; still decodes identically.
_ENTITY_ONE = re.compile(r"&#([xX][0-9a-fA-F]{1,6}|[0-9]{1,7});?")

# F440: bounded named-entity table for the double-decode layer. Only the
# five named entities that can compose into entity runs (amp/lt/gt/quot/
# apos) are decoded -- the full HTML named-entity table is deliberately
# NOT swallowed (e.g. ``&Lt;`` -> U+226A stays out). Case variants mirror
# html.unescape exactly: amp/AMP, lt/LT, gt/GT, quot/QUOT decode (both
# semicolon and legacy no-semicolon forms); apos is lowercase-only and
# semicolon-only (it is not an HTML5 legacy no-semicolon name).
_NAMED_ENTITIES = (
    ("quot", '"'),
    ("QUOT", '"'),
    ("apos", "'"),
    ("amp", "&"),
    ("AMP", "&"),
    ("lt", "<"),
    ("LT", "<"),
    ("gt", ">"),
    ("GT", ">"),
)
_NAMED_ENTITY_MAP = {name: ch for name, ch in _NAMED_ENTITIES}

# html.unescape legacy no-semicolon forms exist only for these 8 names;
# apos is not a legacy name ("&apos" without ';' stays literal).
_LEGACY_NOSEMI = frozenset(("amp", "AMP", "lt", "LT",
                            "gt", "GT", "quot", "QUOT"))


def _decode_entity_refs_once(text):
    """One left-to-right entity-decode pass (F440 helper).

    Decodes numeric entities per _ENTITY_ONE and the bounded named
    table (exact html.unescape case variants, longest-prefix legacy
    rule: ``&amp;``/``&amp`` -> ``&``, ``&AMP;`` -> ``&``,
    ``&amplify`` -> ``&lify``; ``&Lt;`` is NOT in the table and stays
    literal; ``&apos`` without ``;`` stays literal since apos is not an
    HTML5 legacy no-semicolon name). Returns (decoded_text, match_count),
    or None when a matched entity is undecodable (out-of-range /
    surrogate code point) or the match budget is exceeded -- fail closed,
    no variant. Unmatched text (including stray '&') passes through
    literally. Static; never executes.
    """
    out = []
    i = 0
    n = len(text)
    count = 0
    while i < n:
        c = text[i]
        if c != "&":
            out.append(c)
            i += 1
            continue
        # Numeric first, mirroring the real parser's alternation order.
        m = _ENTITY_ONE.match(text, i)
        if m is not None:
            body = m.group(1)
            cp = int(body[1:], 16) if body[0] in "xX" else int(body, 10)
            ch = _safe_chr(cp)
            if ch is None:
                return None
            out.append(ch)
            i = m.end()
            count += 1
        else:
            # Bounded named entity: scan the letter run (names are at
            # most 4 letters), longest table-name prefix wins; a
            # trailing ';' is consumed when present.
            j = i + 1
            while j < n and j - i - 1 < 4 and text[j].isalpha() and text[j].isascii():
                j += 1
            word = text[i + 1:j]
            name = None
            for cand_len in range(min(4, len(word)), 1, -1):
                if word[:cand_len] in _NAMED_ENTITY_MAP:
                    name = word[:cand_len]
                    break
            if name is None:
                out.append(c)
                i += 1
                continue
            # html.unescape legacy no-semicolon forms exist only for the
            # 8 legacy names (amp/AMP/lt/LT/gt/GT/quot/QUOT); apos is NOT
            # a legacy name, so "&apos" (no ';') stays literal.
            has_semi = i + 1 + len(name) < n and text[i + 1 + len(name)] == ";"
            if not has_semi and name not in _LEGACY_NOSEMI:
                out.append(c)
                i += 1
                continue
            out.append(_NAMED_ENTITY_MAP[name])
            i = i + 1 + len(name)
            if has_semi:
                i += 1
            count += 1
        if count > _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS:
            return None
    return "".join(out), count


def _try_double_entity_decode(text):
    """Whole-text bounded named+numeric entity double-decode (F440).

    Closes double-HTML-entity encoding: wrapping every credential char's
    numeric entity in a SECOND entity -- ``&amp;#xHH;`` (named),
    ``&amp#xHH;`` (no-semicolon named) -- evades the entity sub-layer:
    _ENTITY_RUN never matches named entities and the inner ``&#xHH;``
    never materializes within the 2-layer decode budget, while
    html.unescape applied twice (a real parser stack) recovers the
    credential.

    Mirrors html.unescape applied twice, restricted to the bounded
    named table (amp/AMP, lt/LT, gt/GT, quot/QUOT, apos -- exact
    html.unescape case variants, longest-prefix legacy rule) plus
    numeric entities per _ENTITY_ONE. One variant, so
    entity+base64 stacks (``b64-stacked``) also close inside the 2-layer
    budget. Static; never executes; bounded by _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS
    matches per pass; undecodable code points fail closed (no variant).
    Returns the decoded text, or None.
    """
    if "&" not in text:
        return None
    first = _decode_entity_refs_once(text)
    if first is None:
        return None
    decoded1, _ = first
    if decoded1 == text:
        return None
    second = _decode_entity_refs_once(decoded1)
    if second is None:
        return None
    decoded2, _ = second
    return _ascii_printable_or_none(decoded2)


def _safe_chr(cp):
    """chr(cp), or None for out-of-range / surrogate code points.

    Surrogates are rejected rather than pair-combined: credential rules
    are ASCII-only, so a non-BMP decode can never match -- rejecting is
    the strict, safe choice.
    """
    if 0 <= cp <= 0x10FFFF and not 0xD800 <= cp <= 0xDFFF:
        return chr(cp)
    return None


def _decode_one_escape(run, i):
    """Decode one backslash escape at run[i] == '\\\\'.

    Priority is deterministic: \\uXXXX / \\UXXXXXXXX, \\xXX, then CSS
    (1-6 hex + mandatory single space, so ``\\78 `` decodes as 0x78
    rather than octal ``\\7`` + ``8 ``), then octal (1-3 digits, Python
    semantics). Returns (char, next_index) or None when the escape is
    malformed -- the whole run is then ignored.
    """
    n = len(run)
    c = run[i + 1]
    if c == "u":
        h = run[i + 2:i + 6]
        if len(h) != 4 or any(ch not in _HEX_DIGITS for ch in h):
            return None
        ch = _safe_chr(int(h, 16))
        return (ch, i + 6) if ch is not None else None
    if c == "U":
        h = run[i + 2:i + 10]
        if len(h) != 8 or any(ch not in _HEX_DIGITS for ch in h):
            return None
        ch = _safe_chr(int(h, 16))
        return (ch, i + 10) if ch is not None else None
    if c == "x":
        h = run[i + 2:i + 4]
        if len(h) != 2 or any(ch not in _HEX_DIGITS for ch in h):
            return None
        ch = _safe_chr(int(h, 16))
        return (ch, i + 4) if ch is not None else None
    j = i + 1
    while j < n and j < i + 7 and run[j] in _HEX_DIGITS:
        j += 1
    if j > i + 1 and j < n and run[j] == " ":
        ch = _safe_chr(int(run[i + 1:j], 16))
        return (ch, j + 1) if ch is not None else None
    j = i + 1
    while j < n and j < i + 4 and run[j] in _OCT_DIGITS:
        j += 1
    if j == i + 1:
        return None
    ch = _safe_chr(int(run[i + 1:j], 8))
    return (ch, j) if ch is not None else None


def _ascii_printable_or_none(decoded):
    """Gate a decoded variant: ASCII, printable, length-bounded.

    F490: refuses non-str values (a _JSMethodRef that escapes
    unconsumed is not a decodable variant -- fail closed, never crash
    the scanner).
    """
    if not isinstance(decoded, str):
        return None
    if not decoded or len(decoded) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        return None
    try:
        raw = decoded.encode("ascii")
    except UnicodeEncodeError:
        return None
    if not _printable_ascii(raw):
        return None
    return decoded


def _try_escape_decode(run):
    """Strict static decode of one backslash-escape run (F429).

    Returns the decoded string, or None when the run is not fully
    decodable, decodes to non-text, or is unchanged. Never evals.
    """
    if len(run) > 6 * _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        return None
    out = []
    i = 0
    n = len(run)
    while i < n:
        if run[i] != "\\" or i + 1 >= n:
            return None
        r = _decode_one_escape(run, i)
        if r is None:
            return None
        ch, i = r
        out.append(ch)
    return _ascii_printable_or_none("".join(out))


# F433: CSS whitespace that may terminate a space-free escape (CSS
# Syntax L3 4.3.7 -- one whitespace code point is consumed; CRLF counts
# as a single whitespace).
_CSS_WS = frozenset(" \t\n\r\f")


def _decode_one_escape_css_free(run, i):
    """Decode one backslash escape at run[i] == '\\\\' with space-free CSS.

    Second interpretation of an escape run (F433), alongside the F429
    priority in _decode_one_escape. Per CSS Syntax Level 3 4.3.7, a CSS
    escape is backslash + 1-6 hex digits; the escape ends at the first
    non-hex code point, which is NOT consumed (so ``\\\\78o`` is 'x'
    followed by literal 'o'), and a single following whitespace is
    consumed as part of the escape. There is deliberately NO octal
    fallback here: in a CSS context ``\\\\62`` is 0x62 ('b'), never
    octal -- the F429 octal interpretation is tried separately by
    _try_escape_decode, so a run decodes under whichever genuine
    encoding the attacker used. Returns (char, next_index) or None
    when malformed -- the whole run is then ignored. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(run)
    c = run[i + 1]
    if c == "u":
        h = run[i + 2:i + 6]
        if len(h) != 4 or any(ch not in _HEX_DIGITS for ch in h):
            return None
        ch = _safe_chr(int(h, 16))
        return (ch, i + 6) if ch is not None else None
    if c == "U":
        h = run[i + 2:i + 10]
        if len(h) != 8 or any(ch not in _HEX_DIGITS for ch in h):
            return None
        ch = _safe_chr(int(h, 16))
        return (ch, i + 10) if ch is not None else None
    if c == "x":
        h = run[i + 2:i + 4]
        if len(h) != 2 or any(ch not in _HEX_DIGITS for ch in h):
            return None
        ch = _safe_chr(int(h, 16))
        return (ch, i + 4) if ch is not None else None
    j = i + 1
    while j < n and j < i + 7 and run[j] in _HEX_DIGITS:
        j += 1
    if j == i + 1:
        return None
    if j < n and run[j] in _HEX_DIGITS:
        # 7+ hex digits: not a strict single escape; the whole run is
        # ignored rather than guessed at.
        return None
    ch = _safe_chr(int(run[i + 1:j], 16))
    if ch is None:
        return None
    if j < n and run[j] in _CSS_WS:
        # Consume one whitespace terminator (CRLF counts as one).
        if run[j] == "\r" and j + 1 < n and run[j + 1] == "\n":
            j += 1
        j += 1
    return ch, j


def _try_escape_decode_css_free(run):
    """Strict static decode of one escape run with space-free CSS (F433).

    Returns the decoded string, or None when the run is not fully
    decodable, decodes to non-text, or is unchanged. Never evals.
    """
    if len(run) > 6 * _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        return None
    out = []
    i = 0
    n = len(run)
    while i < n:
        if run[i] != "\\" or i + 1 >= n:
            return None
        r = _decode_one_escape_css_free(run, i)
        if r is None:
            return None
        ch, i = r
        out.append(ch)
    return _ascii_printable_or_none("".join(out))


def _try_entity_decode(run):
    """Strict static decode of one numeric-HTML-entity run (F429).

    Returns the decoded string, or None when any entity is malformed,
    out of range, a surrogate, or the decode is not ASCII text.
    """
    out = []
    pos = 0
    for m in _ENTITY_ONE.finditer(run):
        if m.start() != pos:
            return None
        body = m.group(1)
        cp = int(body[1:], 16) if body[0] in "xX" else int(body, 10)
        ch = _safe_chr(cp)
        if ch is None:
            return None
        out.append(ch)
        pos = m.end()
    if pos != len(run) or not out:
        return None
    return _ascii_printable_or_none("".join(out))


def _try_entity_decode_all(text):
    """Whole-text numeric-entity decode (F436): decode every numeric
    entity (strict &#..; / &#x..; and the legacy no-semicolon forms a
    real HTML parser recovers) in a single pass, returning one variant.

    Per-run decoding alone needs one decode layer per entity, so two
    single entities separated by base64 chunks (F436 residual b) --
    or a no-semicolon entity poisoning a base64 run (residual a) --
    cannot compose with the base64 layer inside the 2-layer budget.
    One whole-text variant keeps entity+base64 stacks closable in 2
    layers; undecodable entities are left for the per-run path.

    Returns the decoded text, or None when the text holds no entities
    or any entity is malformed, out of range, a surrogate, or the
    decode is not ASCII text (fail closed: no variant). Bounded by
    _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS entities; static; never executes.
    """
    if "&#" not in text:
        return None
    out = []
    pos = 0
    n = 0
    for m in _ENTITY_ONE.finditer(text):
        body = m.group(1)
        cp = int(body[1:], 16) if body[0] in "xX" else int(body, 10)
        ch = _safe_chr(cp)
        if ch is None:
            return None
        out.append(text[pos:m.start()])
        out.append(ch)
        pos = m.end()
        n += 1
        if n > _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS:
            return None
    if n == 0:
        return None
    out.append(text[pos:])
    return _ascii_printable_or_none("".join(out))


def _try_entity_nul_decode_all(text):
    """Whole-text entity+NUL composition decode (F438): strip NUL
    bytes, then decode every numeric HTML entity in one pass.

    The F438 shape (`&#xHH;\\x00&#xHH;\\x00...`) defeats each
    existing layer on its own: per-run decoding decodes each
    entity+NUL pair as its own single variant (spreading the
    8-candidate budget), and the F436 whole-text entity pass alone
    leaves the NUL bytes in place, so the ASCII-printable gate
    rejects the result -- the fully-decoded + NUL-stripped form is
    unreachable within the depth-2 composition budget. Stripping
    NULs first is the honest decode order (a real chain is
    html.unescape then a control-strip), and it costs one decode
    layer, leaving the depth-2 design bound intact: after stripping,
    the entities still decode under the same bounded
    _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS budget. Malformed references stay literal
    (faithful to html.unescape, which only decodes valid
    references); out-of-range / surrogate / non-ASCII decodes fail
    closed to None (no variant).

    Returns the decoded text, or None when the text holds no NULs,
    no entities, or any matched entity decode fails. Static; never
    executes.
    """
    if "&#" not in text or "\x00" not in text:
        return None
    return _try_entity_decode_all(text.replace("\x00", ""))


def _try_escape_decode_all(text):
    """Whole-text backslash-escape decode (F436): decode every escape
    run under one interpretation in a single pass, returning one
    variant per interpretation (F429 priority via _try_escape_decode,
    space-free CSS via _try_escape_decode_css_free -- the same two
    interpretations the per-run path tries).

    Per-run decoding alone needs one decode layer per escape run, so
    two single escape fragments separated by base64 chunks (the escape
    analogue of F436 residual b) cannot compose with the base64 layer
    inside the 2-layer budget. One whole-text variant per
    interpretation keeps escape+base64 stacks closable in 2 layers.

    Returns a list of 0-2 decoded variants (an interpretation is
    skipped when any run fails under it -- fail closed, the per-run
    path still covers individually-decodable runs). Bounded by
    _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS runs; static; never executes.
    """
    if "\\" not in text:
        return []
    runs = list(_ESC_RUN.finditer(text))[:_MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS + 1]
    if not runs or len(runs) > _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS:
        return []
    variants = []
    for decode in (_try_escape_decode, _try_escape_decode_css_free):
        out = []
        pos = 0
        ok = True
        for m in runs:
            decoded = decode(m.group(0))
            if decoded is None:
                ok = False
                break
            out.append(text[pos:m.start()])
            out.append(decoded)
            pos = m.end()
        if not ok:
            continue
        out.append(text[pos:])
        gated = _ascii_printable_or_none("".join(out))
        if gated is not None and gated != text:
            variants.append(gated)
    return variants


def _try_bslash_unescape_all(text):
    """Escaped-backslash productions (F441): collapse doubled
    backslashes, then decode the exposed escapes.

    Models the first pass of a real double-``unicode_escape`` parser
    stack: ``\\\\uXXXX`` resolves to ``\\uXXXX`` (escaped-backslash
    collapse), which the second pass decodes to the char. Returns up
    to three variants: the pure collapse (the existing escape-run
    layer supplies the second pass -- composes in 2 layers), plus the
    collapse decoded under each whole-text escape interpretation
    (keeps URL-stacked double-backslash shapes inside the 2-layer
    decode budget: URL layer first, this production second).

    Strictly reduces the backslash count on every application, so a
    re-application reaches a fixpoint (pinned -- no decode loop).
    Returns [] when no doubled backslash occurs, the pair count
    exceeds _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS, or the collapse is not ASCII text
    (fail closed). Bounded, static, never executes.
    """
    if "\\\\" not in text:
        return []
    if text.count("\\\\") > _MAX_WHOLETEXT_ITEMS:
        return []
    collapsed = _ascii_printable_or_none(text.replace("\\\\", "\\"))
    if collapsed is None:
        return []
    variants = []
    if collapsed != text:
        variants.append(collapsed)
    for v in _try_escape_decode_all(collapsed):
        variants.append(v)
    return variants


def _try_utf16_null_decode(run):
    """Decode one UTF-16 null-interleaved run (F429) by stripping NULs."""
    return _ascii_printable_or_none(run.replace("\x00", ""))


def _normalize_confusables(text):
    """Whole-string confusable normalization variant (F429, F431, F435).

    NFKC folds fullwidth and compatibility confusables to ASCII;
    zero-width joiners are stripped; then the bounded static
    Cyrillic-homoglyph map (F431) and the bounded static
    Greek/Cherokee-homoglyph map (F435) fold lookalikes NFKC does
    not cover (U+043E and kin, U+03BF and kin). Returns the
    normalized string, or None when it is unchanged. This is a
    VARIANT for the decode layer -- the original text is still
    scanned as-is.
    """
    norm = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", text)
    if _ZERO_WIDTH_RUN.search(norm):
        norm = _ZERO_WIDTH_RUN.sub("", norm)
    folded = norm.translate(_CYRILLIC_FOLD)
    if folded != norm:
        norm = folded
    folded = norm.translate(_GREEK_CHEROKEE_FOLD)
    if folded != norm:
        norm = folded
    return norm if norm != text else None


def _normalize_bidi(text):
    """Whole-string bidi-control normalization variant (F431).

    Strips every bidi-override control (U+202A-U+202E, U+2066-U+2069)
    and reverses right-to-left-governed runs (RLO, RLE) to visual
    order, approximating what a renderer displays: an override/embed/
    isolate opens a frame at the current position, PDF/PDI closes the
    innermost frame (reversing it when it is RTL-governed), unclosed
    frames close at end of string, unmatched pops are dropped. Beyond
    _MAX_BIDI_CONTROLS controls the variant degrades to strip-only
    (still deterministic). Returns the normalized string, or None when
    there is nothing to normalize. A VARIANT -- the original text is
    still scanned as-is.
    """
    if not any(c in _BIDI_ALL for c in text):
        return None
    if sum(1 for c in text if c in _BIDI_ALL) > _MAX_BIDI_CONTROLS:
        stripped = "".join(c for c in text if c not in _BIDI_ALL)
        return stripped if stripped != text else None
    out = []
    stack = []  # (reversing, out_start_index) frames
    for c in text:
        if c in _BIDI_REVERSING:
            stack.append((True, len(out)))
        elif c == _BIDI_LRO or c in _BIDI_EMBED or c in _BIDI_ISOLATE:
            stack.append((False, len(out)))
        elif c in _BIDI_POP:
            if stack:
                reversing, start = stack.pop()
                if reversing:
                    out[start:] = out[start:][::-1]
        else:
            out.append(c)
    for reversing, start in reversed(stack):
        if reversing:
            out[start:] = out[start:][::-1]
    norm = "".join(out)
    return norm if norm != text else None


class _JSExprFail(Exception):
    """A JS-expression run is not strictly decodable (fail closed)."""


_JS_EXPR_START = re.compile(r"['\"`\[]|String\.fromCharCode|String\.fromCodePoint|atob(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|decodeURIComponent(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|encodeURIComponent(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|String[ \t\n\r]*(\(|\.[ \t\n\r]*raw([ \t\n\r]*\(|[ \t\n\r]*\.[ \t\n\r]*call[ \t\n\r]*\()|\[[ \t\n\r]*['\"])|Array(\(|[ \t\n\r]*(\.[ \t\n\r]*(of|apply|call|from)|\[[ \t\n\r]*['\"]))|Array[ \t\n\r]*\.[ \t\n\r]*prototype|String[ \t\n\r]*\.[ \t\n\r]*prototype|Function[ \t\n\r]*\.[ \t\n\r]*prototype|Reflect([ \t\n\r]*(\?\.|\.)[ \t\n\r]*(construct|apply|get)|[ \t\n\r]*\[[ \t\n\r]*['\"`\[])|Object([ \t\n\r]*\.[ \t\n\r]*(assign|getPrototypeOf|getOwnPropertyDescriptor|getOwnPropertyNames|getOwnPropertyDescriptors|values|keys|entries|freeze|create|defineProperty|fromEntries|groupBy)|[ \t\n\r]*\[[ \t\n\r]*['\"])|structuredClone(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|Symbol(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|JSON(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|Number(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|Promise(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|Map[ \t\n\r]*\.[ \t\n\r]*groupBy(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|new[ \t\n\r]+Map(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|new[ \t\n\r]+WeakRef(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|new[ \t\n\r]+Proxy(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|new[ \t\n\r]+URLSearchParams(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|new[ \t\n\r]+URL(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|new[ \t\n\r]+TextDecoder(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|new[ \t\n\r]+String(?![_$A-Za-z0-9])|\(String[ \t\n\r]*\.[ \t\n\r]*raw[ \t\n\r]*`|\(")
# F536: the structuredClone anchor carries its own ident-boundary
# lookahead so `structuredClonex(` never anchors (no wasted F512
# total-attempt budget); the primary branch re-checks the boundary
# defensively.
# F539: `Object.freeze` joins the Object member anchor class (same
# ws-tolerant class as `assign`), so `Object .freeze(...)` reaches
# _js_parse_f539_object_identity. F548/F550: `Object.create` and
# `Object.defineProperty` join the same class (alternation members
# added to the existing pattern -- the module keeps its 28 compiled
# patterns), so they reach _js_parse_f548_object_create /
# _js_parse_f550_define_property. The `new WeakRef` anchor carries
# the same ident-boundary lookahead as structuredClone, so
# `new WeakRefx(` never anchors; the primary branch re-checks both
# keywords defensively via _js_match_kw.
# F455: the Array.of factory anchor tolerates whitespace around the dot
# ([ \t\n\r]*, exactly the _js_skip_ws class the parser skips), so
# `Array .of(...)` / `Array\t.of(...)` / `Array\n.of(...)` /
# `Array . of(...)` reach _js_parse_array_of -- the anchor/parser
# contradiction is closed. F476: the same class covers the
# bracket-member factory, so `Array['of'](...)` / `Array ["of"](...)`
# reach _js_parse_ctor_factory (`[].constructor.of` starts with `[`,
# already in the anchor's character class). F468: the same ws-tolerant
# anchor class
# covers Reflect.construct, so `Reflect .construct(...)` /
# `Reflect . construct(...)` reach _js_parse_reflect_construct. F469:
# the anchor class also covers Reflect.apply, so `Reflect .apply(...)` /
# `Reflect . apply(...)` reach _js_parse_reflect_apply_concat. F471: the
# same ws-tolerant anchor class covers Object.assign, so
# `Object .assign(...)` / `Object . assign(...)` reach
# _js_parse_object_assign. F477: the String() constructor call reaches
# _js_parse_string_ctor, so `String (...)` reaches the parser too. F480:
# the anchor class also covers the constructor-function dispatch
# (`Array .apply(` / `Array .call(`) and the bracket-string member forms
# (`Array['apply']`, `String['fromCharCode']`, `Object['assign']`,
# `Reflect['apply']`), so they reach the parsers below. `[].constructor`
# shapes start with `[`, already in the anchor's character class. F483:
# `Function.prototype` (double dispatch) is covered by the same
# ws-tolerant prototype class. F491: the anchor class also covers
# `Reflect.get(` (so `Reflect .get(` reaches _js_parse_reflect_get)
# and `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(` (reaching
# _js_parse_gopd_value). F493: the String alternative also covers
# `String.raw(` and `String.raw.call(` (ws-tolerant, so
# `String . raw (` / `String.raw . call (` reach _js_parse_string_raw);
# the tag form `String.raw`...`` keeps anchoring via the backtick
# character class, and F613 adds the `\(String.raw`` ` alternative so
# a parenthesized tag ((String.raw`f1`)+'f2') is reached at the group
# paren -- the inner parse then routes through _js_parse_primary's
# F613 tag branch. F504: the Reflect bracket-member class gains
# backtick and nested-bracket (so `Reflect[`apply`](` /
# `Reflect[['apply'][0]](` reach _js_parse_reflect_call),
# literal-alternation only. The added groups are alternations of character-class-bounded literals: matching stays
# linear (no ReDoS surface).
_JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS = 64        # bound on '+' operands per expression
_JS_EXPR_MAX_CODEPOINTS = 512  # bound on fromCharCode argument count
_JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH = 8         # bound on parenthesization depth
_JS_ARRAY_NEST_MAX = 8         # bound on array-literal nesting depth (F485)
_JS_EXPR_MAX_REPEAT = 64       # bound on .repeat(n)
_JS_EXPR_MAX_FILL = 64         # bound on Array(N) element count (F443)
_JS_EXPR_MAX_LITERAL = 4096    # bound on one string literal
_JS_EXPR_MAX_RUN = 100000      # bound on expression run length
_JS_EXPR_MAX_INT_DIGITS = 20  # bound on decimal int-literal digits
                              # (int() digit-limit safety; F477 needs
                              # at most 16 for the 2**53 bound)
_JS_SIMPLE_ESCAPES = {          # single-char backslash escapes (F457: shared
    "n": "\n", "t": "\t", "r": "\r", "b": "\b",   # by _js_parse_string and
    "f": "\f", "v": "\v", "0": "\0"}              # _js_parse_template)
_ASCII_DIGITS = frozenset("0123456789")
_JS_IDENT_START = frozenset(
    "_$abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ")
_JS_IDENT_CONT = frozenset(
    "_$abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789")


def _js_ident_cont_char(ch):
    """Identifier-continue check including Unicode (F567).

    ``_JS_IDENT_CONT`` is ASCII-only, but JS identifiers admit Unicode
    ID_Start/ID_Continue chars: node v24.20.0 parses ``éJSON`` as one
    identifier, so ``éJSON.parse(...)`` throws ReferenceError and the
    decoder must stay quiet. Categories per ECMAScript: ID_Start =
    Lu/Ll/Lt/Lm/Lo/Nl (plus ``$``/``_``, already in
    ``_JS_IDENT_CONT``); ID_Continue adds Mn/Mc/Nd/Pc. ZWNJ/ZWJ
    (category Cf) are also identifier-continue in JS but are excluded
    here -- a documented residual, vanishingly rare in practice.
    Never evals.
    """
    if ch in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        return True
    return unicodedata.category(ch) in (
        "Lu", "Ll", "Lt", "Lm", "Lo", "Nl", "Mn", "Mc", "Nd", "Pc")


def _js_left_boundary_ok(text, i):
    """Left ident-boundary guard for identifier anchors (F567).

    True when position *i* is not the suffix of a longer identifier:
    *i* is 0, or the character immediately left is not an
    identifier-continuation char (Unicode-aware via
    :func:`_js_ident_cont_char` -- ``éJSON`` is one identifier in
    node v24.20.0, so ``éJSON.parse`` throws ReferenceError). The
    caller applies this only to matches that START with an identifier
    char (``text[s] in _JS_IDENT_START``); quote, bracket, and paren
    anchors keep their existing behavior. Never evals.
    """
    return i == 0 or not _js_ident_cont_char(text[i - 1])


def _js_skip_ws(text, i):
    n = len(text)
    while i < n and text[i] in " \t\n\r":
        i += 1
    return i


def _js_parse_string(text, i):
    """Parse a '...' or "..." literal at text[i].

    Returns (value, next_index). Backtick template literals are
    handled by _js_parse_template (F457); they fail closed here.
    Never evals.
    """
    quote = text[i]
    if quote == "`":
        raise _JSExprFail("template handled separately")
    i += 1
    n = len(text)
    out = []
    while True:
        if i >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated string")
        c = text[i]
        if c == quote:
            return "".join(out), i + 1
        if c == "\\":
            if i + 1 >= n:
                raise _JSExprFail("bad escape")
            e = text[i + 1]
            if e in "xuU":
                r = _decode_one_escape(text, i)
                if r is None:
                    raise _JSExprFail("bad escape")
                ch, i = r
                out.append(ch)
                continue
            out.append(_JS_SIMPLE_ESCAPES.get(e, e))
            i += 2
            continue
        if ord(c) < 0x20 and c not in "\t\n\r":
            raise _JSExprFail("control char")
        out.append(c)
        i += 1
        if len(out) > _JS_EXPR_MAX_LITERAL:
            raise _JSExprFail("literal too long")


def _js_parse_template_interp(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse the inside of ${ ... } at text[i] (F457): one string literal.

    Returns (value, next_index) positioned after the closing '}'.
    Only a single string literal is modeled -- '...', "...", or a
    literal-only nested template (recursion is depth-bounded). A bare
    ${}, a variable, a call, or an additive/nested-expression payload
    fails closed (never evals). Whitespace around the payload is
    tolerated exactly as the engine allows.
    """
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    n = len(text)
    if i >= n:
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated interpolation")
    c = text[i]
    if c in "'\"":
        val, i = _js_parse_string(text, i)
    elif c == "`":
        val, i = _js_parse_template(text, i, depth, state)
    elif c in _ASCII_DIGITS:
        # F633: ${123n} -- BigInt literal interpolation. The engine
        # stringifies the BigInt; _JSBigInt carries the node-exact
        # decimal repr. Non-decimal forms and non-'n'-suffixed
        # numbers fail closed here (the additive-literal class stays
        # literal-only).
        j = i
        while j < n and text[j] in _ASCII_DIGITS:
            j += 1
        if not (j < n and text[j] == "n" and (
                j + 1 >= n or text[j + 1] not in _JS_IDENT_CONT)):
            raise _JSExprFail("non-literal interpolation")
        if j - i > _JS_EXPR_MAX_INT_DIGITS:
            raise _JSExprFail("bigint too big")
        if j - i > 1 and text[i] == "0":
            raise _JSExprFail("bigint leading zero")
        val = _JSBigInt(str(int(text[i:j])))
        i = j + 1
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("non-literal interpolation")
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    if i >= n or text[i] != "}":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated interpolation")
    return val, i + 1


def _js_parse_template(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse a `...` template literal at text[i] (F457).

    ${...} interpolation is modeled ONLY when every interpolation is
    a string literal (see _js_parse_template_interp): the parts then
    concatenate exactly as the engine does, since String(literal) is
    the literal itself. Any non-literal interpolation fails closed.
    An escaped \\${ stays the literal characters "${" (the escape
    path consumes the backslash before any ${ check, exactly as the
    engine tokenizes). Nested templates are literal-only or fail
    closed; tagged templates decode the template part as if untagged
    (a documented over-approximation in the fail-closed direction --
    the raw text is always checked first, so this can only add
    blocks). Each interpolation is charged to the part
    budget and marks the expression obfuscation-shaped; the assembled
    output is charged to _MAX_DECODE_BYTES. Depth, literal, and part
    bounds apply; no new regexes; never evals.
    """
    if depth >= _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH:
        raise _JSExprFail("template too deep")
    if text[i] != "`":
        raise _JSExprFail("not a template")
    i += 1  # consume the opening backtick
    n = len(text)
    out = []
    total = 0
    while True:
        if i >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated template")
        c = text[i]
        if c == "`":
            return "".join(out), i + 1
        if c == "$" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "{":
            val, i = _js_parse_template_interp(text, i + 2, depth + 1,
                                               state)
            out.append(val)
            total += len(val)
            if total > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
                raise _JSExprFail("template too long")
            state["parts"] += 1
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            state["ops"] += 1  # an interpolation is obfuscation-shaped
            continue
        if c == "\\":
            if i + 1 >= n:
                raise _JSExprFail("bad escape")
            e = text[i + 1]
            if e in "xuU":
                r = _decode_one_escape(text, i)
                if r is None:
                    raise _JSExprFail("bad escape")
                ch, i = r
                out.append(ch)
                total += len(ch)
            else:
                out.append(_JS_SIMPLE_ESCAPES.get(e, e))
                i += 2
                total += 1
            if total > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
                raise _JSExprFail("template too long")
            continue
        if ord(c) < 0x20 and c not in "\t\n\r":
            raise _JSExprFail("control char")
        out.append(c)
        total += 1
        i += 1
        if total > _JS_EXPR_MAX_LITERAL:
            raise _JSExprFail("literal too long")
        if total > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
            raise _JSExprFail("template too long")


def _js_parse_tag_template(text, i, state):
    """Parse a literal-only `...` tag template at text[i] (F463).

    Unlike _js_parse_template (F457), ANY ${ interpolation fails closed:
    a tagged call hands the engine a TemplateStringsArray, and an
    interpolated tag would make the modeled separator diverge from the
    engine's (String(strings) joins the cooked strings with ",").
    Only literal-only templates are modeled, so the array is exactly
    [cooked] and String([cooked]) is cooked. An escaped \\${ stays the
    literal characters "${" -- the escape path consumes the backslash
    first, exactly as the engine tokenizes (same as _js_parse_template).
    Returns (cooked, next_index). Escape handling, literal, and byte
    bounds match _js_parse_template; no new regexes; never evals.
    """
    if text[i] != "`":
        raise _JSExprFail("not a template")
    i += 1  # consume the opening backtick
    n = len(text)
    out = []
    total = 0
    while True:
        if i >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated template")
        c = text[i]
        if c == "`":
            return "".join(out), i + 1
        if c == "$" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "{":
            raise _JSExprFail("tag interpolation unsupported")
        if c == "\\":
            if i + 1 >= n:
                raise _JSExprFail("bad escape")
            e = text[i + 1]
            if e in "xuU":
                r = _decode_one_escape(text, i)
                if r is None:
                    raise _JSExprFail("bad escape")
                ch, i = r
                out.append(ch)
                total += len(ch)
            else:
                out.append(_JS_SIMPLE_ESCAPES.get(e, e))
                i += 2
                total += 1
            if total > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
                raise _JSExprFail("template too long")
            continue
        if ord(c) < 0x20 and c not in "\t\n\r":
            raise _JSExprFail("control char")
        out.append(c)
        total += 1
        i += 1
        if total > _JS_EXPR_MAX_LITERAL:
            raise _JSExprFail("literal too long")
        if total > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
            raise _JSExprFail("template too long")


def _js_parse_raw_tag_template(text, i, state):
    """Parse the raw text of a literal-only `...` tag template at text[i] (F613).

    The RAW-text twin of _js_parse_tag_template: String.raw`...` returns
    the template's raw strings (escapes uninterpreted), not the cooked
    value. For a literal-only template the raw value is exactly the
    source characters between the backticks, so a backslash escape
    contributes BOTH characters (node v24.20.0: String.raw`a\\nb` ===
    "a\\\\nb"), unlike the cooked twin which decodes them. ${...}
    interpolation fails closed -- the same discipline as the cooked
    twin (the substitution form keeps its existing anchor-at-backtick
    cooked handling, the F463 control). Bounds match
    _js_parse_tag_template; no new regexes; never evals.
    """
    if text[i] != "`":
        raise _JSExprFail("not a template")
    i += 1  # consume the opening backtick
    n = len(text)
    out = []
    total = 0
    while True:
        if i >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated template")
        c = text[i]
        if c == "`":
            return "".join(out), i + 1
        if c == "$" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "{":
            raise _JSExprFail("tag interpolation unsupported")
        if c == "\\":
            if i + 1 >= n:
                raise _JSExprFail("bad escape")
            # Raw: the engine does not interpret escapes in the raw
            # strings -- both characters are kept literally.
            out.append("\\")
            out.append(text[i + 1])
            i += 2
            total += 2
        else:
            if ord(c) < 0x20 and c not in "\t\n\r":
                raise _JSExprFail("control char")
            out.append(c)
            i += 1
            total += 1
        if total > _JS_EXPR_MAX_LITERAL:
            raise _JSExprFail("literal too long")
        if total > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
            raise _JSExprFail("template too long")


def _js_parse_number(text, i):
    """Parse a decimal or 0x integer literal at text[i] (ASCII only)."""
    n = len(text)
    if text.startswith(("0x", "0X"), i):
        j = i + 2
        k = j
        while k < n and text[k] in _HEX_DIGITS:
            k += 1
        if k == j:
            raise _JSExprFail("bad hex number")
        if k - j > 8:  # int() digit-limit safety: fail closed, not loud
            raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
        val = int(text[j:k], 16)
        if val > 0x10FFFF:
            raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
        return val, k
    else:
        j = i
        while j < n and text[j] in _ASCII_DIGITS:
            j += 1
        if j == i:
            raise _JSExprFail("not a number")
        if j - i > 7:  # 0x10FFFF needs 7 decimal digits at most
            raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
        val = int(text[i:j])
    if val > 0x10FFFF:
        raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
    return val, j


_OCT_DIGITS = frozenset("01234567")
_BIN_DIGITS = frozenset("01")


def _js_scan_int_digits(text, i, digits):
    """Scan ASCII digits (with numeric separators) at text[i].

    Separators are allowed only between two digits -- node-verified:
    leading, trailing, doubled, and prefix-adjacent separators are
    SyntaxErrors, so the caller fails closed on them. Returns
    (digit_string_without_separators, next_index); the digit string is
    empty when no digit was scanned.
    """
    n = len(text)
    out = []
    k = i
    while k < n:
        c = text[k]
        if c in digits:
            out.append(c)
            k += 1
        elif c == "_" and out and k + 1 < n and text[k + 1] in digits:
            out.append(text[k + 1])
            k += 2
        else:
            break
    return "".join(out), k


def _js_parse_fcc_number(text, i):
    """Parse one String.fromCharCode integer-literal argument (F458).

    Covers the engine's integer-literal spellings, all node-verified
    against node v24.20.0: decimal with numeric separators (1_20),
    0x/0X hex (with separators), 0o/0O octal (with separators),
    0b/0B binary (with separators), and sloppy-mode legacy octal
    (0170 -> 120; no separators -- 01_41 is a SyntaxError in node).
    A legacy 0NNN containing 8/9 is decimal per sloppy semantics
    (08 -> 8, node-verified). Mixed-radix argument lists work because
    each argument is parsed independently by _js_parse_fromcharcode.
    Anything else -- floats, exponents, signs, bad separators,
    invalid radix digits -- fails closed. ASCII only; the value bound
    is 0x10FFFF, matching _js_parse_number. Deliberately separate
    from _js_parse_number so .repeat()/Array(N) keep their existing
    (narrower) number grammar.
    """
    n = len(text)
    for p0, p1, radix, digits, maxdig in (("0x", "0X", 16, _HEX_DIGITS, 8),
                                         ("0o", "0O", 8, _OCT_DIGITS, 7),
                                         ("0b", "0B", 2, _BIN_DIGITS, 21)):
        if text.startswith((p0, p1), i):
            raw, k = _js_scan_int_digits(text, i + 2, digits)
            if not raw:
                raise _JSExprFail("bad fcc number")
            if len(raw) > maxdig:  # int() digit-limit safety
                raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
            val = int(raw, radix)
            if val > 0x10FFFF:
                raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
            return val, k
    if i < n and text[i] == "0":
        # Sloppy-mode legacy octal: 0NNN with N in 0-7. A lone "0"
        # returns 0 here (k == j); floats/exponents fail closed at the
        # argument separator in _js_parse_fromcharcode.
        j = i + 1
        k = j
        while k < n and text[k] in _ASCII_DIGITS:
            k += 1
        if k == j:
            return 0, j
        digs = text[j:k]
        if all(c in _OCT_DIGITS for c in digs):
            val = int(digs, 8)
        else:
            val = int(digs)  # 8/9 -> sloppy decimal fallback (08 -> 8)
        if val > 0x10FFFF:
            raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
        return val, k
    raw, k = _js_scan_int_digits(text, i, _ASCII_DIGITS)
    if not raw:
        raise _JSExprFail("not a number")
    if len(raw) > 7:  # 0x10FFFF needs 7 decimal digits at most
        raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
    val = int(raw)
    if val > 0x10FFFF:
        raise _JSExprFail("number too big")
    return val, k


def _js_stringify_coercible(arg, label):
    """Engine-exact String() coercion of a parsed primary (F477).

    Arrays coerce via comma-join (F464 toString); F479: array elements
    coerce engine-exactly (null/undefined -> "", booleans ->
    "true"/"false", nested single-level arrays -> their toString).
    Numbers via their node-exact decimal repr (_JSNumStr carries it,
    and is a str subclass so the str branch covers it); plain strings
    are identity. Anything else -- objects -- fails closed. Never
    evals.
    """
    if isinstance(arg, _JSArr):
        return ",".join(_js_join_element(e) for e in arg.elements)
    if isinstance(arg, str):
        return str(arg)
    raise _JSExprFail(label + ": not coercible")


def _js_parse_raw_template_arg(text, j, state):
    """Parse `{raw:[...]}` / `{raw:{0:'lit',...,length:N}}` at text[j]
    for `String.raw` (F493 / F575).

    The template object must have exactly one key, `raw`. The raw value
    is either an array literal of string literals (F493 -- the
    _js_parse_string_literal_list grammar; numeric, nested, marker,
    additive, or template segments fail closed, the same fail-closed
    classes as the F454/F446 factories) or an array-like object literal
    (F575 -- parsed by _js_parse_raw_arraylike: integer/string/numeric
    keys with later-wins, string-literal values, canonical-int length).
    With no substitutions the engine concatenates the raw segments, so
    the decoded value is the plain join. Returns (segments,
    idx_after_}). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "{":
        raise _JSExprFail("String.raw arg not an object")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if not text.startswith("raw", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("String.raw object without raw key")
    k = j + len("raw")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("rawx is not raw")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ":":
        raise _JSExprFail("String.raw raw without colon")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        segs, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j + 1, state, "]")
    elif j < n and text[j] == "{":
        # F575: array-like raw object -- String.raw reads it via
        # LengthOfArrayLike + indexed Gets (node-exact).
        segs, j = _js_parse_raw_arraylike(text, j, state)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("String.raw raw not an array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "}":
        raise _JSExprFail("String.raw object not closed")
    return segs, j + 1


def _js_parse_raw_arraylike(text, j, state):
    """Parse `{0:'lit',1:'lit',length:2}` at text[j] for `String.raw` (F575).

    Node-exact within the modeled literal grammar (v24.20.0): String.raw's
    spec reads ToObject(Get(template,
    "raw")), then LengthOfArrayLike (ToLength(Get(raw, "length"))), then
    ToString(Get(raw, k)) for k in 0..length-1. Keys are identifiers,
    string literals, or numeric literals (later-wins, engine-identical);
    only canonical decimal index strings are ever read -- a quoted
    non-canonical key (e.g. '00') is a distinct string key the loop never
    observes (node-verified), while an unquoted numeric key canonicalizes
    via ToString(number) (e.g. 00 -> '0', node-verified). A missing index
    contributes the literal string "undefined" (ToString(undefined),
    node-verified); a missing length is ToLength(undefined) == 0
    (node-verified: String.raw({raw:{0:'f1'}}) === ''). Values must be
    string literals and a present length a canonical decimal-int literal
    (no leading zeros, no sign) -- anything else fails closed (documented
    scope boundary; the close-when permits coded refusal). The parts
    budget is charged per materialized segment (F452 lesson); lengths
    above 4096 fail closed outright. Returns (segments, idx_after_}).
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)  # consume '{'
    vals = {}
    length = 0
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == "}":
            j += 1
            break
        if j < n and text[j] in "'\"":
            key, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
        elif j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_START:
            key, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        elif j < n and text[j].isdigit():
            m = j
            while m < n and text[m].isdigit():
                m += 1
            if m - j > 4:
                # Numeric keys longer than 4 digits fail closed: runs this
                # long denote indices >= 10000 (never read by the index
                # loop, length <= 4096), but leading-zero forms (00001 ->
                # '1') would canonicalize to an observable key and
                # ToString(number) goes exponential past 1e21 -- the only
                # exactly-modelable class is the short canonicalizable
                # one (documented scope boundary). This also keeps int()
                # off unbounded attacker digit runs (ValueError would
                # escape fail-closed).
                raise _JSExprFail("F575 raw array-like long numeric key")
            key = str(int(text[j:m]))  # ToString(number), exact below 10000
            j = m
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("F575 raw array-like bad key")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n or text[j] != ":":
            raise _JSExprFail("F575 raw array-like without colon")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if key == "length":
            m = j
            while m < n and text[m].isdigit():
                m += 1
            digits = text[j:m]
            if (not digits or (len(digits) > 1 and digits[0] == "0")
                    or (m < n and text[m] in _JS_IDENT_CONT)):
                raise _JSExprFail("F575 raw array-like bad length")
            if len(digits) > 4:
                # Longer than 4 digits always exceeds the 4096 cap; check
                # before int() so unbounded attacker digit runs cannot
                # raise ValueError past fail-closed.
                raise _JSExprFail("F575 raw array-like length too large")
            length = int(digits)
            if length > 4096:
                raise _JSExprFail("F575 raw array-like length too large")
            j = m
        else:
            if j >= n or text[j] not in "'\"":
                raise _JSExprFail("F575 raw array-like non-literal value")
            val, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
            vals[key] = val
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == ",":
            j += 1
            continue
        if j < n and text[j] == "}":
            j += 1
            break
        raise _JSExprFail("F575 raw array-like bad separator")
    segs = []
    for k in range(length):
        segs.append(vals.get(str(k), "undefined"))
        state["parts"] += 1  # each materialized segment is obfuscation-shaped
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    return segs, j


def _js_parse_raw_substitutions(text, j, state):
    """Parse zero or more `, 'lit'` substitution args for `String.raw`
    (F551).

    Returns (subs, next_index) positioned at the first unconsumed char
    (the closing paren when well-formed). Each substitution must be a
    string literal -- template, additive, numeric, and non-literal
    args fail closed (the engine ToString-coerces substitutions, which
    the static decoder cannot model -- documented scope boundary:
    rejected, never mis-decoded). A trailing comma fails closed, the
    same as the F454/F446 literal-list grammar. The parts budget is
    charged per substitution, so substitution runs cannot buy
    unbounded work. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    subs = []
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
            return subs, j
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if j >= n or text[j] not in "'\"":
            raise _JSExprFail("String.raw substitution not a literal")
        sub, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
        state["parts"] += 1  # the substitution is obfuscation-shaped
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        subs.append(sub)


def _js_interleave_raw_substitutions(segs, subs):
    """Node-exact String.raw raw/substitution interleave (F551).

    Engine semantics (node v24.20.0 verified): raw[0] + sub[0] +
    raw[1] + sub[1] + ... + raw[n-1] -- a substitution is appended
    only while a following raw segment exists AND the substitution is
    present. Missing substitutions contribute nothing (never the
    string "undefined"), extra substitutions are dropped, and the
    last substitution has no following segment so it is always
    dropped.
    """
    parts = []
    for k, seg in enumerate(segs):
        parts.append(seg)
        if k < len(segs) - 1 and k < len(subs):
            parts.append(subs[k])
    return "".join(parts)


def _js_parse_string_raw(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `String.raw({raw:[...]})` / `String.raw.call(R, {raw:[...]})`
    (and the F575 array-like-raw-object variants) at text[i]
    (F493, substitutions F551, array-like raw F575).

    The call form of String.raw. The template object `{raw:[...]}`
    parses exactly as in F493 (string-literal-only raw segments --
    node-verified: the engine uses the already-cooked array element
    values verbatim, so the cooked literal grammar is node-exact).
    The `.call` dispatch's thisArg is parsed and discarded (the engine
    ignores it); `.apply`/`.bind` fail closed.

    F551: optional substitution arguments interleave node-exactly
    (raw[0] + sub[0] + raw[1] + ... + raw[n-1], missing subs skipped,
    extra subs dropped). Only string-literal substitutions are
    modeled; non-literal substitutions fail closed (documented).

    The tag form `` String.raw`...` `` is parsed by the caller
    (_js_parse_primary routes a following backtick to
    _js_parse_raw_tag_template, F613); it keeps its F463/F457
    handling when the scan anchors at the backtick itself -- the
    leading-position and substitution forms are unchanged controls.

    Returns (interleaved_string, next_index); the caller applies the
    postfix chain and the ops charge. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + len("String"))
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Stringx is not String")
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("String without dot")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if not text.startswith("raw", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("String member not raw")
    k = j + len("raw")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("rawx is not raw")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j < n and text[j] == ".":
        # F493 dispatch form: String.raw.call(R, {raw:[...]}). The
        # dispatch head commits the parse; only `.call` is modeled.
        peek = _js_peek_dispatch_call(text, j)
        if peek is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("String.raw without call form")
        dname, paren = peek
        if dname != "call":
            raise _JSExprFail("String.raw apply/bind not modeled")
        p = _js_skip_ws(text, paren + 1)
        # The .call thisArg is parsed and discarded (the engine
        # ignores it for String.raw). `_js_parse_ignored_value`
        # covers markers and additive values; a bare identifier
        # thisArg (`String.raw.call(String, ...)`, the F551 filed
        # shape) is not an additive value, so it is skipped as a
        # plain identifier -- node-verified, the engine ignores any
        # thisArg here. Anything else fails closed.
        try:
            _, p = _js_parse_ignored_value(text, p, depth, state)
        except _JSExprFail:
            _, p = _js_parse_ident(text, p)
        p = _js_skip_ws(text, p)
        if p >= n or text[p] != ",":
            raise _JSExprFail("String.raw.call without template arg")
        segs, p = _js_parse_raw_template_arg(text, p + 1, state)
        subs, p = _js_parse_raw_substitutions(text, p, state)
        p = _js_skip_ws(text, p)
        if p >= n or text[p] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("String.raw.call unterminated")
        result = _js_interleave_raw_substitutions(segs, subs)
        if len(result) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
            raise _JSExprFail("String.raw too long")
        return result, p + 1
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("String.raw without paren")
    segs, j = _js_parse_raw_template_arg(text, j + 1, state)
    subs, j = _js_parse_raw_substitutions(text, j, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("String.raw unterminated")
    result = _js_interleave_raw_substitutions(segs, subs)
    if len(result) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        raise _JSExprFail("String.raw too long")
    return result, j + 1


def _js_parse_string_ctor(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse 'String(x)' at text[i] (F477); returns (value, idx).

    Engine-exact String() coercion for the modeled argument classes:
      String(['a','b']) === ['a','b'].toString() === 'a,b' (F464 --
        array args coerce via comma-join; nested non-string elements
        fail closed)
      String(123) === '123' (numbers via their node-exact decimal
        repr; _JSNumStr already carries it)
      String('s') === 's'; String() === ''
    Anything else -- null/undefined/object args (String() is
    'null'/'undefined'/'[object Object]', not modeled), 2+ args
    (extra args are ignored by the engine, not modeled), nested
    arrays, template-literal args (F457 over-approximation would
    apply) -- fails closed. Never evals.
    """
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + len("String"))
    if j >= len(text) or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("not String()")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j < len(text) and text[j] == ")":
        state["ops"] += 1
        return "", j + 1  # String() === ""
    arg, j = _js_parse_additive(text, j, depth, state,
                                _forbid_template=True)
    coerced = _js_stringify_coercible(arg, "String()")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= len(text) or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("bad String()")
    if len(coerced) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        raise _JSExprFail("String() too long")
    state["ops"] += 1
    return coerced, j + 1


def _js_parse_coerced_string_arg(text, i, depth, state, label):
    """Parse a string literal or a String()-coercible array/number (F477).

    The engine coerces non-string padStart/padEnd pad args and
    replace() replacements via String(): arrays via comma-join
    (F464 toString), numbers via their decimal repr. Try a string
    literal first -- but only when text[i] is a real quote char:
    _js_parse_string treats text[i] as the delimiter
    unconditionally, so a leading digit would mis-decode
    ('a'.padStart(5,121) -> '2222a'; node '1211a'). Failing the
    literal, parse a primary and coerce a parsed string array (all
    elements must be strings; nested arrays fail closed) or a number
    (_JSNumStr carries the node-exact repr). Template-literal args
    fail closed (F457 over-approximation would apply); anything else
    fails closed. Never evals.
    """
    if i < len(text) and text[i] in "'\"":
        try:
            return _js_parse_string(text, i)
        except _JSExprFail:
            pass
    arg, i = _js_parse_primary(text, i, depth, state,
                                _forbid_template=True)
    return _js_stringify_coercible(arg, label), i


def _js_parse_fromcharcode(text, i, state=None):
    """Parse String.fromCharCode(n, ...) at text[i]; returns (value, idx)."""
    return _js_parse_fromcharcode_args(text, i + len("String.fromCharCode"),
                                       state=state)


def _js_parse_fromcodepoint(text, i, state=None):
    """Parse String.fromCodePoint(n, ...) at text[i] (F505);
    returns (value, idx).

    Shares the F458/F500 arg-list parser with fromCharCode: the
    integer-literal grammar's RangeError classes (cap 0x10FFFF, no
    negatives, no floats/exponents) are node-identical for
    fromCodePoint (v24.20.0), and _safe_chr is node-exact for
    0..0x10FFFF except lone surrogates, which fail closed. Spread
    array-literal operands decode engine-exactly. Never evals.
    """
    return _js_parse_fromcharcode_args(text, i + len("String.fromCodePoint"),
                                       state=state)


def _js_parse_f742_getuint8_arg(text, i, state):
    """Parse `v.getUint8(k)` at text[i] (F742); returns (char, idx).

    v must name a DataView tracked in state["_f742_dataviews"]
    (a bytearray, zero-initialized like the engine, mutated only by
    modeled setUint8 statements); k is an F458 integer literal. An
    out-of-range k is a RangeError in the engine -- fail closed.
    The env is set only by _js_parse_f742_dataview_iife around its
    return additive; without it this fails closed exactly as the
    F458 grammar did before, so no previously-decoded input
    changes behavior. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    env = state.get("_f742_dataviews") if state else None
    if not env:
        raise _JSExprFail("F742 no dataview env")
    name, j = _js_parse_ident(text, i)
    buf = env.get(name)
    if buf is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F742 unknown dataview")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("F742 getUint8 needs dot")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    m = _js_match_kw(text, j, "getUint8")
    if m is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F742 not getUint8")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, m)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("F742 getUint8 needs paren")
    idx, j = _js_parse_fcc_number(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1))
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("F742 getUint8 needs rparen")
    if idx < 0 or idx >= len(buf):
        raise _JSExprFail("F742 getUint8 out of range")
    ch = _safe_chr(buf[idx])
    if ch is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F742 bad byte")
    return ch, j + 1


def _js_parse_fromcharcode_spread_inner(text, i, state=None):
    """Parse `[n, n, ...]` at text[i] (F500); returns (value, idx_after_bracket).

    The array-literal operand of a fromCharCode spread: integer-literal
    codepoints in the F458 grammar, `]`-terminated. Never evals.
    """
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    if i >= len(text) or text[i] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("fromCharCode spread needs array")
    # Spread operands stay integer-literal-only (F500/F643); the
    # F742 getUint8 alternative is not modeled inside spreads.
    return _js_parse_fromcharcode_args_inner(text, i + 1, "]",
                                             state=None)


def _f643_ta_u8(v):
    return v & 0xFF


def _f643_ta_i8(v):
    v &= 0xFF
    return v - 256 if v >= 128 else v


def _f643_ta_u8c(v):
    return 255 if v > 255 else v


def _f643_ta_u16(v):
    return v & 0xFFFF


def _f643_ta_i16(v):
    v &= 0xFFFF
    return v - 65536 if v >= 32768 else v


def _f643_ta_u32(v):
    return v


def _f643_ta_i32(v):
    return v


_F643_TYPED_ARRAYS = (
    ("Uint8ClampedArray", _f643_ta_u8c),
    ("Uint8Array", _f643_ta_u8),
    ("Int8Array", _f643_ta_i8),
    ("Uint16Array", _f643_ta_u16),
    ("Int16Array", _f643_ta_i16),
    ("Uint32Array", _f643_ta_u32),
    ("Int32Array", _f643_ta_i32),
)


def _js_parse_f643_typed_array(text, i):
    """Parse `new <IntTypedArray>([n, n, ...])` at text[i] (F643);
    returns (decoded_str, idx_after_paren).

    The typed-array operand of a fromCharCode/fromCodePoint spread or
    .apply() argsArray. Integer typed arrays only (Uint8Array,
    Int8Array, Uint8ClampedArray, Uint16Array, Int16Array,
    Uint32Array, Int32Array): one array-literal argument of F458
    integer literals, `]`-then-`)` terminated. Each literal converts
    with the engine's typed-element semantics (node v24.20.0
    verified): Uint8/Uint16 wrap mod 2**N, Int8/Int16 wrap then sign,
    Uint8Clamped clamps 0..255, Uint32/Int32 are identity on the F458
    range (<= 0x10FFFF). Converted values pass the same _safe_chr
    gate as literal codepoints (fromCharCode and fromCodePoint share
    the gate per F505): negatives and lone surrogates fail closed --
    for fromCodePoint the engine throws RangeError there too, so the
    refusal is engine-exact; for fromCharCode the engine wraps
    (ToUint16) but a negative int-array value can only wrap to
    >= 0xFF80, never ASCII, so the refusal cannot hide a credential.
    Uint32Array values in 0x10000..0x10FFFF inherit the pre-existing
    F458 literal-path behavior (astral char, engine wraps via
    ToUint16) -- documented residual. Float and BigInt typed arrays,
    the length form `new Uint8Array(3)`, `.from()`, subclassing, and
    non-literal elements all fail closed (documented residuals).
    The _JS_EXPR_MAX_CODEPOINTS cap counts decoded codepoints. Never
    evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    k = _js_match_kw(text, i, "new")
    if k is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("typed array needs new")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    conv = None
    for _name, _fn in _F643_TYPED_ARRAYS:
        _kk = _js_match_kw(text, k, _name)
        if _kk is not None:
            conv = _fn
            k = _kk
            break
    if conv is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not a modeled typed array")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k >= n or text[k] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("typed array without paren")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if k >= n or text[k] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("typed array needs array literal")
    k += 1
    out = []
    total = 0
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k < n and text[k] == "]":
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if k < n and text[k] == ")":
            return "", k + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("typed array unclosed")
    while True:
        val, k = _js_parse_fcc_number(text, k)
        ch = _safe_chr(conv(val))
        if ch is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("bad typed-array codepoint")
        out.append(ch)
        total += 1
        if total > _JS_EXPR_MAX_CODEPOINTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many codepoints")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k < n and text[k] == ",":
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            continue
        if k < n and text[k] == "]":
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            if k < n and text[k] == ")":
                return "".join(out), k + 1
            raise _JSExprFail("typed array unclosed")
        raise _JSExprFail("bad typed-array args")


def _js_parse_fromcharcode_args_inner(text, i, closer, allow_spread=False,
                                       state=None):
    """Parse `n (, n)* <closer>` -- the F458 fromCharCode codepoint-list
    grammar (F458 / F480 / F486); returns (value, idx).

    The *closer* is ')' for the direct call form and ']' for the
    .apply([...]) dispatch form (F486). The F458 integer-literal
    grammar is unchanged. When *allow_spread* (F500, direct call form
    only), an argument may also be a spread array-literal operand
    `...[n, n, ...]`, so mixed literal+spread lists
    (`String.fromCharCode(120, ...[111])`) decode engine-exactly;
    F643: a spread typed-array operand `...new Uint8Array([n, ...])`
    (integer typed arrays) decodes the same way. The .call/.apply
    dispatch forms keep allow_spread=False (spread fails closed
    there, documented F500 boundary). The
    _JS_EXPR_MAX_CODEPOINTS cap counts decoded codepoints. Never evals.
    """
    out = []
    total = 0
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    if i < len(text) and text[i] == closer:
        return "", i + 1
    while True:
        if allow_spread and text.startswith("...", i):
            _sj = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 3)
            if _js_match_kw(text, _sj, "new") is not None:
                # F643: ...new Uint8Array([...]) -- the `new` keyword
                # commits the typed-array alternative; anything else
                # (including `...newX` idents) falls through to the
                # array-literal spread parser and fails closed there.
                val, i = _js_parse_f643_typed_array(text, _sj)
            else:
                val, i = _js_parse_fromcharcode_spread_inner(text, i + 3)
            out.append(val)
            total += len(val)
        else:
            try:
                val, i = _js_parse_fcc_number(text, i)  # F458: engine spellings
            except _JSExprFail:
                # F742: `v.getUint8(k)` byte reads against a
                # var-bound DataView (env in state, set only by
                # _js_parse_f742_dataview_iife). Without the env
                # this raises exactly as the F458 grammar did
                # before, so no previously-decoded input changes
                # behavior.
                ch, i = _js_parse_f742_getuint8_arg(text, i, state)
                out.append(ch)
                total += 1
            else:
                ch = _safe_chr(val)
                if ch is None:
                    raise _JSExprFail("bad codepoint")
                out.append(ch)
                total += 1
        if total > _JS_EXPR_MAX_CODEPOINTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many codepoints")
        i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
        if i < len(text) and text[i] == ",":
            i = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
            continue
        if i < len(text) and text[i] == closer:
            return "".join(out), i + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("bad fromCharCode args")


def _js_parse_fromcharcode_args(text, j, state=None):
    """Parse the '(n, ...)' argument list of a String.fromCharCode-shaped
    call (F458 / F480); returns (value, idx).

    Shared by the dot form (`String.fromCharCode(...)`) and the
    bracket-string member form (`String['fromCharCode'](...)`, F480 --
    node-identical). The F458 integer-literal grammar is unchanged.
    F500: arguments may be literal codepoints or spread array-literal
    operands (`...[n, n, ...]`), in any mixture; F643: a spread operand
    may also be `...new <IntTypedArray>([n, n, ...])`. The .call/.apply
    dispatch forms keep the literal-only grammar (spread fails closed
    there, documented F500 boundary; F643 admits a typed-array
    .apply() argsArray via the dispatch parser, not here).
    """
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if i >= len(text) or text[i] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("fromCharCode without paren")
    return _js_parse_fromcharcode_args_inner(text, i + 1, ")",
                                             allow_spread=True,
                                             state=state)


def _js_parse_dispatch_thisarg(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse a Function.prototype dispatch thisArg at text[i] (F486).

    The thisArg is parsed -- it must parse, else the dispatch fails
    closed -- but its VALUE is ignored: Array.of and
    String.fromCharCode ignore `this` per spec, node-verified
    (v24.20.0): Array.of.call([], ...), Array.of.call(null, ...),
    Array.of.call('x', ...) and String.fromCharCode.apply(null, ...)
    are all engine-identical to the direct form. The parse accepts the
    built-in constructor reference ([].constructor / Array, plus the
    F497 non-empty array-literal receiver ['lit',...].constructor --
    engine-identical to Array, value discarded anyway), the
    null/undefined/true/false keywords, or any additive value; anything
    else fails closed. Returns (None, next_index) -- the value is
    intentionally discarded. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    try:
        k = _js_parse_array_ctor_ref(text, j)
        return None, k
    except _JSExprFail:
        pass
    if _js_is_nonempty_ctor_recv(text, j):
        # F497: ['lit',...].constructor -- the receiver elements are
        # string literals only (validated, part-budget charged); the
        # reference is engine-identical to Array.
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        _, k = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, k, state, "]")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k < n and text[k] == ".":
            k2 = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            if text.startswith("constructor", k2):
                k3 = k2 + len("constructor")
                if k3 >= n or text[k3] not in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                    return None, k3
    marker, k = _js_parse_marker_kw(text, j)
    if marker is not None:
        return None, k
    _, k = _js_parse_additive(text, j, depth, state)
    return None, k


def _js_parse_of_dispatch(text, paren, dname, depth, state):
    """Parse `FACTORY.call(thisArg, args...)` / `FACTORY.apply(thisArg,
    [args...])` with text[paren] == '(' (F486); returns (_JSArr,
    next_index).

    Desugars static-factory dispatch to the direct factory path:
    node-evaluated (v24.20.0),
      Array.of.call([], 'a','b') === Array.of.apply([], ['a','b'])
        === [].constructor.of.call([].constructor, 'a','b')
        === Array.of('a','b')
    so the argument list reuses the F446 of-factory grammar
    (_js_parse_of_args_inner): string literals, nested array literals,
    and '...'-spreads of array literals (F484 classes), exactly as the
    direct form; numeric, marker, or additive args fail closed -- the
    SAME modeled/fail-closed classes as the direct form, so the desugar
    is faithful (no F481-class composition gap). The thisArg is parsed but ignored
    (_js_parse_dispatch_thisarg). `.bind` on a factory is not modeled
    (the bound factory is not a decodable value) -- the caller fails
    it closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, paren + 1)
    _, i = _js_parse_dispatch_thisarg(text, i, depth, state)
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    if i >= n or text[i] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("factory dispatch without thisArg comma")
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    if dname == "apply":
        if i >= n or text[i] != "[":
            raise _JSExprFail("factory apply argsArray not an array")
        elements, i = _js_parse_of_args_inner(text, i + 1, state, "]")
        i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
        if i >= n or text[i] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("factory apply with extra args")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the dispatch call is obfuscation-shaped
        return elements, i + 1
    # dname == "call": inline args, ')'-terminated (of-factory grammar)
    elements, i = _js_parse_of_args_inner(text, i, state, ")")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the dispatch call is obfuscation-shaped
    return elements, i


def _js_parse_fcc_dispatch(text, paren, dname, depth, state):
    """Parse `String.fromCharCode.call(thisArg, codes...)` /
    `String.fromCharCode.apply(thisArg, [codes...])` with text[paren] ==
    '(' (F486); returns (value, next_index).

    Desugars to the direct fromCharCode path: node-evaluated
    (v24.20.0),
      String.fromCharCode.apply(null, [120,111])
        === String.fromCharCode.call(null, 120, 111)
        === String.fromCharCode(120, 111)
    so the codepoint list reuses the F458 grammar
    (_js_parse_fromcharcode_args_inner): the same integer-literal
    spellings, the same codepoint bounds, the same fail-closed
    classes -- faithful by construction. F643: the .apply() argsArray
    may also be `new <IntTypedArray>([n, n, ...])`, decoded with the
    engine's typed-element semantics. The thisArg is parsed but
    ignored (_js_parse_dispatch_thisarg). `.bind` is not modeled.
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, paren + 1)
    _, i = _js_parse_dispatch_thisarg(text, i, depth, state)
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    if i >= n or text[i] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("fromCharCode dispatch without thisArg comma")
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    if dname == "apply":
        if _js_match_kw(text, i, "new") is not None:
            # F643: .apply(null, new Uint8Array([...]))
            val, i = _js_parse_f643_typed_array(text, i)
        else:
            if i >= n or text[i] != "[":
                raise _JSExprFail("fromCharCode apply argsArray not array")
            val, i = _js_parse_fromcharcode_args_inner(text, i + 1, "]",
                                                       state=state)
        i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
        if i >= n or text[i] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("fromCharCode apply with extra args")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the dispatch call is obfuscation-shaped
        return val, i + 1
    # dname == "call": inline codepoints, ')'-terminated (F458 grammar)
    val, i = _js_parse_fromcharcode_args_inner(text, i, ")", state=state)
    state["ops"] += 1  # the dispatch call is obfuscation-shaped
    return val, i


class _JSArr:
    """Internal: a parsed JS array-literal value.

    F434: array values only survive when immediately consumed by .join();
    a bare (unjoined) array is not obfuscation-shaped and decodes to
    nothing -- _try_js_expr_decode returns None for _JSArr values.
    Elements are str, _JSNumStr (F475 numeric provenance), _JSNil
    (null/undefined, F479), _JSBool (booleans, F479), or nested _JSArr
    (single-level array literals, F479). Mixed-type arrays are
    detectable at predicate-apply time via _js_has_nonstr_element.
    """

    __slots__ = ("elements",)

    def __init__(self, elements):
        self.elements = elements


class _JSNumStr(str):
    """Internal: a str parsed from a JS number literal (F475).

    The decoder's expression grammar is string-typed, so a numeric
    primary is coerced with str(num) at parse -- but the element was
    NOT a string in the engine, and member access on it behaves
    differently there (1..length is undefined; (1).match throws).
    _JSNumStr marks that provenance: it behaves exactly like str
    everywhere (join, len, ord, comparisons) except where a consumer
    explicitly checks for it to fail closed on mixed-type arrays.
    """


class _JSBigInt(str):
    """Internal: a str parsed from a JS BigInt literal (F633).

    Carries the node-exact decimal repr of the BigInt value: Python
    ints are arbitrary precision, so int(digits) is exact within the
    _JS_EXPR_MAX_INT_DIGITS bound. Leading-zero literals (007n) are
    a SyntaxError in the engine and fail closed at parse, never
    reaching this class. Behaves exactly like str everywhere (join,
    len, ord, comparisons) except where a consumer explicitly
    checks for it: the additive grammar does numeric addition when
    BOTH operands are _JSBigInt (1n+2n === 3n in the engine,
    v24.20.0) and string concatenation when exactly one side is a
    _JSBigInt and the other is a plain string ('a'+1n === 'a1'); a
    BigInt mixed with a number literal throws in the engine and
    fails closed here. Non-decimal BigInt literals (0x10n, 0b11n,
    0o17n) fail closed (documented residual); multiplicative BigInt
    arithmetic is unmodeled and fails closed (documented residual).
    """


class _JSThunks:
    """Internal: an array literal of zero-arg arrow thunks returning
    string literals (F620).

    The thunk array is opaque at every position except the three
    F620 invocation patterns (expression-level `.map(f=>f())`,
    statement-IIFE indexed `f[INT]()` calls, statement-IIFE
    `.forEach(f=>{ACC+=f();})` accumulation): the decode happens only
    at the invocation point, never at the array value -- in the
    engine `[()=>'a'].join('')` stringifies the functions themselves
    ("()=> 'a'"), so modeling values at the array position would
    mis-decode. Not a _JSArr: it never reaches value positions (the
    additive and every other postfix branch fail closed on it).
    """

    __slots__ = ("values",)

    def __init__(self, values):
        self.values = values


class _JSNil:
    """Internal: a parsed null/undefined array element (F479).

    Not a str subclass: the engine coerces null/undefined to "" inside
    join()/toString() (via _js_join_element), but member access on them
    throws -- so the type-dependent filter predicates fail closed on
    arrays holding _JSNil, and the truthiness predicate drops it.
    """

    __slots__ = ()

    def __repr__(self):
        return "_JSNil()"


class _JSBool:
    """Internal: a parsed boolean array element (F479).

    Not a str subclass: the engine coerces booleans to "true"/"false"
    inside join()/toString() (via _js_join_element), but (true).length
    is undefined and (true).match throws -- so the type-dependent
    filter predicates fail closed on arrays holding _JSBool, and the
    truthiness predicate keeps "true" / drops "false".
    """

    __slots__ = ("value",)

    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value  # "true" or "false"

    def __repr__(self):
        return "_JSBool(%r)" % (self.value,)


class _JSMethodRef:
    """Internal: a bound-method reference (F490).

    Created only for provably-non-null bases: `[]?.concat` /
    `'s'?.concat` / `?['concat']` on a parsed _JSArr/plain-str value
    (the method is validated against the (base-kind, method) table at
    creation, so the reference is always the real built-in -- never
    undefined), and the `[].constructor?.of` / `[].constructor.of`
    factory reference. The base is discarded (the engine uses only
    the function value when it is later invoked).

    A bare reference is not a decodable value: it must be dispatched
    via .call/.apply/.bind (or applied as the `of` factory); anything
    else fails closed upstream -- _ascii_printable_or_none refuses
    non-str values and _js_parse_additive raises on method-ref
    operands, so a reference can never become a decoded variant or
    crash the scanner. Never a str subclass (deliberately).
    """

    __slots__ = ("kind", "method")

    def __init__(self, kind, method):
        self.kind = kind  # 'array' | 'string' | 'factory'
        self.method = method

    def __repr__(self):
        return "_JSMethodRef(%r, %r)" % (self.kind, self.method)


def _js_join_element(e):
    """Engine-exact String() coercion of one array element for join (F479).

    null/undefined -> "" (dropped, engine-exact); booleans ->
    "true"/"false" (node-verified); nested single-level arrays -> their
    comma-joined toString (engine-exact; deeper nesting fails closed at
    parse, so the recursion here is at most one level); strings and
    _JSNumStr -> as-is (the F475 numeric repr rules compose unchanged).
    Never raises on parsed elements. No new regexes; never evals.
    """
    if isinstance(e, _JSNil):
        return ""
    if isinstance(e, _JSBool):
        return e.value
    if isinstance(e, _JSArr):
        return ",".join(_js_join_element(x) for x in e.elements)
    return str(e)


def _js_truthy_keep(e):
    """Engine-exact Boolean() keep-test for one parsed element (F479).

    null/undefined are falsy (dropped); booleans keep their value;
    nested arrays are always truthy (kept); strings and _JSNumStr use
    the F443 empty-string test (the _JSNumStr numeric-falsy edge is
    pre-existing F443 behavior, unchanged here).
    """
    if isinstance(e, _JSNil):
        return False
    if isinstance(e, _JSBool):
        return e.value == "true"
    return e != ""


def _js_has_nonstr_element(elements):
    """True when any element is not a plain engine string (F475/F479).

    _JSNumStr (numeric provenance), _JSNil (null/undefined), _JSBool,
    and nested _JSArr elements all fail the type-dependent filter
    predicates closed (node: member access throws, or evaluates to
    undefined which the comparison treats as false). Plain str is the
    only decidable class. Supersedes the bare isinstance(_JSNumStr)
    checks -- behavior-identical on the pre-F479 type universe.
    """
    return any(type(e) is not str for e in elements)


def _js_match_kw(text, i, kw):
    """Match keyword *kw* at text[i] (ident-boundary checked).

    Returns the index just past the keyword, or None. The caller must
    ensure text[i] is at a token start (the check covers the trailing
    boundary only). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if not text.startswith(kw, i):
        return None
    k = i + len(kw)
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        return None
    return k


def _js_parse_new_set(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `new Set([...])` at text[i] (text[i] == 'n') (F494).

    Desugars the Set-construction spread source: node-evaluated,
    [...new Set(['f1','f2'])] is element-identical to ['f1','f2'] with
    SameValueZero dedup (first-occurrence order, node v24.20.0
    verified). Elements must be plain string literals -- numeric,
    marker, or nested-array elements fail closed (honest, documented
    boundary; the filed shapes are string-only). Returns
    (_JSArr, next_index). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_match_kw(text, i, "new")
    if j is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not a new-expression")
    j = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j), "Set")
    if j is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not new Set")
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("Set arg not an array literal")
    arr, j = _js_parse_array(text, j, depth, state)
    seen = set()
    elements = []
    for e in arr.elements:
        if not isinstance(e, str) or isinstance(e, _JSNumStr):
            raise _JSExprFail("Set of non-string literals")
        if e not in seen:
            seen.add(e)
            elements.append(e)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the iterable-spread source is obfuscation-shaped
    return _JSArr(elements), j


def _js_parse_new_map_head(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `new Map([[k,v],...])` at text[i] (F494, factored for F625).

    The shared entry-validation head for the Map spread sources:
    entries are [key, value] pairs with string/numeric literal keys
    (SameValueZero identity, later-wins on duplicate keys) and plain
    string-literal values (node v24.20.0 verified); anything else
    fails closed (the F494 literal-only discipline). Returns
    (pairs, next_index): pairs is the later-wins list of
    (kid, key, value) with decoded literal values, next_index just
    past the closing `)` of the `new Map(...)` head -- the caller
    parses its own member (`.values()` / `.entries()` / `.keys()`).
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_match_kw(text, i, "new")
    if j is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not a new-expression")
    j = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j), "Map")
    if j is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not new Map")
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("Map arg not an array literal")
    outer, j = _js_parse_array(text, j, depth, state)
    pairs = []
    index = {}
    for p in outer.elements:
        if not isinstance(p, _JSArr) or len(p.elements) != 2:
            raise _JSExprFail("Map entry not a [k,v] pair")
        k, v = p.elements
        if isinstance(k, _JSNumStr):
            kid = ("n", str(k))
        elif isinstance(k, str):
            kid = ("s", k)
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("Map key not a literal")
        if not isinstance(v, str) or isinstance(v, _JSNumStr):
            raise _JSExprFail("Map value not a string literal")
        if kid in index:
            pairs[index[kid]] = (kid, k, v)
        else:
            index[kid] = len(pairs)
            pairs.append((kid, k, v))
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    return pairs, j


def _js_parse_new_map_values(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `new Map([[k,v],...]).values()` at text[i] (F494).

    Desugars the Map-values spread source: node-evaluated,
    [...new Map([[0,'f1'],[1,'f2']]).values()] is ['f1','f2'] --
    values in insertion order with later-wins on duplicate keys
    (node v24.20.0 verified). Entry validation is the shared
    `_js_parse_new_map_head` (F494/F625); anything non-literal fails
    closed. Returns (_JSArr, next_index). Never evals.
    """
    pairs, j = _js_parse_new_map_head(text, i, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= len(text) or text[j] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("expected .values()")
    j = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1), "values")
    if j is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not .values()")
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the iterable-spread source is obfuscation-shaped
    return _JSArr([v for _, _, v in pairs]), j


def _js_parse_f695_map_entries_spread(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `new Map([[k,v],...])` as a pair-array source (F695).

    Desugars the bare-Map iteration protocol node-exactly
    (v24.20.0): the engine iterates a Map's entries, so
    `[...new Map([['k','f1']])]` is `[['k','f1']]` and
    `Array.from(new Map([['k','f1']]))` is the same -- each entry
    becomes a 2-element _JSArr [k, v]. Entry validation is the
    shared `_js_parse_new_map_head` (F494/F625): insertion order
    with later-wins on duplicate keys (node-exact SameValueZero --
    1 and '1' are distinct keys; the shared head parser does NOT
    collapse -0/0 -- a documented pre-existing residual, always
    fail-closed); keys keep their _JSNumStr provenance (the F600
    discipline), values are string literals. A following `.`, `(`,
    or `[` fails closed: `.entries()` / `.keys()` / `.values()`
    (dot or bracket-string member form, e.g.
    `new Map(...)['entries']()`) keep their own F589/F600/F494
    routes via the primary path.
    The empty-Map form fails closed here (the shared head parser
    rejects `new Map()` without an array argument -- a documented
    residual; the engine yields []). Anything non-literal fails
    closed. Returns (_JSArr of _JSArr pairs, next_index). Never
    evals.
    """
    pairs, j = _js_parse_new_map_head(text, i, depth, state)
    _nj = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if _nj < len(text) and text[_nj] in ".([":
        # Not a bare Map: `.entries()` / `.keys()` / `.values()`
        # (dot form or bracket-string member form, e.g.
        # `new Map(...)['entries']()`) have their own routes
        # (F589/F600/F494) via the primary path -- the F695
        # pair-array desugar applies only to the bare-Map iteration
        # protocol.
        raise _JSExprFail("F695 Map has a postfix member")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the iterable-spread source is obfuscation-shaped
    return _JSArr([_JSArr([k, v]) for _, k, v in pairs]), j


def _js_parse_f712_iter_source(text, j, maps):
    """Parse a var-bound Map iteration source at text[j] (F712).

    The source is `m`, `m.entries()`, `m.keys()`, or `m.values()`
    where *m* is bound in *maps* (name -> pairs list of (kid, k, v)
    from `_js_parse_new_map_head`). The bare form iterates the
    engine's Map iteration protocol, which IS the entries protocol
    (node v24.20.0 verified: `[...m]` is `[[k,v],...]`). Returns
    (items, next_index): for `entries` a list of 2-element _JSArr
    [k, v] (keys keep their _JSNumStr provenance, the F600/F695
    discipline); for `keys` the key list; for `values` the value
    list. Bracket-string member forms (`m["entries"]()`) fail closed
    (documented residual -- the filed shapes use dot form only).
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    name, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
    if name not in maps:
        raise _JSExprFail("F712 iter of unbound map")
    pairs = maps[name]
    q = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    kind = "entries"
    if q < n and text[q] == ".":
        q2 = _js_skip_ws(text, q + 1)
        for meth in ("entries", "keys", "values"):
            mk = _js_match_kw(text, q2, meth)
            if mk is not None:
                q3 = _js_expect(text, mk, "(")
                q3 = _js_expect(text, q3, ")")
                kind, j = meth, q3
                break
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("F712 bad iter member")
    if kind == "entries":
        items = [_JSArr([k, v]) for _, k, v in pairs]
    elif kind == "keys":
        items = [k for _, k, _ in pairs]
    else:
        items = [v for _, _, v in pairs]
    return items, j


def _js_parse_f712_iter_index(text, j, items):
    """Apply `[I]` (and, for pair items, `[J]`) to *items* at text[j]
    (F712).

    Indices are canonical decimal literals (the F535/F565
    discipline -- `042`/`0x10`/`1e3` fail closed, never
    mis-decoded). Negative indices fail closed: node treats
    `arr[-1]` as a property miss (undefined), never the last
    element -- Python's negative indexing would mis-decode there.
    Out-of-range indices fail closed: node throws TypeError there
    (`[...m.entries()][5][1]` is undefined[1]), so no value is
    ever produced. For pair items (`entries`), `[I]` alone yields
    the pair _JSArr (the caller stringifies node-exactly via
    Array.prototype.toString); `[I][J]` requires J < 2. For scalar
    items (`keys`/`values`) a second index fails closed
    (documented bound -- node would do string char-indexing,
    outside the string-only discipline). Returns (value,
    next_index). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "[")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    ilit, j = _js_parse_canonical_int(text, j, "F712")
    idx = int(ilit)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "]")
    if idx < 0 or idx >= len(items):
        raise _JSExprFail("F712 index out of range")
    item = items[idx]
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        if not isinstance(item, _JSArr):
            raise _JSExprFail("F712 scalar reindex")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        jlit, j = _js_parse_canonical_int(text, j, "F712")
        jdx = int(jlit)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, "]")
        if jdx < 0 or jdx >= len(item.elements):
            raise _JSExprFail("F712 pair index out of range")
        item = item.elements[jdx]
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    return item, j


def _js_parse_f712_operand(text, j, maps, state):
    """Parse one F712 return-additive operand at text[j].

    An operand is a string literal, a var-bound Map iteration
    spread read (`[...m]` / `[...m.entries()]` / `[...m.keys()]` /
    `[...m.values()]` indexed), or the `Array.from` twin
    (`Array.from(m)` / `Array.from(m.entries())` /
    `Array.from(m.keys())` / `Array.from(m.values())` indexed).
    The spread/`Array.from` of a var-bound Map desugars to the
    engine's Map iteration protocol (node v24.20.0). A pair read
    without a second index (`[...m.entries()][0]`) yields the pair
    array, stringified node-exactly here via Array.prototype.toString
    (comma-join) so the additive loop's string assumption holds --
    without this the loop's `len()` would raise TypeError on the
    _JSArr (a scanner crash, worse than a miss). Each iteration
    read charges one op (obfuscation-shaped). Returns (value,
    next_index). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] in "'\"":
        val, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
        return val, j
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        j = _js_expect(text, j, "[")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        for _ in range(3):
            j = _js_expect(text, j, ".")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        items, j = _js_parse_f712_iter_source(text, j, maps)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, "]")
        val, j = _js_parse_f712_iter_index(text, j, items)
        state["ops"] += 1  # the iteration read is obfuscation-shaped
        return _js_parse_f712_coerce(val), j
    if text.startswith("Array", j):
        ja = j + len("Array")
        jd = _js_skip_ws(text, ja)
        if jd >= n or text[jd] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("F712 Array without dot")
        kf = _js_skip_ws(text, jd + 1)
        if not text.startswith("from", kf) or \
                text.startswith("fromAsync", kf):
            raise _JSExprFail("F712 Array not from")
        fe = kf + len("from")
        if fe < n and text[fe] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F712 Array.fromx is not from")
        fe = _js_skip_ws(text, fe)
        fe = _js_expect(text, fe, "(")
        items, fe = _js_parse_f712_iter_source(text, fe, maps)
        fe = _js_skip_ws(text, fe)
        fe = _js_expect(text, fe, ")")
        val, fe = _js_parse_f712_iter_index(text, fe, items)
        state["ops"] += 1  # the iteration read is obfuscation-shaped
        return _js_parse_f712_coerce(val), fe
    raise _JSExprFail("F712 bad operand")


def _js_parse_f712_coerce(val):
    """Stringify an F712 operand value node-exactly (F712).

    Pair reads without a second index (`[...m.entries()][0]`)
    yield the pair _JSArr; in the decoder's string-only discipline
    the array is represented by its Array.prototype.toString form
    (elements comma-joined, node v24.20.0). Elements are flat
    strings (keys keep _JSNumStr provenance, which stringifies
    canonically). Plain strings pass through unchanged. Never
    evals.
    """
    if isinstance(val, _JSArr):
        return ",".join(str(e) for e in val.elements)
    return val


def _js_f735f736_bound_operand(text, i, env):
    """Parse one F735/F736 additive operand at text[i]: a string
    literal or a bound identifier. Returns (str, next_index). An
    unbound identifier fails closed (the engine throws
    ReferenceError -- the decoder never decodes a throw); `this`
    and non-string shapes fail closed. Never evals.
    """
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    n = len(text)
    if j >= n:
        raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 empty operand")
    c = text[j]
    if c in "'\"":
        return _js_parse_string(text, j)
    if c in _JS_IDENT_START:
        name, k = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        if name not in env:
            raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 unbound ident")
        return env[name], k
    raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 bad operand")


def _js_f735f736_bound_additive(text, i, env, depth, state):
    """Parse `LIT|BOUND (+ LIT|BOUND)*` at text[i] (F735/F736).

    Both operands are plain strings by construction, so `+` is
    string concatenation (node-exact, v24.20.0). Returns (value,
    next_index). Never evals.
    """
    if depth >= _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH:
        raise _JSExprFail("too deep")
    n = len(text)
    val, j = _js_f735f736_bound_operand(text, i, env)
    while True:
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if k >= n or text[k] != "+":
            return val, j
        rhs, j = _js_f735f736_bound_operand(text, k + 1, env)
        if len(val) + len(rhs) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
            raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 concat too long")
        val = val + rhs
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")


def _js_f735f736_eval_body(text, bs, be, env, depth, state):
    """Evaluate a nested-function body span `[bs, be)` (F735/F736).

    The span is `return ADD;` with ADD a bound-operand additive
    resolved against *env* -- the call-time environment (the
    IIFE's string bindings with the call's parameter bindings
    shadowing, node-exact closure semantics). Requires the
    terminating `;` and nothing else before *be*. Returns the
    string value. Never evals.
    """
    val, j = _js_f735f736_bound_additive(text, bs, env, depth,
                                         state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ";")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j != be:
        raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 trailing in body")
    return val


def _js_f735f736_parse_nested_fn(text, j, state):
    """Parse `function [NAME]([p='LIT',...]){return ADD;}` at text[j]
    (F735/F736).

    Returns (name_or_None, params, body_start, body_end,
    next_index): params is the [(pname, default_or_None)] list
    (defaults are string literals only; a param without `=` has
    no default), and body_start/body_end span the `return ADD;`
    text -- evaluated lazily at call time so closures read the
    call-time environment (node-exact). The body must be exactly
    one `return` statement; anything else fails closed. Never
    evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    g = _js_match_kw(text, j, "function")
    if g is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 not a function")
    q = _js_skip_ws(text, g)
    name = None
    if q < n and text[q] in _JS_IDENT_START:
        name, q = _js_parse_ident(text, q)
    q = _js_expect(text, q, "(")
    params = []
    q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
    if not (q < n and text[q] == ")"):
        while True:
            q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
            pname, q = _js_parse_ident(text, q)
            if pname == "arguments":
                raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 param named arguments")
            default = None
            q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
            if (q < n and text[q] == "="
                    and not text.startswith(("==", "=>"), q)):
                q = _js_skip_ws(text, q + 1)
                if q >= n or text[q] not in "'\"":
                    raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 default not literal")
                default, q = _js_parse_string(text, q)
                state["parts"] += 1  # the default is obfuscation-shaped
                if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                    raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            if any(p == pname for p, _ in params):
                raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 dup param")
            params.append((pname, default))
            q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
            if q < n and text[q] == ",":
                q = q + 1
                continue
            break
    q = _js_expect(text, q, ")")
    bq = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
    if bq >= n or text[bq] != "{":
        raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 body not braced")
    inner_start, inner_end, after = _js_span_braced(text, bq)
    rk = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, inner_start), "return")
    if rk is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 body not return")
    return name, params, _js_skip_ws(text, rk), inner_end, after


def _js_f735f736_terminal_operand(text, i, strings, funcs, depth,
                                  state):
    """Parse one F735/F736 terminal operand at text[i]: a string
    literal, a bound string identifier, or a call of a bound
    nested function with string-literal args. Returns (str,
    next_index).

    Node-exact call semantics (v24.20.0): explicit args bind
    positionally; a missing arg takes the parameter's default
    (a missing arg with no default is engine `undefined` --
    outside the string-only discipline, fail closed); extra args
    fail closed (conservative); the body evaluates with the
    call-time environment (outer string bindings shadowed by the
    parameter bindings). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    if j >= n:
        raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 empty operand")
    c = text[j]
    if c in "'\"":
        return _js_parse_string(text, j)
    if c in _JS_IDENT_START:
        name, k = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        k2 = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k2 < n and text[k2] == "(":
            if name not in funcs:
                raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 call of unbound")
            params, bs, be = funcs[name]
            args = []
            q = _js_skip_ws(text, k2 + 1)
            if q < n and text[q] != ")":
                while True:
                    q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
                    if q >= n or text[q] not in "'\"":
                        raise _JSExprFail(
                            "F735/F736 call arg not literal")
                    arg, q = _js_parse_string(text, q)
                    args.append(arg)
                    state["parts"] += 1
                    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                        raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
                    q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
                    if q < n and text[q] == ",":
                        q = q + 1
                        continue
                    break
            q = _js_expect(text, q, ")")
            if len(args) > len(params):
                raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 too many args")
            call_env = dict(strings)
            for idx, (pname, default) in enumerate(params):
                if idx < len(args):
                    call_env[pname] = args[idx]
                elif default is not None:
                    call_env[pname] = default
                else:
                    raise _JSExprFail(
                        "F735/F736 missing arg without default")
            val = _js_f735f736_eval_body(text, bs, be, call_env,
                                        depth, state)
            state["ops"] += 1  # the nested call is obfuscation-shaped
            return val, q
        if name not in strings:
            raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 unbound ident")
        return strings[name], k
    raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 bad operand")


def _js_f735f736_terminal_additive(text, i, strings, funcs, depth,
                                   state):
    """Parse the F735/F736 return-terminal additive
    `OP (+ OP)*` at text[i], where OP is a string literal, a
    bound string identifier, or a nested-function call. Returns
    (value, next_index). Never evals.
    """
    if depth >= _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH:
        raise _JSExprFail("too deep")
    n = len(text)
    val, j = _js_f735f736_terminal_operand(text, i, strings, funcs,
                                           depth, state)
    while True:
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if k >= n or text[k] != "+":
            return val, j
        rhs, j = _js_f735f736_terminal_operand(text, k + 1, strings,
                                               funcs, depth, state)
        if len(val) + len(rhs) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
            raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 concat too long")
        val = val + rhs
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")


def _js_parse_f735f736_nested_fn_iife(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `(function(){[STMT;]* return TERM;})()` with
    nested-function fragment sources at text[i] (F735/F736).

    F735 (LOW, attack round 50): nested function closure reads
    bypass the bounded static JS-expression decoder -- the
    decoder's IIFE-factory grammar has no nested-function
    production: `var f='LIT';function g(){return f;}return
    g()+'LIT';` and the `var g=function(){return f;};` twin.
    F736 (LOW, attack round 50): default parameter reads --
    `function h(a='LIT'){return a;}return h()+'LIT';` and
    `function h(a='LIT',b='LIT'){return a+b;}return h('LIT');`.

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): statements are exactly
    - `var|let|const X='LIT'[,'LIT'...];` -- string bindings
      (duplicate `var` rebinds last-wins, duplicate
      `let`/`const` fails closed -- engine-exact);
    - `function G([p='LIT',...]){return ADD;}` -- nested function
      declaration (name required);
    - `var|let|const G=function([p='LIT',...]){return ADD;};` --
      var-bound function expression (anonymous).
    A call `G('LIT'*)` evaluates the body with the call-time
    environment: the IIFE's string bindings shadowed by the
    parameter bindings (explicit args bind positionally, missing
    args take defaults). The terminal is `return TERM;` with
    TERM a `+`-joined additive of string literals, bound string
    idents, and nested-function calls.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - params without defaults called with missing args (engine
      `undefined`), non-literal defaults or call args, rest /
      destructuring params, duplicate param names;
    - nested-function bodies that are not exactly `return ADD;`
      (extra statements, no `;`), bodies reading unbound idents
      (engine ReferenceError), `this` / `arguments` in bodies,
      bodies calling (nested) functions;
    - named function expressions, generator / async functions,
      arrow functions, `new`, method calls, extra call args;
    - function or binding redeclaration (a string binding may
      not shadow a function name and vice versa);
    - forward references (statements process in order);
    - non-`;`-terminated statements, trailing statements after
      the return.
    Tried after F712: every earlier route requires its own
    binding family or statement head and fails closed on a
    `function` declaration/expression statement (red-first
    verified: 2 BREAK each on the open tree), so no
    previously-decoded input changes behavior. Returns (value,
    next_index), or None when text[i] is not this shape. Never
    evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if depth >= _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH:
        return None
    try:
        brace = _js_f655_head(text, i)
        if brace is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 not our head")
        inner_start, inner_end, j = _js_span_braced(text, brace)
        strings = {}
        funcs = {}
        sj = _js_skip_ws(text, inner_start)
        while True:
            sj = _js_skip_ws(text, sj)
            if sj >= inner_end:
                raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 body without return")
            rk = _js_match_kw(text, sj, "return")
            if rk is not None:
                break
            fk = _js_match_kw(text, sj, "function")
            dk, dnext = _js_match_decl_kw(text, sj)
            if fk is not None:
                name, params, bs, be, q = _js_f735f736_parse_nested_fn(
                    text, sj, state)
                if name is None:
                    raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 anonymous nested fn")
                if name in funcs or name in strings:
                    raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 fn redeclared")
                funcs[name] = (params, bs, be)
                sj = q
            elif dk is not None:
                q = _js_skip_ws(text, dnext)
                vname, q = _js_parse_ident(text, q)
                if vname == "arguments":
                    raise _JSExprFail(
                        "F735/F736 binding named arguments")
                q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
                if not (q < n and text[q] == "="
                        and not text.startswith(("==", "=>"), q)):
                    raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 bad decl")
                q = _js_skip_ws(text, q + 1)
                if q < n and text[q] in "'\"":
                    names = [vname]
                    lits = []
                    lit, q = _js_parse_string(text, q)
                    lits.append(lit)
                    while True:
                        qq = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
                        if qq >= n or text[qq] != ",":
                            break
                        qq = _js_skip_ws(text, qq + 1)
                        nm2, qq = _js_parse_ident(text, qq)
                        if nm2 == "arguments":
                            raise _JSExprFail(
                                "F735/F736 binding named arguments")
                        qq = _js_skip_ws(text, qq)
                        if not (qq < n and text[qq] == "="
                                and not text.startswith(("==", "=>"),
                                                        qq)):
                            raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 bad decl")
                        qq = _js_skip_ws(text, qq + 1)
                        if qq >= n or text[qq] not in "'\"":
                            raise _JSExprFail(
                                "F735/F736 decl RHS not literal")
                        lit2, qq = _js_parse_string(text, qq)
                        names.append(nm2)
                        lits.append(lit2)
                        q = qq
                    q = _js_expect(text, q, ";")
                    for nm2, lit2 in zip(names, lits):
                        if nm2 in funcs:
                            raise _JSExprFail(
                                "F735/F736 string shadows fn")
                        if nm2 in strings and dk != "var":
                            raise _JSExprFail(
                                "F735/F736 let/const rebind")
                        strings[nm2] = lit2  # dup var: last wins
                        state["parts"] += 1
                        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
                elif _js_match_kw(text, q, "function") is not None:
                    name2, params, bs, be, q = (
                        _js_f735f736_parse_nested_fn(text, q, state))
                    if name2 is not None:
                        raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 named fn expr")
                    q = _js_expect(text, q, ";")
                    if vname in funcs or vname in strings:
                        raise _JSExprFail(
                            "F735/F736 fn-expr redeclared")
                    funcs[vname] = (params, bs, be)
                else:
                    raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 decl RHS not modeled")
                sj = q
            else:
                raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 bad statement")
        sj = _js_skip_ws(text, rk)
        total, sj = _js_f735f736_terminal_additive(
            text, sj, strings, funcs, depth, state)
        sj = _js_skip_ws(text, sj)
        sj = _js_expect(text, sj, ";")
        sj = _js_skip_ws(text, sj)
        if sj != inner_end:
            raise _JSExprFail("F735/F736 trailing statements")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the iife wrapper is obfuscation-shaped
        return total, j
    except _JSExprFail:
        return None


        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the iife wrapper is obfuscation-shaped
        return total, j
    except _JSExprFail:
        return None


def _js_parse_f746_new_sub_head(text, i, subname, depth, state):
    """Parse `new SUBNAME(...)` at text[i] (F746).

    SUBNAME is a class name previously bound by a `class SUBNAME
    extends Map{}` declaration. The argument is empty (the empty
    map) or the F563 literal-entries array `[[k,v],...]`
    (string/numeric literal keys with SameValueZero identity,
    plain string-literal values, later-wins on duplicates --
    node v24.20.0). Anything else fails closed. Returns
    (entries_dict, next_index). Raises _JSExprFail. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_match_kw(text, i, "new")
    if j is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 not a new-expression")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    name, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
    if name != subname:
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 not the subclass constructor")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    entries = {}
    if j < n and text[j] == ")":
        return entries, j + 1
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 Map arg not an array literal")
    outer, j = _js_parse_array(text, j, depth, state)
    for p in outer.elements:
        if not isinstance(p, _JSArr) or len(p.elements) != 2:
            raise _JSExprFail("F746 Map entry not a [k,v] pair")
        k, v = p.elements
        if isinstance(k, str) and not isinstance(k, _JSNumStr):
            kid = ("s", k)
        elif isinstance(k, _JSNumStr):
            kid = ("n", str(k))
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("F746 Map key not a literal")
        if not isinstance(v, str) or isinstance(v, _JSNumStr):
            raise _JSExprFail("F746 Map value not a string")
        entries[kid] = v  # later wins, node-exact
        state["parts"] += 1  # F452: charge per entry
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    return entries, j


def _js_parse_f746_class_decl(stmts, j):
    """Parse `class NAME extends Map{}` at stmts[j] (F746).

    The subclass declaration: exactly `class`, an identifier, the
    `extends` keyword, the `Map` identifier, and an EMPTY class
    body (whitespace only -- methods, constructors, fields, or
    computed names fail closed; the engine would give them
    semantics the static model cannot see). Returns (name,
    next_index). Raises _JSExprFail. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(stmts)
    k = _js_match_kw(stmts, j, "class")
    if k is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 not a class declaration")
    k = _js_skip_ws(stmts, k)
    name, k = _js_parse_ident(stmts, k)
    if name == "arguments":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 class named arguments")
    k = _js_skip_ws(stmts, k)
    e = _js_match_kw(stmts, k, "extends")
    if e is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 class without extends")
    e = _js_skip_ws(stmts, e)
    base, e = _js_parse_ident(stmts, e)
    if base != "Map":
        # Only Map subclasses are modeled (a Set/WeakMap/Array
        # subclass has different iteration semantics).
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 superclass not Map")
    e = _js_skip_ws(stmts, e)
    if e >= n or stmts[e] != "{":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 class without body")
    e = _js_skip_ws(stmts, e + 1)
    if e >= n or stmts[e] != "}":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 non-empty class body")
    return name, e + 1


def _js_parse_f746_values_spread_join(stmts, j, mname, entries,
                                     depth, state):
    """Parse `[...m.values()].join('SEP')` at stmts[j] (F746).

    The values-spread read: `[...m.values()]` desugars to the
    entry values in insertion order (node v24.20.0); `.join(SEP)`
    joins with the string-literal separator (node-exact
    Array.prototype.join). A missing separator (engine joins
    with ",") fails closed -- outside the modeled grammar.
    Returns (str, next_index). Raises _JSExprFail. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(stmts)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, "[")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    for _ in range(3):
        j = _js_expect(stmts, j, ".")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    rname, j = _js_parse_ident(stmts, j)
    if rname != mname:
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 spread of another ident")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, ".")
    mn, j = _js_parse_ident(stmts, _js_skip_ws(stmts, j))
    if mn != "values":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 spread not values()")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, ")")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, "]")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, ".")
    jn, j = _js_parse_ident(stmts, _js_skip_ws(stmts, j))
    if jn != "join":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 spread not joined")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    if j >= n or stmts[j] not in "'\"":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 join separator not a literal")
    sep, j = _js_parse_string(stmts, j)
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, ")")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the iteration read is obfuscation-shaped
    return sep.join(entries.values()), j


def _js_parse_f746_forof_accum(stmts, j, mname):
    """Parse `for(var|let v of m.values())` head at stmts[j] (F746).

    Returns (loop_var_name, next_index, braced): next_index is
    just past the `)` (or past `{` when the body is braced --
    the caller parses the `s+=v;` body and the closing `}`).
    Raises _JSExprFail. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(stmts)
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    k = _js_match_kw(stmts, j, "for")
    if k is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 not a for-of")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, k)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    dkw, j = _js_match_decl_kw(stmts, j)
    if dkw is None or dkw == "const":
        # `const` is engine-legal for-of, but the modeled body
        # never reassigns the loop var; keep the decl family
        # tight (var/let) -- a const loop var is a documented
        # residual.
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 for-of decl not var/let")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    vname, j = _js_parse_ident(stmts, j)
    if vname == "arguments":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 loop var named arguments")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    o = _js_match_kw(stmts, j, "of")
    if o is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 for-of without of")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, o)
    rname, j = _js_parse_ident(stmts, j)
    if rname != mname:
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 for-of over another ident")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, ".")
    mn, j = _js_parse_ident(stmts, _js_skip_ws(stmts, j))
    if mn != "values":
        raise _JSExprFail("F746 for-of not values()")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, ")")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    j = _js_expect(stmts, j, ")")
    j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j)
    braced = False
    if j < n and stmts[j] == "{":
        braced = True
        j = _js_skip_ws(stmts, j + 1)
    return vname, j, braced


def _js_parse_f746_subclass_map_iife(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `(function(){class M extends Map{}DECL;* return ADD;})()` (F746).

    F746 (LOW): the decoder models `new Map()` / `new Set()`
    literal construction (F494/F669, CLOSED) but not subclassed
    builtins -- `class M extends Map{}` with `new M()` /
    `new M([[k,v],...])` spreads node-exactly while the decoder
    produces zero variants.

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): the class declaration binds
    M as a Map subclass (empty body only); `var|let|const m =
    new M(ARGS?)` constructs the entries map with the F563
    discipline (string/numeric literal keys, SameValueZero
    identity, later-wins); `m.set('K','V');` statements mutate it
    engine-exactly (the F708 discipline); the optional
    accumulator `var|let s='LIT'; for(var|let v of
    m.values())s+=v;` concatenates values in insertion order;
    the return is `[...m.values()].join('SEP')` or the bound
    accumulator, with an additive string-literal tail.

    Disjointness: tried after F712. Every earlier route requires
    its own binding family or statement head and fails closed on
    a leading `class` declaration (red-first verified: 2 BREAK
    on the open tree), so no previously-decoded input changes
    behavior.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded): a
    non-empty class body, a non-Map superclass, `new M` with
    non-literal entries, `.set` with non-string keys/values or on
    another receiver, `.values()` on another ident, a missing
    `.join` separator, a non-`values()` iteration source, loop-var
    shadowing, accumulator rebinding, extra statements, call
    args on the IIFE. Set subclasses are a documented residual.
    Returns (value, next_index), or None when the head does not
    match or a guard fails. Never evals.
    """
    j = _js_parse_f6xx_iife_head(text, i)
    if j is None:
        return None
    try:
        brace = j
        inner_start, inner_end, j = _js_span_braced(text, brace)
        stmts = text[inner_start:inner_end]
        n = len(stmts)
        sj = _js_skip_ws(stmts, 0)
        # `class M extends Map{}`
        subname, sj = _js_parse_f746_class_decl(stmts, sj)
        sj = _js_skip_ws(stmts, sj)
        # `var|let|const m = new M(ARGS?);`
        dkw, q = _js_match_decl_kw(stmts, sj)
        if dkw is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("F746 map decl not var/let/const")
        q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q)
        mname, q = _js_parse_ident(stmts, q)
        if mname in (subname, "arguments"):
            raise _JSExprFail("F746 map shadows subclass")
        q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q)
        q = _js_expect(stmts, q, "=")
        q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q)
        entries, q = _js_parse_f746_new_sub_head(
            stmts, q, subname, depth, state)
        entries = dict(entries)
        q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q)
        q = _js_expect(stmts, q, ";")
        state["parts"] += 1  # the construction is shaped
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        sj = q
        acc = None
        # `(m.set('K','V');)*`
        while True:
            q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, sj)
            if _js_match_kw(stmts, q, "return") is not None:
                break
            # Peek: `m.set(` head, else the accumulator/for-of or
            # the return.
            try:
                sname, q2 = _js_parse_ident(stmts, q)
            except _JSExprFail:
                break
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            if not (sname == mname and q2 < n
                    and stmts[q2] == "."):
                break
            _mn, q2 = _js_parse_ident(
                stmts, _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2 + 1))
            if _mn != "set":
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 map member not set")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_expect(stmts, q2, "(")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            if q2 >= n or stmts[q2] not in "'\"":
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 set key not a string")
            skey, q2 = _js_parse_string(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_expect(stmts, q2, ",")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            if q2 >= n or stmts[q2] not in "'\"":
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 set value not a string")
            sval, q2 = _js_parse_string(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_expect(stmts, q2, ")")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_expect(stmts, q2, ";")
            entries[("s", skey)] = sval  # later wins, node-exact
            state["parts"] += 1  # the mutation is shaped
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            sj = q2
        # Optional `var|let s='LIT'; for(var|let v of
        # m.values())s+=v;` accumulator.
        q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, sj)
        rk = _js_match_kw(stmts, q, "return")
        if rk is None:
            # Try the accumulator decl.
            dkw2, q2 = _js_match_decl_kw(stmts, q)
            if dkw2 is None or dkw2 == "const":
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 bad middle statement")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            aname, q2 = _js_parse_ident(stmts, q2)
            if aname in (mname, subname, "arguments"):
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 accumulator shadows")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_expect(stmts, q2, "=")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            if q2 >= n or stmts[q2] not in "'\"":
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 accumulator not a string")
            aval, q2 = _js_parse_string(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_expect(stmts, q2, ";")
            vname, q2, braced = _js_parse_f746_forof_accum(
                stmts, q2, mname)
            if vname in (aname, mname, subname):
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 loop var shadows")
            # Body: `s+=v;` / `s=s+v;`, optionally braced.
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            bname, q2 = _js_parse_ident(stmts, q2)
            if bname != aname:
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 accum body not s+=v")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            if q2 + 1 < n and stmts[q2] == "+" \
                    and stmts[q2 + 1] == "=":
                q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2 + 2)
            elif q2 < n and stmts[q2] == "=":
                q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2 + 1)
                bname2, q2 = _js_parse_ident(stmts, q2)
                if bname2 != aname:
                    raise _JSExprFail("F746 accum body not s=s+v")
                q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
                if q2 >= n or stmts[q2] != "+":
                    raise _JSExprFail("F746 accum body not s=s+v")
                q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2 + 1)
            else:
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 accum body not s+=v")
            vname2, q2 = _js_parse_ident(stmts, q2)
            if vname2 != vname:
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 accum body wrong var")
            q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
            q2 = _js_expect(stmts, q2, ";")
            if braced:
                q2 = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q2)
                q2 = _js_expect(stmts, q2, "}")
            for _v in entries.values():
                if len(aval) + len(_v) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
                    raise _JSExprFail("F746 accum too long")
                aval = aval + _v
                state["parts"] += 1  # F452: charge per iteration
                if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                    raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            state["ops"] += 1  # the for-of is obfuscation-shaped
            acc = (aname, aval)
            sj = q2
        # `return TERM (+ 'LIT')*;`
        q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, sj)
        rk = _js_match_kw(stmts, q, "return")
        if rk is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("F746 body not return")
        q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, rk)
        if q < n and stmts[q] == "[":
            val, q = _js_parse_f746_values_spread_join(
                stmts, q, mname, entries, depth, state)
        else:
            tname, q = _js_parse_ident(stmts, q)
            if acc is None or tname != acc[0]:
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 return not the accum")
            val = acc[1]
            q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q)
        while True:
            t = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q)
            if t >= n or stmts[t] != "+":
                break
            t = _js_skip_ws(stmts, t + 1)
            if t >= n or stmts[t] not in "'\"":
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 tail not a literal")
            _tail, t = _js_parse_string(stmts, t)
            if len(val) + len(_tail) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
                raise _JSExprFail("F746 concat too long")
            val = val + _tail
            state["parts"] += 1
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            state["ops"] += 1
            q = t
        q = _js_skip_ws(stmts, q)
        q = _js_expect(stmts, q, ";")
        if _js_skip_ws(stmts, q) != n:
            raise _JSExprFail("F746 trailing statements")
        if depth >= _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH:
            raise _JSExprFail("too deep")
        return _js_parse_f6xx_iife_tail(text, j, depth, state, val)
    except _JSExprFail:
        return None


def _js_parse_f712_map_iter_iife(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `(function(){[DECL M=new Map([[...]]);]* return ADD;})()`
    with var-bound Map iteration reads at text[i] (F712).

    F712 (LOW, wave-381 lane 2 residual): a VAR-BOUND literal Map's
    iteration reads bypass the decoder -- F695 (CLOSED) models only
    the INLINE bare-Map iteration protocol, and F589/F600 (CLOSED)
    model only inline `.entries()`/`.keys()` iteration.

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): each `var|let|const M=new
    Map([[k,v],...]);` binds M to the later-wins (kid, k, v) pair
    list via the shared `_js_parse_new_map_head` (F494/F625 --
    string/numeric literal keys, plain string-literal values;
    anything else fails closed). The return is a `+`-joined
    additive of string literals and var-bound iteration reads:
    `[...m]` / `[...m.entries()]` (pair arrays), `[...m.keys()]`,
    `[...m.values()]`, `Array.from(m)` / `Array.from(m.entries())`
    / `Array.from(m.keys())` / `Array.from(m.values())`, each
    indexed (`[I]` / `[I][J]`, canonical decimal, bounds-checked --
    out-of-range fails closed exactly where node throws TypeError).
    Keys keep their _JSNumStr provenance (the F600/F695
    discipline), so numeric-key reads concat node-exactly.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    non-Map declarations, `m.set(...)` tails (F708 owns those),
    bracket-string member forms (`m["entries"]()`), scalar
    re-indexing (`[...m.keys()][0][1]` -- node does string
    char-indexing there, outside the string-only discipline),
    non-canonical indices, redeclaration, forward references,
    trailing statements, ASI forms. Tried after F692: every
    earlier route requires its own binding family or statement
    head and fails closed on a var-bound-`new Map` initializer
    (red-first verified: 5 BREAK on the open tree), so no
    previously-decoded input changes behavior. Returns (value,
    next_index), or None when text[i] is not this shape. Never
    evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if depth >= _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH:
        return None
    try:
        brace = _js_f655_head(text, i)
        if brace is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("F712 not our head")
        inner_start, inner_end, j = _js_span_braced(text, brace)
        maps = {}
        sj = _js_skip_ws(text, inner_start)
        while True:
            sj = _js_skip_ws(text, sj)
            if sj >= inner_end:
                raise _JSExprFail("F712 body without return")
            rk = _js_match_kw(text, sj, "return")
            if rk is not None:
                break
            dk, dnext = _js_match_decl_kw(text, sj)
            if dk is None:
                raise _JSExprFail("F712 bad statement")
            q = _js_skip_ws(text, dnext)
            vname, q = _js_parse_ident(text, q)
            q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
            q = _js_expect(text, q, "=")
            q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
            pairs, q = _js_parse_new_map_head(text, q, depth, state)
            q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
            q = _js_expect(text, q, ";")
            if vname in maps:
                raise _JSExprFail("F712 map redeclared")
            maps[vname] = pairs
            state["parts"] += 1  # the map binding is obfuscation-shaped
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            sj = q
        sj = _js_skip_ws(text, rk)
        total = None
        while True:
            term, sj = _js_parse_f712_operand(text, sj, maps, state)
            state["parts"] += 1
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            if total is None:
                total = term
            else:
                if len(total) + len(term) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
                    raise _JSExprFail("concat too long")
                total = total + term
            state["ops"] += 1
            sj = _js_skip_ws(text, sj)
            if sj < n and text[sj] == "+":
                sj = sj + 1
                continue
            break
        sj = _js_expect(text, sj, ";")
        sj = _js_skip_ws(text, sj)
        if sj != inner_end:
            raise _JSExprFail("F712 trailing statements")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the iife wrapper is obfuscation-shaped
        return total, j
    except _JSExprFail:
        return None
    """Parse `['LIT',...]` (the yield* delegation operand, F659).

    Node-exact (v24.20.0): `yield* <array>` delegates iteration --
    each element is yielded in order, so the delegation desugars to
    the array's element list. Only string-literal elements; holes,
    nested arrays, templates, spreads, or non-literal elements fail
    closed (the engine would yield `undefined`/objects there -- an
    honest miss, never a mis-decode). One optional paren pair
    around the array is tolerated (the F494 parenthesized-literal
    idiom). The empty array is legal (yields nothing). Returns
    (elements, next_index); the caller charges parts per element
    (F452). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    paren = False
    if j < n and text[j] == "(":
        paren = True
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("F659 yield* operand not an array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    elements = []
    if j < n and text[j] == "]":
        j += 1
    else:
        while True:
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j >= n:
                raise _JSExprFail("F659 array unterminated")
            if text[j] == ",":
                raise _JSExprFail("F659 array hole")
            if text[j] not in "'\"":
                raise _JSExprFail("F659 array element not a literal")
            val, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
            elements.append(val)
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j < n and text[j] == ",":
                j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
                if j < n and text[j] == "]":
                    j += 1  # trailing comma is node-legal
                    break
                continue
            if j < n and text[j] == "]":
                j += 1
                break
            raise _JSExprFail("F659 array not closed")
    if paren:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    return elements, j


def _js_parse_f6xx_fixed_yield_body(text, j, state,
                                   _allow_trailing_return,
                                   _gen_env=None):
    """Parse a fixed-yield generator `{...}` body (F494/F619/F659/F677).

    Shared body grammar: *j* is just past the opening `{`. The body
    is a bare sequence of `yield '<literal>';` statements
    (ws-tolerant, optional parens around the literal, ASI-tolerant
    before `}`) plus F659 `yield* ['LIT',...];` delegation
    statements over literal string arrays (each element yielded in
    order, node-exact v24.20.0). F677 adds `yield* IDENT();`
    delegation over a generator declared earlier in the same
    statement IIFE: when _gen_env is not None and IDENT is bound in
    it (declaration-before-use; forward/self references fail
    closed), the callee's concrete yield list is spliced in order
    (node-exact v24.20.0 -- `yield* h()` yields h()'s yields; the
    splice is by value, so delegation chains cannot recurse at
    parse time). When _allow_trailing_return, a trailing `return`
    (with or without a literal) is skipped node-exactly (it never
    contributes to the spread -- the F494 expression form);
    otherwise any `return` fails closed (the F619 statement form).
    Parameters, non-literal yields, loops, or any other statement
    fail closed. Parts are charged per yield (F452). Returns
    (yields, next_index) just past the closing `}`. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    yields = []
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == "}":
            j += 1
            break
        rk = _js_match_kw(text, j, "return")
        if rk is not None:
            if not _allow_trailing_return:
                raise _JSExprFail("generator body has return")
            # A trailing `return` (with or without a literal) never
            # contributes to the spread -- node-exact to skip it; it
            # must be the last statement.
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, rk)
            if j < n and text[j] in "'\"":
                _, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
                j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j < n and text[j] == ";":
                j += 1
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j >= n or text[j] != "}":
                raise _JSExprFail("return not last in generator")
            j += 1
            break
        j = _js_match_kw(text, j, "yield")
        if j is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("generator body not fixed yields")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == "*":
            # F659: `yield* ['LIT',...];` -- delegation over a literal
            # array desugars to the array's elements in order
            # (node-exact, v24.20.0). F677: `yield* IDENT();` --
            # delegation over a generator declared earlier in the
            # same statement IIFE (splice the callee's concrete
            # yields, node-exact v24.20.0). Only inputs the old
            # grammars failed closed are accepted here, so no
            # previously decoded input changes behavior.
            _ys = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            if _ys < n and text[_ys] in "[(":
                _ysa, j = _js_parse_f659_yield_star_array(
                    text, j + 1, state)
                for _yv in _ysa:
                    yields.append(_yv)
                    state["parts"] += 1
                    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                        raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
                j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
                if j < n and text[j] == ";":
                    j += 1
                continue
            if (_gen_env is not None and _ys < n
                    and text[_ys] in _JS_IDENT_START):
                _dn, _dk = _js_parse_ident(text, _ys)
                _dk = _js_skip_ws(text, _dk)
                if (_dk < n and text[_dk] == "(" and _dn in _gen_env):
                    _dk = _js_skip_ws(text, _dk + 1)
                    if _dk < n and text[_dk] == ")":
                        _dk = _js_skip_ws(text, _dk + 1)
                        if _dk < n and text[_dk] == ";":
                            _dk += 1
                        for _yv in _gen_env[_dn]:
                            yields.append(_yv)
                            state["parts"] += 1
                            if (state["parts"] >
                                    _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS):
                                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
                        j = _dk
                        continue
            raise _JSExprFail("yield* of non-literal")
        if j < n and text[j] == "(":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            paren = True
        else:
            paren = False
        if j >= n or text[j] not in "'\"":
            raise _JSExprFail("yield of non-literal")
        val, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
        if paren:
            j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == ";":
            j += 1
        yields.append(val)
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    if not yields:
        raise _JSExprFail("generator yields nothing")
    return yields, j


def _js_parse_generator_iife(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `(function*(){yield 'lit';...})()` at text[i] (F494).

    Desugars the immediately-invoked fixed-yield generator spread
    source: node-evaluated, [...(function*(){yield 'f1';yield
    'f2'})()] is ['f1','f2'] -- yields happen in order, and a
    trailing `return` (with or without a literal) contributes nothing
    to the spread (node v24.20.0 verified). The body must be a bare
    sequence of `yield '<literal>';` statements (ws-tolerant,
    optional parens around the literal, ASI-tolerant before `}`);
    F659 additionally accepts `yield* ['LIT',...];` delegation
    statements over literal string arrays (each element yielded in
    order, node-exact); parameters, non-literal yields, loops, a
    non-trailing `return`, or call args all fail closed (honest,
    documented boundary; the filed shapes are fixed-literal yields).
    Returns (_JSArr, next_index). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_expect(text, i, "(")
    j = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j), "function")
    if j is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not a function expression")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "*":
        raise _JSExprFail("not a generator")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_START:
        # Optional generator name: (function* gen(){...})().
        _, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "{")
    yields, j = _js_parse_f6xx_fixed_yield_body(
        text, j, state, _allow_trailing_return=True)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the iterable-spread source is obfuscation-shaped
    return _JSArr(yields), j


def _js_parse_spread_copy(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse '[...X]' spread-copy factory at text[i] (F471).

    Desugars the array-copy factory: node-evaluated, [...X] is
    element-identical to X for parsed string arrays (F485: sparse holes
    in the source parse as _JSNil and join as "", exactly like the
    decoder's other hole models). The source composes through the
    (_js_parse_primary): array literals, Array.of / Array(...)
    factories, Reflect.construct, Object.assign copies, and postfix
    chains on them. Returns a fresh _JSArr over the source's element
    list (a copy, so downstream mutation-shaped steps cannot alias the
    source).

    Bounded scope: the spread must be the ONLY element -- mixed forms
    ([...X, 'y']) fail closed; nested spread copies ([...[...X]]) fail
    closed via the in_spread_copy guard; spread of a non-array
    ([...'ab'.length]) fails closed. (F499) Spread of a STRING literal
    is modeled engine-exactly: the engine iterates the string's code
    points, so [...'ab'] is element-identical to ['a','b'] (node
    v24.20.0 verified; Python's str iteration is the same code-point
    iteration). Multiple string spreads may chain ([...'a', ...'b']);
    any other element in the chain fails closed.
    (F494) spread of the non-literal iterables `new Set([...])`,
    `new Map([[k,v],...]).values()`, and immediately-invoked
    fixed-yield generators `(function*(){yield 'lit';...})()` desugars
    engine-exactly (SameValueZero dedup, insertion-order values with
    later-wins keys, ordered yields); anything outside those bounded
    classes fails closed. (F695) spread of a bare literal
    `new Map([[k,v],...])` desugars to the insertion-ordered [k, v]
    pair arrays (the engine's Map iteration protocol, node v24.20.0
    -- `.entries()` / `.keys()` / `.values()` postfix forms keep
    their own F589/F600/F494 routes). No new parts are charged: every
    element was parts-charged at source parse (same convention as
    F461's reversal), except the F499 string-spread chars, which are
    parts-charged one per char. Whitespace after '...' is tolerated
    (punctuator + _js_skip_ws, node-verified). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if state.get("in_spread_copy"):
        raise _JSExprFail("nested spread copy")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    if not text.startswith("...", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("not a spread copy")
    state["in_spread_copy"] = True
    try:
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 3)
        src = None
        kk = _js_match_kw(text, k, "new")
        if kk is not None:
            # F494: new Set([...]) / new Map([...]).values() spread
            # sources. Any other `new X` falls through to the primary
            # path, which fails closed exactly as before.
            kkk = _js_skip_ws(text, kk)
            if _js_match_kw(text, kkk, "Set") is not None:
                src, j = _js_parse_new_set(text, k, depth, state)
            elif _js_match_kw(text, kkk, "Map") is not None:
                try:
                    src, j = _js_parse_new_map_values(text, k, depth,
                                                      state)
                except _JSExprFail:
                    try:
                        # F695: bare `new Map([[k,v],...])` spread --
                        # the engine's iteration protocol yields the
                        # insertion-ordered [k, v] pairs (node
                        # v24.20.0); each pair becomes a 2-element
                        # _JSArr. Previously fail-closed everywhere,
                        # so no previously-decoded input changes
                        # behavior.
                        src, j = _js_parse_f695_map_entries_spread(
                            text, k, depth, state)
                    except _JSExprFail:
                        # F589: not `.values()` and not a bare Map --
                        # it may be `.entries()`, which the primary
                        # path resolves via the _JSMap postfix branch
                        # (the _JSMapEntries iterator unwraps below).
                        # Any other shape fails closed there exactly
                        # as before (the re-parse charges parts again
                        # -- intentional double charge, F469
                        # precedent: the budget trips sooner).
                        src = None
        elif k < n and text[k] == "(":
            # F494: (function*(){yield ...})() spread source. A plain
            # parenthesized primary falls through to the primary path.
            kp = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1), "function")
            if kp is not None:
                kf = _js_skip_ws(text, kp)
                if kf < n and text[kf] == "*":
                    src, j = _js_parse_generator_iife(text, k, depth,
                                                      state)
        if src is None:
            src, j = _js_parse_primary(text, j + 3, depth, state)
    finally:
        state["in_spread_copy"] = False
    if isinstance(src, _JSMapEntries):
        # F589: spread of a Map entries iterator -- the engine's
        # iteration protocol yields the insertion-ordered [k, v]
        # pairs; desugared to a shallow copy of the pair list (the
        # F471 convention, so list-level steps cannot reorder the
        # source's pairs; pair elements are shared, never mutated).
        src = _JSArr(list(src.pairs))
    if isinstance(src, _JSMapKeys):
        # F600: spread of a Map keys iterator -- the engine's
        # iteration protocol yields the insertion-ordered keys;
        # desugared to a shallow copy of the key list (the F471
        # convention; numeric keys keep _JSNumStr provenance).
        src = _JSArr(list(src.keys))
    if isinstance(src, str) and not isinstance(src, _JSNumStr):
        # F499: spread of a string value iterates its characters
        # (node v24.20.0 verified). _JSNumStr is excluded: the engine
        # throws TypeError on [...42], so a numeric source fails
        # closed; every other plain-str primary is a genuine string
        # value in the engine, and spreading it is always the
        # char-iteration modeled here.
        return _js_parse_string_spread_rest(text, j, src, depth, state)
    if not isinstance(src, _JSArr):
        raise _JSExprFail("spread of non-array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("spread copy not closed")
    return _JSArr(list(src.elements)), j + 1


def _js_parse_string_spread_rest(text, j, first, depth, state):
    """Finish '[...<str>, ...<str>, ...]' after the first string source (F499).

    text[j] is just past the first string source; `first` is its value.
    Each character of every string source becomes one array element
    (parts-charged one per char). Sources are comma-separated and each
    must be '...'-prefixed and parse via _js_parse_primary to a genuine
    string value (plain str, never _JSNumStr -- the engine throws on
    non-iterables). Mixed shapes ([...'a', 'b'], [...['a'], ...'b'])
    fail closed -- documented scope boundary: only chained
    string-literal spreads are modeled. The in_spread_copy guard is
    held across the chain so nested spreads fail closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    elems = []
    src = first
    state["in_spread_copy"] = True
    try:
        while True:
            for ch in src:
                elems.append(ch)
                state["parts"] += 1
                if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                    raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
                break
            jj = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            if not text.startswith("...", jj):
                raise _JSExprFail("mixed spread copy")
            src, j = _js_parse_primary(text, jj + 3, depth, state)
            if not (isinstance(src, str) and
                    not isinstance(src, _JSNumStr)):
                raise _JSExprFail("mixed spread copy")
    finally:
        state["in_spread_copy"] = False
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("spread copy not closed")
    return _JSArr(elems), j + 1


def _js_parse_array(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse '[' element (, element)* ']' at text[i] (F434).

    Elements are additive string expressions; null/undefined/boolean
    keywords parse as _JSNil/_JSBool markers and nested array literals
    parse as _JSArr elements (F479/F485 -- nested to _JS_ARRAY_NEST_MAX
    levels deep; all coerce engine-exactly at the join/toString apply
    sites). Sparse holes (F485) parse as _JSNil markers: the engine
    elides them and join()/toString() coerce them to "", exactly like
    null/undefined, and filter/map skip-or-fail-closed on them exactly
    like the other markers (fail-safe). A trailing comma closes the
    array without adding a hole (node-verified: ['a','b',].length is
    2). Factories and spread inside arrays fail closed. Returns
    (_JSArr, next_index). Element count is charged to the expression
    part budget; the joined output is charged to _MAX_DECODE_BYTES in
    _js_apply_method.
    """
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    if text.startswith("...", j):
        # F471: the spread-copy factory is checked before the
        # nested-array guard so the source can itself be an array
        # literal parsed through the existing parsers.
        return _js_parse_spread_copy(text, i, depth, state)
    if state.get("in_array"):
        raise _JSExprFail("nested array")
    state["in_array"] = True
    try:
        i += 1
        elements = []
        n = len(text)
        while True:
            i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
            if i >= n:
                raise _JSExprFail("unterminated array")
            if text[i] == "]":
                # Empty array, or a trailing comma: ['a','b',] is the
                # 2-element array in the engine (F485).
                return _JSArr(elements), i + 1
            if text[i] == ",":
                # F485: sparse hole -- the engine elides it; modeled
                # as _JSNil so join/toString coerce it to "" and the
                # callback sites treat it exactly like null/undefined.
                elements.append(_JSNil())
                state["parts"] += 1
                if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                    raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
                i = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
                continue
            elem, i = _js_parse_array_element(text, i, depth, state,
                                             _nest=0)
            elements.append(elem)
            state["parts"] += 1
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
            if i < n and text[i] == ",":
                i = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
                continue
            if i < n and text[i] == "]":
                return _JSArr(elements), i + 1
            raise _JSExprFail("bad array")
    finally:
        state["in_array"] = False


def _js_parse_array_element(text, i, depth, state, _nest):
    """Parse one array-literal element at text[i] (F479/F485).

    Extends the additive-string grammar with the null/undefined/true/
    false keywords (ident-boundary checked; parsed as _JSNil/_JSBool
    markers -- they are NOT added to _js_parse_primary, so every other
    primary position still fails closed on them) and with nested array
    literals (F485: parsed by _js_parse_nested_array, depth-bounded by
    _JS_ARRAY_NEST_MAX via _nest -- the top-level array calls with
    _nest=0, so nesting deeper than the bound fails closed, never
    mis-decoded). Additive chains over the keywords (null+'x') fail
    closed -- honest, unmodeled. Factories and further array literals
    inside reach _js_parse_additive, where the outer array's in_array
    guard fails them closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    for kw in ("null", "undefined", "true", "false"):
        if text.startswith(kw, j):
            k = j + len(kw)
            if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                break
            if kw in ("null", "undefined"):
                return _JSNil(), k
            return _JSBool("true" if kw == "true" else "false"), k
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        if _nest >= _JS_ARRAY_NEST_MAX:
            raise _JSExprFail("array nesting too deep")
        return _js_parse_nested_array(text, j, depth, state, _nest=_nest + 1)
    elem, i = _js_parse_additive(text, i, depth, state)
    if isinstance(elem, _JSArr):
        raise _JSExprFail("nested array")
    return elem, i


def _js_parse_nested_array(text, i, depth, state, _nest=1):
    """Parse a nested array literal element (F479/F485).

    text[i] == '['. Elements use _js_parse_array_element at the current
    nesting level, so arrays nest to _JS_ARRAY_NEST_MAX levels deep
    (node-verified: [['f2']], [[['f2']]] etc. stringify recursively;
    deeper nesting fails closed -- an honest, documented boundary).
    Holes and trailing commas are honored exactly like the top-level
    array grammar (F485). Factories inside fail closed via the outer
    array's in_array guard. Element count is charged to the part
    budget. Returns (_JSArr, next_index). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    elements = []
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated nested array")
        if text[j] == "]":
            return _JSArr(elements), j + 1
        if text[j] == ",":
            # F485: sparse hole, same model as the top-level array.
            elements.append(_JSNil())
            state["parts"] += 1
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            continue
        elem, j = _js_parse_array_element(text, j, depth, state,
                                         _nest=_nest)
        elements.append(elem)
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == ",":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            continue
        if j < n and text[j] == "]":
            return _JSArr(elements), j + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("bad nested array")


def _js_parse_additive(text, i, depth, state, _forbid_template=False):
    """Parse expr ('+' expr)* at text[i]; returns (value, end_index).

    _forbid_template (F477): template literals anywhere in the chain
    fail closed instead of decoding.
    """
    val, i = _js_parse_primary(text, i, depth, state, _forbid_template)
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
        if j >= len(text) or text[j] != "+":
            return val, i
        rhs, i = _js_parse_primary(text, j + 1, depth, state,
                                   _forbid_template)
        if isinstance(val, _JSMethodRef) or isinstance(rhs, _JSMethodRef):
            # F490: a method reference is not an additive operand --
            # fail closed (never mis-decoded, never a TypeError).
            raise _JSExprFail("method ref in additive")
        if isinstance(val, (_JSArr, _JSThunks)) or isinstance(
                rhs, (_JSArr, _JSThunks)):
            raise _JSExprFail("array in additive")  # F434: no JS '+' on arrays
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        if len(val) + len(rhs) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
            raise _JSExprFail("concat too long")
        if isinstance(val, _JSBigInt) or isinstance(rhs, _JSBigInt):
            # F633: BigInt '+' semantics, node-exact (v24.20.0).
            # Both sides BigInt: numeric addition (1n+2n === 3n).
            # Exactly one side BigInt and the other a PLAIN string:
            # string concatenation ('a'+1n === 'a1'). A BigInt mixed
            # with a number literal throws in the engine
            # (1+2n is a TypeError) -- fail closed, never concat.
            if isinstance(val, _JSBigInt) and isinstance(rhs, _JSBigInt):
                val = _JSBigInt(str(int(val) + int(rhs)))
            elif ((isinstance(val, _JSBigInt) and type(rhs) is str)
                    or (isinstance(rhs, _JSBigInt)
                        and type(val) is str)):
                val = str(val) + str(rhs)
            else:
                raise _JSExprFail("bigint/number mix throws")
        else:
            val = val + rhs
        state["ops"] += 1


def _js_parse_ident(text, i):
    """Parse a JS identifier at text[i] (ASCII only); returns (name, next).

    Fail closed on anything else -- never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if i >= n or text[i] not in _JS_IDENT_START:
        raise _JSExprFail("not an identifier")
    j = i + 1
    while j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        j += 1
    return text[i:j], j


def _js_expect(text, i, ch):
    """Skip whitespace, then require the literal char *ch*; returns next."""
    i = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    if i >= len(text) or text[i] != ch:
        raise _JSExprFail("expected %r" % (ch,))
    return i + 1


def _js_parse_of_args_inner(text, k, state, closer):
    """Parse `arg (, arg)* <closer>` -- the F446 of-factory argument
    grammar (F446 / F476 / F484 / F486).

    Arguments are string literals (template literals included, as the
    engine evaluates them before the call), nested array literals
    (F484: node-verified, e.g. Array.of('f1',['f2']) is the 2-element
    array ['f1',['f2']]; the join/toString apply sites coerce nested
    _JSArr elements engine-exactly via _js_join_element, reusing the
    F479/F485 nested-array grammar depth-bounded by _JS_ARRAY_NEST_MAX),
    or a single '...'-spread of an array literal (F484: node-verified,
    Array.of(...['f1','f2']) === Array.of('f1','f2'); the spread source
    must be an array literal -- anything else fails closed, same as the
    F471 spread-copy boundary). Numeric args (of(2) is the 1-element
    array [2] in JS) and additive ('a'+'b') args fail closed
    (documented scope boundary: rejected, never mis-decoded). Factories
    inside nested args fail closed via the in_array guard, exactly like
    array literals. Zero args is the empty array. Element count is
    charged to the part budget. The *closer* is ')' for the direct call
    form and ']' for the .apply([...]) dispatch form (F486). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    elements = []
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k < n and text[k] == closer:
        return _JSArr(elements), k + 1
    while True:
        if k >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated of-factory")
        if text[k] == "[":
            # F484: nested array-literal arg. Guarded by in_array so
            # factories inside fail closed exactly like array
            # literals; the nested grammar itself is the F479/F485
            # one (holes, trailing commas, bounded depth).
            was_in_array = state.get("in_array")
            state["in_array"] = True
            try:
                arr, k = _js_parse_nested_array(text, k, 0, state,
                                               _nest=1)
            finally:
                state["in_array"] = was_in_array
            elements.append(arr)
            state["parts"] += 1
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        elif text.startswith("...", k):
            # F484: '...'-spread of an array literal. The source must
            # be an array literal; _js_parse_array charges the parts.
            k2 = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 3)
            if k2 >= n or text[k2] != "[":
                raise _JSExprFail("of-factory spread of non-array")
            arr, k = _js_parse_array(text, k2, 0, state)
            elements.extend(arr.elements)
        elif text[k] in "'\"`":
            if text[k] == "`":
                frag, k = _js_parse_template(text, k, 0, state)
            else:
                frag, k = _js_parse_string(text, k)
            elements.append(frag)
            state["parts"] += 1
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("of-factory arg not a string literal")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated of-factory")
        if text[k] == ",":
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            continue
        if text[k] == closer:
            return _JSArr(elements), k + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("bad of-factory args")


def _js_parse_of_args(text, k, state):
    """Parse the '(arg, ...)' argument list of an Array.of-shaped factory
    (F446 / F476 / F484).

    Delegates to _js_parse_of_args_inner with the ')' closer: string
    literals, nested array literals, and '...'-spreads of array literals
    (F484); numeric and additive args fail closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k >= n or text[k] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("of-factory without paren")
    return _js_parse_of_args_inner(text, k + 1, state, ")")


def _js_parse_array_of(text, i, state):
    """Parse 'Array.of(arg, ...)' at text[i] (F446).

    Returns (_JSArr, next_index). The argument list reuses the
    _js_parse_of_args grammar (string literals, nested array literals,
    and '...'-spreads of array literals; numeric or additive args fail
    closed). The 'of' name is
    ident-boundary-checked (Array.off / Array.ofx fail closed);
    whitespace is tolerated around the dot, the name, and the parens,
    exactly as the engine allows. Nested calls inside array literals
    fail closed, same as Array(N) (F443). Element count is charged to
    the part budget; the joined output is charged to _MAX_DECODE_BYTES
    in _js_apply_method. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if state.get("in_array"):
        raise _JSExprFail("Array inside array literal")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + len("Array"))
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("Array without dot")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if not text.startswith("of", k):
        # F508: Array.from(src[, mapFn]) -- the from factory (mapping
        # function / array-like / Set sources). Anything else still
        # fails closed ("Array not of").
        if text.startswith("from", k):
            # F520: fromAsync first -- it starts with "from".
            if text.startswith("fromAsync", k):
                _fe = k + len("fromAsync")
                if _fe < n and text[_fe] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                    raise _JSExprFail("Array.fromAsyncx is not fromAsync")
                return _js_parse_array_from_async(text, _fe, state)
            _fe = k + len("from")
            if _fe < n and text[_fe] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                raise _JSExprFail("Array.fromx is not from")
            return _js_parse_array_from(text, _fe, state)
        raise _JSExprFail("Array not of")
    k += 2
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Array.ofx is not of")
    # F486: Array.of.call/apply(...) -- static-factory dispatch through
    # Function.prototype. The dispatch head commits the parse: a
    # malformed dispatch fails closed here (never falls through to the
    # direct form, which would misread the args).
    _kd = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if _kd < n and text[_kd] == ".":
        _peek = _js_peek_dispatch_call(text, _kd)
        if _peek is not None:
            _dname, _paren = _peek
            if _dname not in ("call", "apply"):
                raise _JSExprFail("factory bind not modeled")
            return _js_parse_of_dispatch(text, _paren, _dname, 0, state)
    return _js_parse_of_args(text, k, state)


def _js_parse_array_from(text, j, state):
    """Parse the '(src[, mapFn])' argument list of Array.from (F508).

    *j* is just past the 'from' member name (dot or bracket-string
    form). The source is one of three modeled classes, each desugared
    to the element list engine-exactly (node v24.20.0 verified):
      - an array literal: `Array.from(['f1','f2'])` === `['f1','f2']`
        (parsed by _js_parse_array -- additive elements are engine
        values before Array.from reads them);
      - an array-like object literal: `Array.from({length:2, 0:'f1',
        1:'f2'})` === `['f1','f2']` (parsed by _js_parse_arraylike_recv,
        F487 -- the engine reads length then indices 0..length-1; the
        F487 grammar's all-indices-present requirement is conservative
        and engine-exact on the modeled class);
      - `new Set([...])`: `Array.from(new Set(['f1','f2']))` ===
        `['f1','f2']` (parsed by _js_parse_new_set, F494 --
        SameValueZero dedup, engine-exact);
      - `new Map([[k,v],...]).values()`: `Array.from(new
        Map([['a','f1']]).values())` === `['f1']` (parsed by
        _js_parse_new_map_values, F494/F598 -- Map.values() is an
        iterable of the values in insertion order with later-wins
        keys; a bare `new Map(...)` still fails closed since the
        engine iterates entries, not values).
      - `new Map([...]).keys()`: `Array.from(new Map([['f1',1],
        ['f2',2]]).keys())` === `['f1','f2']` (F600 -- the keys
        iterator yields the insertion-ordered keys; any other Map
        postfix shape fails closed since the engine iterates
        [k, v] pairs there).
      - a bare `new Map([[k,v],...])`: `Array.from(new
        Map([['k','f1']]))` === `[['k','f1']]` (F695 -- the
        engine's Map iteration protocol yields the
        insertion-ordered [k, v] pairs, node v24.20.0; each pair
        is a 2-element _JSArr; numeric keys keep _JSNumStr
        provenance; only the strict-literal class with string
        values is admitted).
    The optional mapper must be an identity callback
    (`x=>x` / `(x)=>x` / `function(x){return x;}` / `s=>{return s;}`,
    via _js_parse_map_callback, F437/F448) -- the elements pass
    through untouched, exactly as the engine applies it. The bare
    `String` mapper (stringifies numerics) fails closed (documented
    scope boundary -- the filed shapes are string-only); a thisArg
    third parameter fails closed; any other source (generators, bare
    Map, strings, numbers) fails closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("from without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        arr, j = _js_parse_array(text, j, 0, state)
    elif j < n and text[j] == "{":
        arr, j = _js_parse_arraylike_recv(text, j, 0, state)
    elif _js_match_kw(text, j, "new") is not None:
        # new Set(...) (a bare `new Map` iterates entries, not
        # values, so _js_parse_new_set fails it closed -- never
        # mis-decoded as a Set). F598: `new Map([[k,v],...]).values()`
        # is an iterable of the values in insertion order with
        # later-wins keys (node v24.20.0; via _js_parse_new_map_values,
        # F494) -- each added form was previously fail-closed
        # everywhere, so no previously-decoded input changes behavior.
        # F600: `new Map(...).keys()` desugars to the insertion-ordered
        # key list (engine-identical, node v24.20.0 verified). Any other
        # Map postfix shape fails closed (the engine's iteration
        # protocol yields [k, v] pairs, never bare values).
        _kk = _js_skip_ws(text, j + len("new"))
        if _js_match_kw(text, _kk, "Map") is not None:
            _mval = None
            try:
                _mval, _mj = _js_parse_primary(text, j, 0, state)
            except _JSExprFail:
                _mval = None
            if isinstance(_mval, _JSMapKeys):
                arr = _JSArr(list(_mval.keys))
                j = _mj
            else:
                try:
                    arr, j = _js_parse_new_map_values(text, j, 0, state)
                except _JSExprFail:
                    # F695: bare `new Map([[k,v],...])` -- the
                    # engine's iteration protocol yields the
                    # insertion-ordered [k, v] pairs (node
                    # v24.20.0); each pair becomes a 2-element
                    # _JSArr (the shared strict-literal grammar:
                    # string keys/values, keys keep _JSNumStr
                    # provenance). Previously fail-closed, so no
                    # previously-decoded input changes behavior.
                    try:
                        arr, j = _js_parse_f695_map_entries_spread(
                            text, j, 0, state)
                    except _JSExprFail:
                        raise _JSExprFail(
                            "from Map source not keys/values iterator")
        else:
            arr, j = _js_parse_new_set(text, j, 0, state)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("from source not modeled")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == ",":
        _me, _stringifies = _js_parse_map_callback(text,
                                                   _js_skip_ws(text,
                                                               j + 1))
        if _stringifies:
            raise _JSExprFail("from String mapper not modeled")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, _me)
        if j < n and text[j] == ",":
            raise _JSExprFail("from thisArg not modeled")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("from without close paren")
    return arr, j + 1


def _js_parse_array_from_async(text, j, state):
    """Parse 'Array.fromAsync(src[, mapFn]).then(cb)' at text[j] (F520).

    *j* is just past the 'fromAsync' member name (dot or bracket-string
    form). The source grammar is exactly _js_parse_array_from's (array
    literal / array-like / new Set, optional identity mapper) --
    node-verified (v24.20.0): Array.fromAsync resolves to the element
    list (SameValueZero dedup for Sets), and `.then(a=>a.join(''))`
    runs the callback on the resolved array, so the whole shape
    desugars to the resolved elements joined with ''.

    The returned Promise is not a decodable value; the `.then` must be
    present (a bare Array.fromAsync(...) fails closed, as does
    `.catch`/`.finally` directly on fromAsync(...) without `.then`).
    The callback must be a single-param arrow (`a=>`, `(a)=>`,
    `a=>{return ...;}`) or `function(a){return ...;}` whose body is
    exactly `<param>.join('')` -- the join separator must be the empty
    string literal (single or double quotes). After `.then(cb)` the
    postfix ends at the unmodeled step: a chained `.catch`/`.finally`
    leaves the decoded join value standing, engine-exact on fulfilled
    promises (node: the modeled source class cannot reject, so catch /
    finally pass the value through). Extra .then args, non-empty
    separators, and non-join callbacks fail closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    arr, j = _js_parse_array_from(text, j, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("fromAsync without then")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if not text.startswith("then", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("fromAsync without then")
    k = j + len("then")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("thenx is not then")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("then without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # Callback head: arrow or function, single param.
    if text.startswith("function", j):
        k = j + len("function")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
            raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
        param, j = _js_parse_ident(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1))
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if j >= n or text[j] != "{":
            raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("return", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
        k = j + len("return")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        func_body = True
    else:
        j0 = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j0 < n and text[j0] == "(":
            param, j = _js_parse_ident(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j0 + 1))
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
                raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        else:
            param, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j0)
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if not text.startswith("=>", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 2)
        func_body = False
        if j < n and text[j] == "{":
            # Block-body arrow: `a=>{return a.join('');}`
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            if not text.startswith("return", j):
                raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
            k = j + len("return")
            if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
            func_body = True
    # Body must be `<param>.join('')`.
    q, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
    if q != param:
        raise _JSExprFail("then callback not a join")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("then callback not a join")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if not text.startswith("join", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("then callback not a join")
    k = j + len("join")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("joinx is not join")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("then callback not a join")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] not in "'\"":
        raise _JSExprFail("then join separator not empty")
    qq = text[j]
    if j + 1 >= n or text[j + 1] != qq:
        raise _JSExprFail("then join separator not empty")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 2)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("then callback not a join")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if func_body:
        if j < n and text[j] == ";":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if j >= n or text[j] != "}":
            raise _JSExprFail("bad then callback")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # End of .then(: exactly one arg, then close paren.
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("then with extra args")
    j += 1
    # Desugar: the resolved array joined with ''.
    result = "".join(_js_join_element(e) for e in arr.elements)
    if len(result) > _MAX_DECODE_BYTES:
        raise _JSExprFail("fromAsync join too long")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the fromAsync.then dispatch is obfuscation-shaped
    return result, j


def _js_parse_bracket_member(text, j):
    """Parse `['<name>']` / `["<name>"]` (ws-tolerant) at text[j] == '['
    (F476 / F480); returns (name, idx_after_rbracket).

    The member name must be a non-empty ASCII identifier; anything else
    (non-literal keys, empty strings, names with whitespace) fails
    closed. Shared by the F476 factory member (`['of']`) and the F480
    dispatch/member members (`['apply']`, `['call']`, `['fromCharCode']`,
    `['assign']`). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] not in "'\"":
        raise _JSExprFail("member not a string literal")
    q = text[j]
    k = j + 1
    while k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        k += 1
    name = text[j + 1:k]
    if not name:
        raise _JSExprFail("empty member name")
    if k >= n or text[k] != q:
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated member string")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated member bracket")
    return name, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_f680_symbol_expr(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `Symbol.keyFor(Symbol.for('LIT'))` /
    `Symbol.for('LIT').description` at text[i] (F680).

    Symbol-mediated fragment reads: the decoder never resolved
    symbol keys or descriptions. Node-exact (v24.20.0):
    - `Symbol.keyFor(Symbol.for('LIT'))` is the registry key name
      ('LIT') -- keyFor returns undefined for non-registry symbols,
      which fail closed inside _js_parse_symbol_for_expr (bare
      `Symbol()` never parses there);
    - `Symbol.for('LIT').description` is the description ('LIT').
    A bare `Symbol.for('LIT')` (no .description) is NOT a string --
    the engine throws TypeError on symbol+string concatenation, so
    this production fails closed on it. (Note: tree-wide, a bare
    `Symbol.for('LIT')+'LIT2'` may still flag via the pre-existing
    shared paren-anchor quirk -- the `(` of the call parses as a
    grouping paren -- which over-blocks in the fail-closed
    direction; that quirk predates F680 and is out of scope.)
    Any other Symbol member (`Symbol.iterator`,
    `Symbol.asyncIterator`, ...) fails closed.
    Returns (value, next_index) with the postfix chain applied, or
    None when text[i] is not this shape. The call charges one op
    (obfuscation-shaped). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    m = _js_match_kw(text, i, "Symbol")
    if m is None:
        return None
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, m)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        return None
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    try:
        mk = _js_match_kw(text, j, "keyFor")
        if mk is not None:
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, mk)
            j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
            key, j = _js_parse_symbol_for_expr(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j))
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
            state["ops"] += 1  # the keyFor call is obfuscation-shaped
            return _js_parse_postfix(text, j, key, depth, state)
        mf = _js_match_kw(text, j, "for")
        if mf is not None:
            key, j = _js_parse_symbol_for_expr(text, i)
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
                raise _JSExprFail("Symbol.for without description")
            dm = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1),
                              "description")
            if dm is None:
                raise _JSExprFail("Symbol.for member not description")
            state["ops"] += 1  # the description read is
                               # obfuscation-shaped
            return _js_parse_postfix(text, dm, key, depth, state)
        return None
    except _JSExprFail:
        return None


def _js_parse_symbol_for_expr(text, j):
    """Parse `Symbol.for('K')` at text[j] (F588; shared with F552).

    The bracket form `[Symbol.for('K')]` (F552) wraps this expression;
    the bare form appears as the Reflect.get key (F588). `Symbol.for`
    returns the same symbol on every call (node v24.20.0), so a write
    `[Symbol.for('K')]:v` and a read `Symbol.for('K')` provably
    address the same key. Bare `Symbol()` creates a unique symbol per
    call -- a static model can never match a write to a read, so it
    fails closed (documented boundary; engine-exact for the static
    decoder: the two-call shape never resolves in node either).

    Returns (registry_key_name, next_index). Malformed shapes fail
    closed via _JSExprFail. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    k2 = _js_match_kw(text, j, "Symbol")
    if k2 is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not a Symbol key")
    k2 = _js_skip_ws(text, k2)
    if k2 >= n or text[k2] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("Symbol key needs .for")
    k3 = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, k2 + 1), "for")
    if k3 is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("Symbol key needs Symbol.for")
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Symbol.for needs paren")
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3 + 1)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] not in "'\"":
        raise _JSExprFail("Symbol.for key not a literal")
    key, k3 = _js_parse_string(text, k3)
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("Symbol.for unterminated")
    return key, _js_skip_ws(text, k3 + 1)


def _js_parse_f552_symbol_for(text, j):
    """Parse `[Symbol.for('K')]` at text[j] == '[' (F552).

    Symbol-keyed properties: only the shared-registry form is
    modeled. `Symbol.for('K')` returns the same symbol on every call
    (node v24.20.0), so a write `[Symbol.for('K')]:v` and a read
    `[Symbol.for('K')]` provably address the same key. Bare
    `Symbol()` creates a unique symbol per call -- a static model can
    never match a write to a read, so it fails closed (documented
    boundary; engine-exact for the static decoder: the two-call
    shape never resolves in node either).

    Returns (registry_key_name, next_index_after_rbracket).
    Malformed shapes fail closed via _JSExprFail. Never evals.
    """
    key, k = _js_parse_symbol_for_expr(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1))
    if k >= len(text) or text[k] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("Symbol key unterminated")
    return key, _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)


def _js_ownkeys_order(props):
    """Engine-exact Reflect.ownKeys order for F535-parsed props (F579).

    The engine orders own keys: integer indices (canonical decimal
    strings in 0..2**32-2) ascending numerically first, then other
    string keys in insertion order, then symbol keys in insertion
    order (node v24.20.0). props is the insertion-ordered dict from
    _js_parse_f535_object (keys are str or ("symbol", name) tuples).
    This is the same engine rule as _js_order_object_keys (F517),
    re-expressed for the F535 props dict (which the F517 key_order
    bookkeeping cannot consume directly); symbol keys are appended
    after strings per the engine's ownKeys order. Non-canonical
    digit strings (e.g. "01") are treated as plain string keys --
    the F535 parser stores key text verbatim, so the model follows
    the stored text, which is engine-exact (node v24.20.0:
    Reflect.ownKeys({'01':'x','1':'y',b:'z'}) -> ["1","01","b"]). Never evals.
    """
    int_keys = []
    str_keys = []
    sym_keys = []
    for k in props:
        if isinstance(k, tuple):
            sym_keys.append(k)
            continue
        if _js_is_integer_index_key(k):
            int_keys.append(k)
            continue
        str_keys.append(k)
    int_keys.sort(key=int)
    return int_keys + str_keys + sym_keys


def _js_parse_f579_ownkeys_key(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse `[Reflect.ownKeys(OBJLIT)[INTLIT]]` at text[j] == '[' (F579).

    Resolves the terminal index read on the engine-exact ownKeys key
    list to a plain string key: `Reflect.ownKeys({c:'f1',d:'f2'})[0]`
    is exactly 'c' (node v24.20.0). The caller uses the result exactly
    like a literal bracket key. Only the dot form on the Reflect
    namespace, only object literals parsed by _js_parse_f535_object,
    only a canonical non-negative integer terminal index; a symbol
    key at the index, an out-of-bounds index, or any other shape
    fails closed (the decoder never decodes `undefined`). The F496
    predicate-dispatch form (filter/find on prototypes) is a
    different shape on a different path and is unaffected. Never
    evals.

    Returns (key_string, idx_after_rbracket). Malformed shapes fail
    closed via _JSExprFail.
    """
    n = len(text)
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    k2 = _js_match_kw(text, k, "Reflect")
    if k2 is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("not a Reflect.ownKeys key")
    k2 = _js_skip_ws(text, k2)
    if k2 >= n or text[k2] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.ownKeys needs dot")
    k3 = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, k2 + 1), "ownKeys")
    if k3 is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect member not ownKeys")
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys without paren")
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3 + 1)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] != "{":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys arg not an object literal")
    props, k3 = _js_parse_f535_object(text, k3, depth, state)
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys unterminated")
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3 + 1)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys without terminal index")
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3 + 1)
    m = k3
    while m < n and text[m] in _ASCII_DIGITS:
        m += 1
    if m == k3:
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys index not an integer")
    digits = text[k3:m]
    if len(digits) > 10 or str(int(digits)) != digits:
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys index non-canonical")
    idx = int(digits)
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, m)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys index unterminated")
    k3 = _js_skip_ws(text, k3 + 1)
    if k3 >= n or text[k3] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys key bracket unterminated")
    keys = _js_ownkeys_order(props)
    if idx >= len(keys):
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys index out of bounds")
    key = keys[idx]
    if not isinstance(key, str):
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys resolved to a symbol key")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the key indirection is obfuscation-shaped
    return key, _js_skip_ws(text, k3 + 1)


def _js_fold_key_expr(text, k, depth, state):
    """Fold a computed member-key expression to its string value (F492).

    Parses one string literal or template literal followed by zero or
    more `+` literal operands at text[k] (whitespace tolerated exactly
    as the engine tokenizes), returning (folded_name, next_index).
    This is the engine's own constant evaluation for the filed key
    forms: `'con'+'cat'` === 'concat', `` `con${'cat'}` `` ===
    'concat' (node v24.20.0 verified) -- the cooked template string,
    which is what computed property access uses as the key.

    Only string-literal and literal-only-template operands are
    folded: numeric/additive/variable/call operands fail closed
    (documented scope boundary -- the decoder never guesses a key).
    The folded name must be a non-empty ASCII identifier, the same
    constraint as _js_parse_bracket_member; anything else fails
    closed. Each `+` operand is charged to the part budget. Never
    evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k >= n:
        raise _JSExprFail("empty key expression")
    c = text[k]
    if c in "'\"":
        part, k = _js_parse_string(text, k)
    elif c == "`":
        part, k = _js_parse_template(text, k, depth, state)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("key not a literal")
    out = [part]
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if j >= n or text[j] != "+":
            break
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("dangling key +")
        c = text[j]
        if c in "'\"":
            part, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
        elif c == "`":
            part, j = _js_parse_template(text, j, depth, state)
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("key operand not a literal")
        out.append(part)
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many key parts")
        k = j
    name = "".join(out)
    if not name or any(ch not in _JS_IDENT_CONT for ch in name):
        raise _JSExprFail("folded key not an identifier")
    return name, k


def _js_fold_bracket_key(text, j, depth, state):
    """Fold `['a'+'b']` / `` [`a${'b'}`] `` at text[j] == '[' (F492).

    Returns (folded_name, idx_after_rbracket), whitespace-tolerant.
    The plain-literal case is handled by _js_parse_bracket_member;
    callers try that first and fall back here for computed keys.
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    name, j = _js_fold_key_expr(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated computed key bracket")
    return name, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_indexed_key_array(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse `[<lit>, ...][<idx>]` with text[j] == '[' (array open).

    F504 core, shared with the F514 array-wrapped key: a string-literal
    array literal indexed by a literal non-negative integer is
    engine-identical to the selected element (node v24.20.0 verified).
    Returns (elems[idx], idx_after_index_close). A non-literal element,
    a non-literal or out-of-range index, an empty array, or a malformed
    bracket structure fails closed (documented scope boundary -- the
    decoder never guesses a key). Each element is charged to the part
    budget. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("indexed key not an array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    elems = []
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated key array")
        elem_start = j
        lit_val = None
        if text[j] in "'\"":
            lit_val, lit_end = _js_parse_string(text, j)
            lit_end = _js_skip_ws(text, lit_end)
            if lit_end < n and text[lit_end] in ",]":
                lit, j = lit_val, lit_end
            else:
                # A literal not terminated by `,`/`]` (e.g. `'con'`
                # followed by `+`) is not a plain element -- rewind
                # and try the fold below.
                lit_val = None
        if lit_val is None:
            # F514: folded inner elements ('con'+'cat',
            # `con${'cat'}`) compose with the F492 folder --
            # engine-identical to the literal element (node v24.20.0
            # verified). Anything else fails closed.
            try:
                lit, j = _js_fold_key_expr(text, elem_start, depth, state)
            except _JSExprFail:
                raise _JSExprFail("key array element not a string literal")
        elems.append(lit)
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many key parts")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated key array")
        if text[j] == ",":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            continue
        if text[j] == "]":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            break
        raise _JSExprFail("bad key array")
    if not elems:
        raise _JSExprFail("empty key array")
    # Shape: `[...][idx]` -- the array's `]` is already consumed; next
    # comes the index bracket.
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("key array without index")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    k = j
    while k < n and text[k].isdigit():
        k += 1
    if k == j:
        raise _JSExprFail("key index not a literal")
    idx = int(text[j:k])
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated key index")
    if idx >= len(elems):
        raise _JSExprFail("key index out of range")
    return elems[idx], _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_nested_bracket_key(text, j, depth, state):
    """Fold `[[<lit>, ...]][<idx>]` at text[j] == '[' (member open) (F504).

    Parses a string-literal array literal wrapped in a second bracket
    pair and indexed by a literal non-negative integer:
    `[['apply'][0]]` is engine-identical to `['apply']` (node
    v24.20.0 verified), so `Reflect[['apply'][0]]` is engine-identical
    to `Reflect['apply']`. Returns (element, idx_after_rbracket). A
    non-literal element, a non-literal or out-of-range index, an
    empty array, or a malformed bracket structure fails closed
    (documented scope boundary -- the decoder never guesses a key).
    Each element is charged to the part budget. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    name, j = _js_parse_indexed_key_array(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated nested key")
    return name, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_array_wrapped_array(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse `[[['concat'][0]]]` at text[j] == '[' (wrapper-array open).

    F514 core, extracted from _js_parse_array_wrapped_key: a
    single-element array literal whose element is an F504
    indexed-key fold (`['concat'][0]`): `[[['concat'][0]]]` is
    engine-identical to the single-string array `['concat']` (node
    v24.20.0 verified) -- ToPropertyKey of a single-string array is
    the string itself. Returns (folded_name, idx_after_wrapper_close).
    A multi-element wrapper array, a malformed structure, or a
    missing wrapper close fails closed (documented scope boundary --
    the decoder never guesses a key). The wrapper layer charges one
    part. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("array-wrapped key not an array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # The wrapper array's single element is itself an F504 indexed-key
    # fold (`['concat'][0]`): parse it at the inner array open.
    name, j = _js_parse_indexed_key_array(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated array-wrapped key")
    state["parts"] += 1
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many key parts")
    return name, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_array_wrapped_key(text, j, depth, state):
    """Fold `[[[<lit>, ...][<idx>]]]` at text[j] == '[' (member open)
    (F514).

    A single-element array literal whose element is an F504 indexed-key
    fold: `[[['concat'][0]]]` is engine-identical to `['concat']`
    (node v24.20.0 verified) -- ToPropertyKey of a single-string array
    is the string itself, so `String('f1')[[['concat'][0]]]` is
    engine-identical to `String('f1')['concat']`. Returns (folded_name,
    idx_after_rbracket). A multi-element wrapper array, a malformed
    structure, or a missing member close fails closed (documented
    scope boundary -- the decoder never guesses a key). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    name, j = _js_parse_array_wrapped_array(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated member key")
    return name, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_triple_nested_bracket_key(text, j, depth, state):
    """Fold `[[[['concat'][0]]][0]]` at text[j] == '[' (member open)
    (F523).

    A member-open bracket whose key is a double-array-wrapped F504
    indexed-key fold indexed by a literal integer: with
    E = `['concat'][0]` (engine-identical to the string `'concat'`),
    `[[E]]` is the single-element array `[['concat']]`, and
    `[[E]][0]` is the array `['concat']` (node v24.20.0 verified) --
    whose ToPropertyKey is `'concat'`, so
    `'f1'[[[['concat'][0]]][0]]` is engine-identical to
    `'f1'['concat']`. Returns (folded_name, idx_after_rbracket). A
    fourth nesting, a non-literal index, an index other than 0 (the
    double-wrapped array has exactly one element; `[1]` is
    `undefined`, whose ToPropertyKey is a different key), or a
    malformed structure fails closed (documented scope boundary --
    the decoder never guesses a key). The double-wrap layer
    charges one part. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("triple-nested key not double-array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("triple-nested key not double-array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # Inner element: the F504 indexed-key fold E (`['concat'][0]`).
    name, j = _js_parse_indexed_key_array(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated triple-nested inner array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated triple-nested outer array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("triple-nested key without index")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    k = j
    while k < n and text[k].isdigit():
        k += 1
    if k == j:
        raise _JSExprFail("triple-nested key index not a literal")
    if k - j > _JS_EXPR_MAX_INT_DIGITS:
        raise _JSExprFail("triple-nested key index too long")
    idx = int(text[j:k])
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated triple-nested key index")
    if idx != 0:
        raise _JSExprFail("triple-nested key index out of range")
    state["parts"] += 1
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many key parts")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated triple-nested member key")
    return name, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_quad_nested_bracket_key(text, j, depth, state):
    """Fold `[[[[['concat'][0]]][0]][0]]` at text[j] == '[' (member open)
    (F527).

    A member-open bracket whose key is the F523 triple-nested key
    re-wrapped once more and indexed again: with
    T = the triple key `[[['concat'][0]]][0]` (engine-identical to the
    array `[['concat']]`), the quad key is `[[T]][0]` -- `[T]` is the
    single-element array `[[['concat']]]`, and `[[T]][0]` is the array
    `[['concat']]` (node v24.20.0 verified) -- whose ToPropertyKey is
    `'concat'`, so `'f1'[[[[['concat'][0]]][0]][0]]` is
    engine-identical to `'f1'['concat']`. Returns (folded_name,
    idx_after_rbracket). A fifth nesting, a non-literal index, an
    index other than 0 (each wrapped array has exactly one element;
    `[1]` is `undefined`, whose ToPropertyKey is a different key), or
    a malformed structure fails closed (documented scope boundary --
    the decoder never guesses a key). The extra wrap layer charges one
    part. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("quad-nested key not quad-array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("quad-nested key not quad-array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("quad-nested key not quad-array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # Inner element: the F504 indexed-key fold E (`['concat'][0]`).
    name, j = _js_parse_indexed_key_array(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated quad-nested inner array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated quad-nested middle array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # First index: `[[[E]]][0]` (the F523 triple-key shape).
    j = _js_parse_quad_nested_index(text, j, "first")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated quad-nested wrap close")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # Second index: `[[T]][0]` where T is the triple key.
    j = _js_parse_quad_nested_index(text, j, "second")
    state["parts"] += 1
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many key parts")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated quad-nested member key")
    return name, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_quad_nested_index(text, j, which):
    """Parse `[<literal 0>]` at text[j] (F527 helper).

    The two index applications in the quadruple-nested key must both
    be the literal integer 0 (each wrapped array has exactly one
    element). Returns the index just after the index's `]`. A
    non-literal or non-zero index fails closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("quad-nested %s index without open" % which)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    k = j
    while k < n and text[k].isdigit():
        k += 1
    if k == j:
        raise _JSExprFail("quad-nested %s index not a literal" % which)
    if k - j > _JS_EXPR_MAX_INT_DIGITS:
        raise _JSExprFail("quad-nested %s index too long" % which)
    if int(text[j:k]) != 0:
        raise _JSExprFail("quad-nested %s index out of range" % which)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated quad-nested %s index" % which)
    return _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)


def _js_parse_reflect_member_key(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse a Reflect member key in literal/template/folded/nested-bracket
    form (F504). Returns (name, j_after). Each form is engine-identical to
    the literal member name (node v24.20.0 verified); any other form fails
    closed (documented scope boundary -- the decoder never guesses a key).
    """
    try:
        return _js_parse_bracket_member(text, j)
    except _JSExprFail:
        pass
    try:
        return _js_fold_bracket_key(text, j, depth, state)
    except _JSExprFail:
        pass
    return _js_parse_nested_bracket_key(text, j, depth, state)


# Node v24.20.0-verified own-property key lists (string keys only; the
# engine's lists also carry symbols, which can never === a string
# literal, so they are irrelevant to the filed predicate class).
# Used by the F496 ownKeys-selection parser to decide engine-exactly
# whether the selected name is really an own key of the enumeration
# source: `Reflect.ownKeys(SRC).filter(k=>k==='NAME')[0]` is NAME iff
# NAME is an own key of SRC, else undefined (and TARGET[undefined] is
# undefined, so any call throws -- not a decodable shape).
_F496_ARRAY_PROTO_KEYS = frozenset((
    "length", "constructor", "at", "concat", "copyWithin", "fill",
    "find", "findIndex", "findLast", "findLastIndex", "lastIndexOf",
    "pop", "push", "reverse", "shift", "unshift", "slice", "sort",
    "splice", "includes", "indexOf", "join", "keys", "entries",
    "values", "forEach", "filter", "flat", "flatMap", "map", "every",
    "some", "reduce", "reduceRight", "toReversed", "toSorted",
    "toSpliced", "with", "toLocaleString", "toString",
))
_F496_STRING_PROTO_KEYS = frozenset((
    "length", "constructor", "anchor", "at", "big", "blink", "bold",
    "charAt", "charCodeAt", "codePointAt", "concat", "endsWith",
    "fontcolor", "fontsize", "fixed", "includes", "indexOf",
    "isWellFormed", "italics", "lastIndexOf", "link", "localeCompare",
    "match", "matchAll", "normalize", "padEnd", "padStart", "repeat",
    "replace", "replaceAll", "search", "slice", "small", "split",
    "strike", "sub", "substr", "substring", "sup", "startsWith",
    "toString", "toWellFormed", "trim", "trimStart", "trimLeft",
    "trimEnd", "trimRight", "toLocaleLowerCase", "toLocaleUpperCase",
    "toLowerCase", "toUpperCase", "valueOf",
))
_F496_ARRAY_CTOR_KEYS = frozenset((
    "length", "name", "prototype", "isArray", "from", "fromAsync", "of",
))


def _js_parse_ownkeys_bracket_key(text, j, state):
    """Parse `[Reflect.ownKeys(SRC).filter(k=>k==='NAME')[0]]` /
    `[Reflect.ownKeys(SRC).filter(k=>k==='NAME')]` (1-element result
    coerces via ToPropertyKey) /
    `[Reflect.ownKeys(SRC).find(k=>k==='NAME')]` at text[j] == '[' (F496).

    Returns (selected_name, idx_after_rbracket), whitespace-tolerant.
    The selection is engine-identical to the string NAME when NAME is
    an own key of SRC (node v24.20.0 verified: the filter/find yields
    exactly NAME, and `[0]` on the 1-element filter result is NAME),
    and undefined otherwise -- the membership check against the
    node-verified key lists makes the desugar engine-exact, so the
    caller can treat the result exactly like a literal/computed member
    key and reuse its existing method-table validation (faithful by
    construction, no F481-class composition gap).

    Only the filed predicate class is modeled: a single arrow
    function (`k` or `(k)`) whose body is `k === 'NAME'` / `k == 'NAME'`
    (operand order either way, string literal only). SRC is one of the
    modeled enumeration sources (Array.prototype / String.prototype /
    Array / [].constructor, via _js_parse_reflect_get_target); anything
    else fails closed. Non-literal comparators, extra filter/find
    arguments, non-zero indices, and `Object.getOwnPropertyNames`
    fail closed (documented scope boundary: rejected, never
    mis-decoded). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # Reflect.ownKeys( -- dot form only (filed); the bracket form is
    # F498 territory for `construct` and unfiled for `ownKeys`.
    if not text.startswith("Reflect", k):
        raise _JSExprFail("not a Reflect.ownKeys selection")
    k += len("Reflect")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflectx is not Reflect")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys member not dotted")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if not text.startswith("ownKeys", k):
        raise _JSExprFail("not ownKeys")
    k2 = k + len("ownKeys")
    if k2 < n and text[k2] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeysx is not ownKeys")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k2)
    if k >= n or text[k] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys without paren")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    (kind, tname), k = _js_parse_reflect_get_target(text, k)
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys unterminated")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("no select call after ownKeys")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if text.startswith("filter", k):
        sel, k = "filter", k + len("filter")
    elif text.startswith("find", k):
        sel, k = "find", k + len("find")
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("select not filter/find")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("filterx/findx is not filter/find")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k >= n or text[k] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("select without paren")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    # Arrow parameter: `k` or `(k)`.
    if k < n and text[k] == "(":
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        param, k = _js_parse_ident(text, k)
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("bad arrow params")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    else:
        param, k = _js_parse_ident(text, k)
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if not text.startswith("=>", k):
        raise _JSExprFail("select predicate not an arrow")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 2)
    # Body: `param === 'NAME'` / `'NAME' === param` (== allowed).
    if k < n and text[k] in "'\"":
        name, k = _js_parse_string(text, k)
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if text.startswith("===", k):
            k += 3
        elif text.startswith("==", k):
            k += 2
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("predicate not an equality")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        pname, k = _js_parse_ident(text, k)
        if pname != param:
            raise _JSExprFail("predicate compares the wrong name")
    elif k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_START:
        pname, k = _js_parse_ident(text, k)
        if pname != param:
            raise _JSExprFail("predicate compares the wrong name")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if text.startswith("===", k):
            k += 3
        elif text.startswith("==", k):
            k += 2
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("predicate not an equality")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k >= n or text[k] not in "'\"":
            raise _JSExprFail("predicate key not a literal")
        name, k = _js_parse_string(text, k)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("predicate body not modeled")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated select call")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if sel == "filter":
        if k < n and text[k] == "[":
            # [0] -- the filter result is a 1-element array engine-exactly.
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            if k >= n or text[k] != "0":
                raise _JSExprFail("filter select index not 0")
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            if k >= n or text[k] != "]":
                raise _JSExprFail("unterminated [0]")
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        elif k < n and text[k] == "]":
            # No index: the 1-element filter result coerces to NAME via
            # ToPropertyKey (Array.prototype[['concat']] is
            # Array.prototype['concat'], node v24.20.0 verified). Falls
            # through to the closing-bracket check below.
            pass
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("filter select without [0]")
    if k >= n or text[k] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("unterminated ownKeys bracket")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if kind == "ctor":
        ok = name in _F496_ARRAY_CTOR_KEYS
    elif tname == "array":
        ok = name in _F496_ARRAY_PROTO_KEYS
    else:
        ok = name in _F496_STRING_PROTO_KEYS
    if not ok:
        # Engine-exact: the selection is undefined (NAME is not an own
        # key of SRC), so TARGET[undefined] is undefined and any call
        # throws in node -- not a decodable shape. Fail closed, never
        # mis-decoded.
        raise _JSExprFail("ownKeys name not an own key of SRC")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the indirection is obfuscation-shaped
    return name, k


def _js_parse_ctor_factory(text, i, state):
    """Parse '[].constructor.<member>(...)' / '[].constructor[<member>](...)'
    at text[i] (F476 / F480).

    Desugars the constructor-property factory path to the fragment list:
    `[].constructor` is engine-identical to `Array`
    (node v24.20.0 verified), and the same holds for any non-empty
    array-literal receiver with string-literal elements (F497):
    `['x'].constructor` is engine-identical to `Array` (any array
    literal's [[Prototype]] is Array.prototype, whose `constructor` is
    Array). The receiver elements are discarded. `['of']` is
    engine-identical to `.of` (node v24.20.0 verified). The argument
    list reuses the F446/F484 factory grammar (_js_parse_of_args):
    string literals, nested array literals, and '...'-spreads of array
    literals decode node-exactly; numeric or additive args still fail
    closed (same scope boundary as _js_parse_array_of --
    `[].constructor.of(1)` node-evaluates to `[1]` but is refused,
    consistent with F446's Array.of(1) refusal). The
    F480 dispatch members (`apply`/`call`, dot or bracket-string) route
    to the constructor-dispatch parser. Only the `of`/`apply`/`call`
    members are modeled: `[].constructor.from('a','b')` throws
    TypeError in the engine (from's second arg must be a function), so
    `from` with string args fails closed -- it is never mis-decoded as
    `of`; `[].constructor.from(['a','b'])` needs no factory model: the
    inner array literal already splices through the pre-existing
    array-literal + `.join` path (the `from(` wrapper is copied verbatim
    around it, node agrees on the joined text), so find_credential flags
    it without any constructor-property modeling (documented, not
    filed). The `of`/`apply`/`call`/`constructor` names are
    ident-boundary-checked (`[].constructor.ofx`, `[].constructor['ofx']`
    fail closed); computed member access with non-literal keys
    (`[].constructor[x]`) and `Array.prototype.constructor` chains fail
    closed; the receiver is exactly an array literal -- empty (`[]`,
    F476) or non-empty with string-literal elements (F497); numeric,
    nested, template, or spread receivers fail closed (documented scope
    boundary: rejected, never mis-decoded); whitespace
    is tolerated between every token of the factory call itself, exactly
    as the engine allows (the following method chain follows the
    decoder's existing postfix whitespace rules -- no space before the
    '.', as with every other modeled method). The `Array[<member>]`
    receiver form is handled by _js_parse_array_bracket_dispatch.
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if state.get("in_array"):
        raise _JSExprFail("Array inside array literal")
    # [].constructor.<member> -- exact empty-array receiver (F476);
    # ['lit',...].constructor.<member> -- non-empty array-literal
    # receiver (F497). _js_parse_string_literal_list validates the
    # elements (string literals only, part-budget charged) and the
    # caller routes here only after the quote-aware
    # _js_is_nonempty_ctor_recv peek matched '].constructor'.
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    if j < n and text[j] == "]":
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    else:
        _, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j, state, "]")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        raise _JSExprFail("no dot after receiver")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if not text.startswith("constructor", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("not constructor")
    j += len("constructor")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("constructorx is not constructor")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "(":
        # F488: [].constructor('f1','f2') -- direct constructor
        # invocation via the constructor property (no factory member).
        # Desugars to the F454 multi-arg Array constructor
        # (string-literal-only args, same fail-closed classes).
        elements, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j + 1, state,
                                                     ")")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the call is obfuscation-shaped
        return _JSArr(elements), j
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # [].constructor['of'] / [].constructor['apply'] -- bracket-string
        # member on the constructor property. F496: an ownKeys
        # filter/find selection resolves to its engine-exact key and
        # routes identically.
        try:
            name, j = _js_parse_bracket_member(text, j)
        except _JSExprFail:
            name, j = _js_parse_ownkeys_bracket_key(text, j, state)
    elif j < n and text[j] == "?":
        # F490: [].constructor?.of -- `?.` adjacent (engine SyntaxError
        # otherwise, node v24.20.0 verified); [].constructor is
        # provably non-null so ?. ≡ .
        if j + 1 >= n or text[j + 1] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("no member after constructor")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 2)
        name, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
    elif j < n and text[j] == ".":
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        name, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("no member after constructor")
    if name == "of":
        # F486: [].constructor.of.call/apply(...) -- static-factory
        # dispatch through Function.prototype. The dispatch head
        # commits the parse: a malformed dispatch fails closed here.
        _kd = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if _kd < n and text[_kd] == ".":
            _peek = _js_peek_dispatch_call(text, _kd)
            if _peek is not None:
                _dname, _paren = _peek
                if _dname not in ("call", "apply"):
                    raise _JSExprFail("factory bind not modeled")
                return _js_parse_of_dispatch(text, _paren, _dname, 0,
                                             state)
        if _kd < n and text[_kd] == "(":
            return _js_parse_of_args(text, j, state)
        # F490: ([].constructor?.of) / ([].constructor.of) -- the
        # factory as a reference; the following call applies it (see
        # _js_parse_methodref_step). A bare unapplied reference fails
        # closed upstream (a _JSMethodRef is never a decoded variant).
        return _JSMethodRef("factory", "of"), j
    if name in ("apply", "call"):
        # F480: [].constructor['apply']([].constructor, [...]) --
        # constructor-function dispatch through the constructor property.
        return _js_parse_ctor_apply(text, i, state)
    raise _JSExprFail("constructor member not modeled")


def _js_parse_ctor_callee(text, i):
    """Peek a constructor-function callee reference at text[i] (F488).

    Matches (ws-tolerant, ident-boundary-checked):
      [].constructor | []['constructor'] | []["constructor"]
      Object.getPrototypeOf([]).constructor
      Object.getPrototypeOf([])['constructor']
      Object.getPrototypeOf([])["constructor"]
    All four are engine-identical to the Array constructor
    (node v24.20.0 verified). Returns the end index, or None when
    text[i] is not such a callee. Pure lookahead on no-match -- never
    raises for a non-callee; a malformed bracket member after a
    matched head returns None (the caller falls through to normal
    parsing, which fails closed). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # [].constructor or []['constructor']
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if k >= n or text[k] != "]":
            return None
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if k < n and text[k] == ".":
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            if not text.startswith("constructor", k):
                return None
            k += len("constructor")
            if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                return None
            return k
        if k < n and text[k] == "[":
            try:
                name, k = _js_parse_bracket_member(text, k)
            except _JSExprFail:
                return None
            if name != "constructor":
                return None
            return k
        return None
    if text.startswith("Object", j):
        k = j + len("Object")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            return None
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
            return None
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if not text.startswith("getPrototypeOf", k):
            return None
        k += len("getPrototypeOf")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            return None
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k >= n or text[k] != "(":
            return None
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if k >= n or text[k] != "[":
            return None
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if k >= n or text[k] != "]":
            return None
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
            return None
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if k < n and text[k] == ".":
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            if not text.startswith("constructor", k):
                return None
            k += len("constructor")
            if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                return None
            return k
        if k < n and text[k] == "[":
            try:
                name, k = _js_parse_bracket_member(text, k)
            except _JSExprFail:
                return None
            if name != "constructor":
                return None
            return k
        return None
    return None


def _js_parse_array_bracket_dispatch(text, i, state):
    """Parse 'Array[<member>](...)' at text[i] (F476 / F480).

    Dispatches on the bracket-string member name: 'of' reuses the F446
    factory grammar (F476); 'apply'/'call' route to the F480
    constructor-dispatch parser. Anything else fails closed. Never
    evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if state.get("in_array"):
        raise _JSExprFail("Array inside array literal")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + len("Array"))
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Arrayx is not Array")
    name, j = _js_parse_bracket_member(text, j)
    if name == "of":
        # F486: Array['of'].call/apply(...) -- static-factory dispatch
        # through Function.prototype (bracket-member form). The dispatch
        # head commits the parse: a malformed dispatch fails closed here
        # (never falls through to the direct form, which would misread
        # the args).
        _kd = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if _kd < n and text[_kd] == ".":
            _peek = _js_peek_dispatch_call(text, _kd)
            if _peek is not None:
                _dname, _paren = _peek
                if _dname not in ("call", "apply"):
                    raise _JSExprFail("factory bind not modeled")
                return _js_parse_of_dispatch(text, _paren, _dname, 0, state)
        return _js_parse_of_args(text, j, state)
    if name in ("apply", "call"):
        return _js_parse_ctor_apply(text, i, state)
    if name == "from":
        # F508: Array['from'](...) -- bracket-string member form of the
        # from factory, engine-identical to the dot form (F480
        # precedent).
        return _js_parse_array_from(text, j, state)
    if name == "fromAsync":
        # F520: Array['fromAsync'](...) -- bracket-string member form
        # of the async factory, engine-identical to the dot form (F480
        # precedent).
        return _js_parse_array_from_async(text, j, state)
    raise _JSExprFail("Array bracket member not modeled")


def _js_parse_carrier_escaped_string(text, j):
    """Parse `\\'...\\'` / `\\"..."` at text[j] (F590).

    The whole-text scanner anchors INSIDE an outer string literal
    (the "carrier"), so the raw text carries the carrier's escapes:
    raw `\\'` is `'` in the carrier's value (node-exact). This
    parses one carrier-escaped element to its value, applying the
    standard string-literal termination rule at the VALUE level (a
    value quote preceded by an odd run of value backslashes is an
    escaped interior quote, not the terminator -- so raw `\\\\\\'`
    correctly fails closed exactly where the value-level parse
    would). The resolved value interior is re-parsed by
    `_js_parse_string`, so value-level escapes resolve through the
    tested path. Exotic carrier escapes (`\\n`, `\\xNN`, ...) fail
    closed -- conservative, documented. Only called where a plain
    backslash always raised before: strictly additive, never
    mis-decodes. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    quote = text[j + 1]
    k = j + 2  # past the opening \'
    val_chars = []
    trail_bs = 0  # trailing run of value backslashes (F452: O(1)
                  # amortized -- the old rescan was O(L^2))
    while True:
        if k >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("carrier string unterminated")
        c = text[k]
        if c == "\\" and k + 1 < n and text[k + 1] in "'\"\\":
            vch = text[k + 1]  # \' -> ', \" -> ", \\ -> \
            k += 2
            if vch == quote:
                # Value-level termination rule: the value string
                # ends here iff the quote is not value-escaped.
                if trail_bs % 2 == 0:
                    break  # terminator: not part of the value
                val_chars.append(vch)
                trail_bs = 0
                continue
            val_chars.append(vch)
            trail_bs = trail_bs + 1 if vch == "\\" else 0
            continue
        if c == "\\":
            raise _JSExprFail("carrier exotic escape fails closed")
        if c == quote:
            # A bare delimiter quote cannot appear in genuine
            # carrier text (it would end the carrier) -- fail
            # closed rather than mis-decode.
            raise _JSExprFail("bare quote in carrier element")
        val_chars.append(c)
        trail_bs = 0
        k += 1
    val, end = _js_parse_string(quote + "".join(val_chars) + quote, 0)
    if end != len(quote + "".join(val_chars) + quote):
        raise _JSExprFail("carrier value re-parse trailing text")
    return val, k


def _js_parse_string_carrier_tolerant(text, j):
    """Parse one string literal, tolerating carrier escapes (F590).

    When the whole-text scanner anchors inside an outer string
    literal (the "carrier"), raw `\\'` is `'` in the carrier's
    value (node-exact). Delegates to
    `_js_parse_carrier_escaped_string` in that case, else to
    `_js_parse_string` (backtick still raises there -- fail
    closed). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if j < n and text[j] == "\\" and j + 1 < n \
            and text[j + 1] in "'\"":
        return _js_parse_carrier_escaped_string(text, j)
    return _js_parse_string(text, j)


def _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j, state, closer):
    """Parse `lit (, lit)* <closer>` -- a string-literal-only argument
    list (F454 / F446 / F480 grammar); returns (elements, idx_after_closer).

    Every element must be a string literal -- numeric, nested, marker,
    additive, or template args fail closed (documented scope boundary:
    rejected, never mis-decoded -- the same fail-closed classes as the
    F454 multi-arg Array constructor and the F446 Array.of factory, so
    any desugar reusing this grammar is faithful to its direct form --
    no F481-class composition gap). Zero elements (empty list) is the
    empty list. Element count is charged to the part budget. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    elements = []
    if j < n and text[j] == closer:
        return elements, j + 1
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        frag, j = _js_parse_string_carrier_tolerant(text, j)
        elements.append(frag)
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated args")
        if text[j] == ",":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            continue
        if text[j] == closer:
            return elements, j + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("bad args")


def _js_parse_array_ctor_ref(text, j):
    """Parse exactly 'Array' / '[].constructor' at text[j] (F480);
    returns the end index.

    Only the bare constructor reference is modeled: 'Arrayx',
    '[1].constructor', 'Array.prototype.constructor', and any other
    receiver fail closed (documented scope boundary). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[" and _js_is_ctor_recv(text, j):
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)    # ']'
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)    # '.'
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)    # 'constructor'
        return k + len("constructor")
    if text.startswith("Array", j):
        k = j + len("Array")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("Arrayx is not the Array constructor")
        return k
    raise _JSExprFail("not the Array constructor")


def _js_parse_ctor_apply(text, i, state):
    """Parse 'Array.apply(Array, [...])' / 'Array.call(Array, ...)' and the
    bracket-string-member / '[].constructor' receiver variants at text[i]
    (F480); returns (_JSArr, next_index).

    Desugars the constructor-function dispatch: node-evaluated
    (v24.20.0), the Array constructor called as a plain function with an
    argument list is engine-identical to the multi-argument Array
    constructor (F454):
      Array.apply(Array, ['a','b']) === Array.call(Array, 'a','b')
        === Array['apply'](Array, ['a','b'])
        === [].constructor['apply']([].constructor, ['a','b'])
        === Array('a', 'b')
    so the argument list reuses the F454 string-literal-only grammar
    (_js_parse_string_literal_list): numeric, nested, marker, or
    additive args fail closed -- the SAME fail-closed classes as the
    direct form, so the desugar is faithful (no F481-class gap).

    Bounds (all fail closed -- documented scope boundary: rejected,
    never mis-decoded):
    - Receiver: exactly the Array constructor or [].constructor
      (_js_parse_array_ctor_ref). Array.prototype.apply and
      user-function receivers are F478 territory or unmodeled --
      fail closed.
    - Member: .apply / .call / ['apply'] / ['call'] (ws-tolerant,
      ident-boundary-checked). Any other member fails closed.
    - thisArg (the dispatch's first argument): exactly the Array
      constructor / [].constructor. Node ignores thisArg for the Array
      constructor, but anything else fails closed (conservative --
      documented residual, never mis-decoded).
    - apply takes a single argsArray '[' ... ']' of string literals;
      call takes the arguments inline (zero args is the empty array,
      engine-exact). A second apply argument, a missing comma, or
      non-literal args fail closed.
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if state.get("in_array"):
        raise _JSExprFail("Array inside array literal")
    j = _js_parse_array_ctor_ref(text, i)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == ".":
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        name, k = _js_parse_ident(text, k)
        j = k
    elif j < n and text[j] == "[":
        name, j = _js_parse_bracket_member(text, j)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("constructor dispatch without member")
    if name not in ("apply", "call"):
        raise _JSExprFail("constructor dispatch member not apply/call")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("constructor dispatch without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    j = _js_parse_array_ctor_ref(text, j)  # thisArg: exactly Array
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("constructor dispatch without thisArg comma")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if name == "apply":
        if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
            raise _JSExprFail("apply argsArray not an array")
        elements, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j + 1, state,
                                                     "]")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("constructor dispatch with extra args")
        return _JSArr(elements), j + 1
    if j < n and text[j] == ")":
        elements, j = [], j + 1  # Array.call(Array) === Array() === []
    else:
        elements, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j, state, ")")
    return _JSArr(elements), j


def _js_match_entries_values_member(text, m):
    """Match `entries` / `values` at text[m] (F581 helper).

    Returns (member_name, next_index) with the ident-boundary guard
    (entriesx is not entries); raises _JSExprFail on no match. Shared
    by the dot and F490 `?.` member forms so the two spellings cannot
    drift. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    for _name in ("entries", "values"):
        if text.startswith(_name, m):
            _m2 = m + len(_name)
            if _m2 < n and text[_m2] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                raise _JSExprFail("F581 %sx is not %s" % (_name, _name))
            return _name, _m2
    raise _JSExprFail("F581 member not entries/values")


def _js_parse_reflect_apply_enumb(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse 'Reflect.apply(Object.entries|values, thisArg, [X])' (F581).

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): Reflect.apply(F, thisArg,
    argsList) is F.call(thisArg, ...argsList); Object.entries and
    Object.values ignore their this-value (node-verified), so with a
    single-element argsList this is exactly Object.entries(X) /
    Object.values(X). X parses via _js_parse_primary and must be a
    _JSArr of all-string elements (the filed shape is the F581
    alias-return IIFE, i.e. the arguments exotic; a plain
    string-literal array is engine-identical -- the entries of an
    array-like are the [index-string, value] pairs in ascending
    index order, the values are the elements themselves, node
    v24.20.0). The member accepts the dot form, the F490 `?.` form,
    and the F480 bracket-string form (all engine-identical, node
    verified). Returns (_JSArr, next_index); the caller applies the
    postfix chain. Never evals.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - a target other than Object.entries/Object.values (the callee
      re-parses from i; a peek mis-route fails closed here);
    - an argsArray that is not exactly one element;
    - an element that is not a _JSArr of all-string elements
      (numeric elements would need ToString coercion -- the decoder
      never guesses; documented);
    - thisArg is parsed and discarded via _js_parse_ignored_value
      (F519 precedent -- the builtins ignore it, node-verified).
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = i + len("Reflect")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflectx is not Reflect")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "?" and j + 1 < n and text[j + 1] == ".":
        # F490: Reflect?.apply -- `?.` adjacent (engine SyntaxError
        # otherwise, node v24.20.0 verified); Reflect is provably
        # non-null so ?. === . (same normalization as the ctor path).
        j += 1
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        _mname, j = _js_parse_reflect_member_key(text, j, depth, state)
        if _mname != "apply":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect bracket member not apply")
    else:
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without dot")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("apply", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without apply")
        k = j + len("apply")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("applyx is not apply")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # Target: Object.entries / Object.values (re-parsed from here;
    # the peek is lookahead only).
    if not text.startswith("Object", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 target not Object")
    k = j + len("Object")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 Objectx is not Object")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    member = None
    if k < n and text[k] == ".":
        member, k = _js_match_entries_values_member(text,
                                                    _js_skip_ws(text,
                                                                k + 1))
    elif k + 1 < n and text[k] == "?" and text[k + 1] == ".":
        # F490: Object?.entries -- `?.` adjacent (engine SyntaxError
        # otherwise); Object is provably non-null so ?. === .
        member, k = _js_match_entries_values_member(text,
                                                    _js_skip_ws(text,
                                                                k + 2))
    elif k < n and text[k] == "[":
        _bm, k = _js_parse_bracket_member(text, k)
        if _bm not in ("entries", "values"):
            raise _JSExprFail("F581 target not entries/values")
        member = _bm
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 target without member")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 target without thisArg")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    _, j = _js_parse_ignored_value(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 thisArg without argsArray")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 argsArray not an array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    elem, j = _js_parse_primary(text, j, depth, state)
    if not isinstance(elem, _JSArr):
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 argsArray element not an array")
    if any(type(_e) is not str for _e in elem.elements):
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 argsArray element not all-string")
    state["parts"] += 1  # the apply dispatch is obfuscation-shaped
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 argsArray not a single element")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("F581 with extra args")
    elements = list(elem.elements)
    if member == "entries":
        # The entries of an array-like are the [index-string, value]
        # pairs in ascending index order (node v24.20.0) -- the
        # arguments exotic and the plain array agree element-for-
        # element, so the desugar is faithful for both.
        value = _JSArr([_JSArr([str(_idx), _el])
                        for _idx, _el in enumerate(elements)])
    else:
        value = _JSArr(list(elements))
    state["ops"] += 1  # the apply dispatch is obfuscation-shaped
    return value, j + 1


def _js_parse_reflect_apply_ctor(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse 'Reflect.apply(Array, Array, [...])' /
    "Reflect['apply'](Array, Array, [...])" at text[i] (F480);
    returns (_JSArr, next_index).

    Desugar: Reflect.apply(Array, thisArg, argsList) calls the Array
    constructor as a plain function with the argsList elements as its
    arguments -- node-evaluated (v24.20.0):
      Reflect.apply(Array, Array, [['a'],['b']]) === Array(['a'],['b'])
      Reflect.apply(Array, Array, ['a','b'])     === Array('a','b')
    The thisArg is ignored by the Array constructor in node; we
    conservatively require exactly Array / [].constructor and fail
    closed on anything else. Each argsList element is one constructor
    argument: a string literal or a string-literal array literal (the
    F468 element grammar via _js_parse_string_literal_array -- nested
    _JSArr elements survive through the F479 join coercion,
    engine-exact). Numeric, marker, additive, template, or
    doubly-nested elements fail closed -- the same classes the
    F454/F468 grammars fail closed on, so the desugar is faithful.
    A fourth argument fails closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = i + len("Reflect")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflectx is not Reflect")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "?" and j + 1 < n and text[j + 1] == ".":
        # F490: Reflect?.apply -- `?.` adjacent (engine SyntaxError
        # otherwise, node v24.20.0 verified); Reflect is provably
        # non-null so ?. ≡ . (The decoder has no binding forms, so a
        # shadowed Reflect cannot arise in a parsed shape --
        # documented.)
        j += 1
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # F480: Reflect['apply'](...) -- the bracket form is routed here
        # by _js_parse_reflect_call, which already verified the member
        # name; re-verify (defense-in-depth: the dispatch cannot
        # mis-route). F504: template and nested-bracket member forms
        # are engine-identical to the literal form (node v24.20.0
        # verified), so the same fallback chain applies here.
        _mname, j = _js_parse_reflect_member_key(text, j, depth, state)
        if _mname != "apply":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect bracket member not apply")
    else:
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without dot")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("apply", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without apply")
        k = j + len("apply")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("applyx is not apply")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    j = _js_parse_array_ctor_ref(text, j)  # target: exactly Array
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without thisArg")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # F490: thisArg may be null/undefined -- the target is provably
    # Array, which ignores its thisArg, so null/undefined is
    # engine-identical to Array here (node v24.20.0 verified).
    _marker, _mk = _js_parse_marker_kw(text, j)
    if _marker is not None and isinstance(_marker, _JSNil):
        j = _mk
    else:
        j = _js_parse_array_ctor_ref(text, j)  # thisArg: exactly Array
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without argsList")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply argsList not an array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    elements = []
    if j < n and text[j] == "]":
        j += 1
    else:
        while True:
            if j < n and text[j] in "'\"":
                frag, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
                elements.append(frag)
            elif j < n and text[j] == "[":
                sub, j = _js_parse_string_literal_array(text, j, state)
                elements.append(sub)
            else:
                raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply arg not decodable")
            # Parts are charged per argsList element on top of the parts
            # the element sub-parsers already charged (intentional double
            # charge: the budget trips sooner, i.e. fails closed sooner
            # -- do not "dedupe"; F469 precedent).
            state["parts"] += 1
            if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
                raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j >= n:
                raise _JSExprFail("unterminated Reflect.apply args")
            if text[j] == ",":
                j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
                continue
            if text[j] == "]":
                j += 1
                break
            raise _JSExprFail("bad Reflect.apply args")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply with extra args")
    return _JSArr(elements), j + 1


def _js_is_ctor_recv(text, i):
    """Cheap text-level peek: does text[i] start a '[].constructor' shape?

    Matches '[' ws ']' ws '.' ws 'constructor' (ident-boundary) exactly.
    The F471 spread-copy '[' and ordinary array literals never have ']'
    (ws-tolerant) as the second token, and empty-array method calls like
    `[].concat(...)` (F473) never have `constructor` after the dot, so
    the F476 factory path and the array/concat paths are disjoint.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        return False
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        return False
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if not text.startswith("constructor", j):
        return False
    j += len("constructor")
    return j >= n or text[j] not in _JS_IDENT_CONT


def _js_is_nonempty_ctor_recv(text, i):
    """Cheap text-level peek: does text[i] start a
    '[<array-literal>].constructor' shape with a NON-EMPTY receiver (F497)?

    Quote-aware scan for the receiver's matching ']' (nested '['
    tracked, string/template literals skipped so a ']' inside a
    literal cannot terminate the scan early), then '.' 'constructor'
    (ident-boundary) exactly. Malformed receivers return False (the
    caller falls through to the normal array-literal path, which fails
    closed on them). Disjoint from _js_is_ctor_recv (empty receiver),
    the F471 spread-copy '[' (never followed by a string literal that
    closes before '.constructor'... the scan requires the literal
    '.constructor' after the match), and ordinary array literals
    (`['x'].join('')` has no '.constructor' after the bracket).
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] == "]":
        return False  # empty receiver -- _js_is_ctor_recv territory
    depth = 0
    k = j
    while k < n:
        c = text[k]
        if c in "'\"`":
            q = c
            k += 1
            while k < n and text[k] != q:
                if text[k] == "\\":
                    k += 1
                k += 1
            if k >= n:
                return False
            k += 1
        elif c == "[":
            depth += 1
            k += 1
        elif c == "]":
            if depth == 0:
                break
            depth -= 1
            k += 1
        else:
            k += 1
    if k >= n or text[k] != "]":
        return False
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
        return False
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if not text.startswith("constructor", k):
        return False
    k += len("constructor")
    return k >= n or text[k] not in _JS_IDENT_CONT


def _js_parse_array_constructor(text, i, state):
    """Parse 'Array()' / 'Array(N)' / 'Array(a, b, ...)' / 'Array.of(...)'.

    The '.of' factory form dispatches to _js_parse_array_of (F446);
    the plain form is the F443 single-integer constructor plus the
    F454 multi-argument constructor. Returns (_JSArr, next_index).

    N is a decimal or 0x integer literal 0.._JS_EXPR_MAX_FILL; the
    zero-arg form is an empty array. Elements start as the empty
    string: real Array(N) is sparse, but holes join exactly as ""
    does, so modeling them as "" is byte-identical through
    .join/.fill/.filter(Boolean). The multi-argument form
    Array('frag', ...) requires every argument to be a string
    literal (node evaluates the call to the fragment list, exactly
    as modeled); a single string arg is the 1-element list
    (node: ['a']). Numeric, mixed, nested, or additive args fail
    closed (documented scope boundary: rejected, never
    mis-decoded), as do nested calls inside array literals. The
    result only survives when consumed by .join(); a bare
    Array(...)/Array.of(...) decodes to nothing (not
    obfuscation-shaped). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + len("Array"))
    if j < n and text[j] == ".":
        # F480: Array.apply(Array, ...) / Array.call(Array, ...) route to
        # the constructor-dispatch parser; 'of' keeps the F446 factory;
        # anything else falls through to _js_parse_array_of, which fails
        # closed ("Array not of").
        _mk = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        for _mname in ("apply", "call"):
            _me = _mk + len(_mname)
            if text.startswith(_mname, _mk) and \
                    (_me >= n or text[_me] not in _JS_IDENT_CONT):
                return _js_parse_ctor_apply(text, i, state)
        return _js_parse_array_of(text, i, state)
    if state.get("in_array"):
        raise _JSExprFail("Array inside array literal")
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Array without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j < n and text[j] == ")":
        return _JSArr([]), j + 1
    if j < n and text[j] in "'\"`":
        # F454: multi-argument constructor -- parse the string-literal
        # argument list exactly like _js_parse_array_of's (same
        # budgets, same fail-closed arg classes). The shared helper
        # keeps the F480 constructor-dispatch desugar faithful to this
        # direct form.
        elements, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j, state, ")")
        return _JSArr(elements), j
    count, j = _js_parse_number(text, j)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("bad Array args")
    j += 1
    if count > _JS_EXPR_MAX_FILL:
        raise _JSExprFail("Array too big")
    state["parts"] += count
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    return _JSArr([""] * count), j


def _js_parse_string_literal_array(text, i, state):
    """Parse '[' lit (, lit)* ']' -- string literals only (F468).

    The F468 Reflect.construct args-list array: every element must be a
    string literal ('...', "...", or `...` via _js_parse_template,
    mirroring _js_parse_array_of's element classes). Numeric, additive,
    nested, or otherwise non-literal elements fail closed (documented
    scope boundary -- consistent with the F454/F446 arg classes the
    desugar targets: the F454 multi-arg constructor also requires
    string-literal args). Element count is charged to the part budget.
    Nested inside another array literal fails closed (same rule as the
    F443/F454 constructors). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if state.get("in_array"):
        raise _JSExprFail("Array inside array literal")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    elements = []
    if j < n and text[j] == "]":
        return _JSArr(elements), j + 1
    while True:
        if j >= n or text[j] not in "'\"`":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect args not string literals")
        if text[j] == "`":
            frag, j = _js_parse_template(text, j, 0, state)
        else:
            frag, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
        elements.append(frag)
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated Reflect args")
        if text[j] == ",":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            continue
        if text[j] == "]":
            return _JSArr(elements), j + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("bad Reflect args")


def _js_parse_f595_json_array(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `[` ELT (`,` ELT)* `]` at text[i] (text[i] == '[') (F595).

    JSON-literal array elements: string literals ('...', "...", or `...`
    templates via _js_parse_template, mirroring
    _js_parse_string_literal_array's element classes) or nested arrays
    (recursive, depth-bounded by _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH). Used ONLY in the
    F571 stringify VALUE position: the flat form is element-identical
    to _js_parse_string_literal_array (which it replaces there), and
    the nested form was previously fail-closed there -- so no
    previously-decoded input changes behavior. Numerics, objects,
    and non-literal elements fail closed (documented boundary --
    consistent with the F468 element classes this mirrors). The
    `in_array` guard is preserved: an outer array-literal context
    still fails closed as before. Element count charges the F430
    parts budget. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if state.get("in_array"):
        raise _JSExprFail("Array inside array literal")
    if depth >= _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH:
        raise _JSExprFail("F595 array too deep")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, i + 1)
    elements = []
    if j < n and text[j] == "]":
        return _JSArr(elements), j + 1
    while True:
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated F595 array")
        c = text[j]
        if c in "'\"`":
            if c == "`":
                frag, j = _js_parse_template(text, j, 0, state)
            else:
                frag, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
            elements.append(frag)
        elif c == "[":
            sub, j = _js_parse_f595_json_array(text, j, depth + 1, state)
            elements.append(sub)
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("F595 array element not a literal")
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated F595 array")
        if text[j] == ",":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            continue
        if text[j] == "]":
            return _JSArr(elements), j + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("bad F595 array")


def _js_parse_reflect_construct(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse 'Reflect.construct(Array, <argsArray>)' at text[i] (F468).

    Desugars the reflection-API factory: node-evaluated,
    Reflect.construct(Array, X) calls new Array(...X). The target must
    be the bare `Array` identifier -- any other target (String, Object,
    an expression, newTarget as a third argument) fails closed. The
    arguments-list X must parse as a fragment array: a string-literal
    array literal (via _js_parse_string_literal_array -- numeric,
    additive, or nested elements fail closed, consistent with the
    F454/F446 arg classes), the F446 Array.of(...) factory, or the
    F443/F454 Array constructor call forms (`Array(...)` / `Array(N)`;
    the `new Array(...)` spelling as reflect-args fails closed --
    conservative, documented). They compose through the existing
    parsers, within their own part budgets. Anything else
    (numeric args, nested calls, additive args, a third argument)
    fails closed -- documented scope boundary: rejected, never
    mis-decoded.

    (F498) The bracket-string member form `Reflect['construct']` /
    `Reflect["construct"]` is modeled identically to the dot form
    (node v24.20.0 verified): literal keys via _js_parse_bracket_member,
    constant-folded keys via _js_fold_bracket_key (F492); any other
    member fails closed. In the bracket form the target may also be
    `[].constructor` (engine-identical to Array, via the shared F480
    _js_parse_array_ctor_ref); the dot form keeps the bare-Array-only
    target scope above.

    The result only survives when consumed by .join() etc. through
    _js_parse_postfix; a bare Reflect.construct(...) with no call
    decodes to nothing (not obfuscation-shaped, same as bare
    Array(...)). Whitespace is tolerated around the dot (F455-class,
    exactly the _js_skip_ws class), matching the _JS_EXPR_START
    anchor. Reflect.apply is parsed by _js_parse_reflect_apply_concat
    (F469) -- this function handles construct only. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = i + len("Reflect")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflectx is not Reflect")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # F498: Reflect['construct'] -- bracket-string member access,
        # node-identical to the dot form (node v24.20.0 verified).
        # Literal keys via _js_parse_bracket_member; constant-folded
        # keys via _js_fold_bracket_key (F492); any other member fails
        # closed. The target may be the bare Array identifier or
        # [].constructor (engine-identical, via the shared F480
        # _js_parse_array_ctor_ref); the dot form keeps F468's
        # bare-Array-only target scope.
        _mname, j = _js_parse_reflect_member_key(text, j, depth, state)
        if _mname != "construct":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect bracket member not construct")
        is_bracket = True
    else:
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without dot")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("construct", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without construct")
        k = j + len("construct")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("constructx is not construct")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        is_bracket = False
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.construct without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if is_bracket:
        j = _js_parse_array_ctor_ref(text, j)
    else:
        # Target: exactly the bare Array identifier (F468 scope).
        tgt, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        if tgt != "Array":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.construct non-Array target")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.construct without args")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        arr, j = _js_parse_string_literal_array(text, j, state)
    elif text.startswith("Array", j):
        arr, j = _js_parse_array_constructor(text, j, state)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.construct args not a fragment array")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        # A third argument (newTarget) changes the created object's
        # identity -- fails closed.
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.construct with extra args")
    return arr, j + 1


def _js_extend_concat_apply_args(text, j, elements, depth, state):
    """Parse the `[` argsArray of a concat-application at text[j] (F469).

    Shared by the direct `Reflect.apply(T, thisArg, argsArray)` form
    and the F506 higher-order dispatch desugar -- both reduce to
    thisArg.concat(...argsArray) (engine-identical, node v24.20.0
    verified), so they share the exact element grammar. Extends
    *elements* in place: each argsArray element is one concat
    argument (array args extended one level, engine-exact). Parts are
    charged per element on top of the parts the element sub-parsers
    already charged (intentional double charge, F430 precedent: the
    budget trips sooner, i.e. fails closed sooner -- do not "dedupe").
    Returns the index just after `]`. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)  # past the `[`
    if j < n and text[j] == "]":
        return j + 1
    while True:
        if j < n and text[j] in "'\"":
            frag, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
            elements.append(frag)
        elif j < n and text[j] == "`":
            frag, j = _js_parse_template(text, j, depth, state)
            elements.append(frag)
        elif j < n and text[j] == "[":
            sub, j = _js_parse_array(text, j, depth, state)
            if any(not isinstance(e, str) for e in sub.elements):
                raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply arg non-string element")
            elements.extend(sub.elements)
        elif text.startswith("Array", j):
            sub, j = _js_parse_array_constructor(text, j, state)
            if not isinstance(sub, _JSArr):
                raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply arg not an array")
            if any(not isinstance(e, str) for e in sub.elements):
                raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply arg non-string element")
            elements.extend(sub.elements)
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply arg not decodable")
        # Parts are charged per argsArray element here on top of the
        # parts the element sub-parsers already charged (intentional
        # double charge: the budget trips sooner, i.e. fails closed
        # sooner -- do not "dedupe").
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n:
            raise _JSExprFail("unterminated Reflect.apply args")
        if text[j] == ",":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            continue
        if text[j] == "]":
            return j + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("bad Reflect.apply args")


def _js_parse_reflect_apply_concat(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse 'Reflect.apply(Array.prototype.concat, <thisArg>, <argsArray>)'
    at text[i] (F469).

    Desugars the reflection-API method-application shape: node-evaluated,
    Reflect.apply(fn, thisArg, argsArray) calls fn.apply(thisArg,
    argsArray); with fn = Array.prototype.concat the result is
    thisArg.concat(...argsArray) with engine-exact concat semantics --
    each argsArray element becomes one concat argument: array arguments
    are appended element-wise (one level only), string arguments are
    appended as-is. The result only survives when consumed by .join()
    etc. through _js_parse_postfix; a bare Reflect.apply(...) with no
    call decodes to nothing (not obfuscation-shaped, same as bare
    Array(...)).

    Bounds (all fail closed -- documented scope boundary: rejected,
    never mis-decoded):
    - Target: exactly the bare chain Array.prototype.concat
      (ws-tolerant dots, F455-class), or the F504 `[].concat` form
      (empty-array-literal receiver, ws-tolerant): node-evaluated,
      [].concat === Array.prototype.concat (v24.20.0), so the desugar
      is engine-identical. Any other target (Math.max,
      Array.prototype.push, user functions, non-empty-array receivers
      like `['x'].concat`, a bare `concat` ident) fails closed.
    - thisArg: a string-literal array literal (via _js_parse_array) or
      the F446/F454 Array constructor call forms (via
      _js_parse_array_constructor); `new Array(...)` as thisArg fails
      closed (conservative, consistent with F468). Non-array thisArg
      fails closed; numeric elements decode through the pre-existing
      _js_parse_array number-to-string element model (join-exact,
      node-verified).
    - argsArray: a '[' element-list; each element is a string literal,
      a literal-only template literal, an array literal (via
      _js_parse_array -- its elements are extended one level,
      engine-exact for concat array args), or an Array constructor call
      form (via _js_parse_array_constructor, _JSArr with all-str
      elements). Bare numeric/additive args (`[42]`) and nested arrays
      (`[[['a']]]`) fail closed; numerics, additives, and fromCharCode
      nested inside array args decode through the pre-existing
      _js_parse_array element model (join-exact, node-verified).
    - A third argument fails closed. Whitespace is tolerated around
      the dots (F455-class, exactly the _js_skip_ws class), matching
      the _JS_EXPR_START anchor. No new regexes; never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = i + len("Reflect")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflectx is not Reflect")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # F504: Reflect['apply'] / Reflect[`apply`] /
        # Reflect[['apply'][0]] -- the non-dot member forms are routed
        # here by _js_parse_reflect_call, which already verified the
        # member name; re-verify (defense-in-depth: the dispatch cannot
        # mis-route). All three are engine-identical to the dot form
        # (node v24.20.0 verified).
        _mname, j = _js_parse_reflect_member_key(text, j, depth, state)
        if _mname != "apply":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect bracket member not apply")
    else:
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without dot")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("apply", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without apply")
        k = j + len("apply")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("applyx is not apply")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # F504: `[].concat` -- the empty-array-literal receiver.
        # Node: [].concat === Array.prototype.concat (v24.20.0), so
        # the concat-application desugar below is engine-identical.
        # Non-empty receivers fail closed (documented scope boundary).
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply non-empty-array target")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply target without dot")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        ident, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        if ident != "concat":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply non-concat target")
    else:
        # Target: exactly the bare chain Array.prototype.concat.
        ident, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        if ident != "Array":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply non-Array target")
        for name in ("prototype", "concat"):
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
            if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
                raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply target without dot")
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            ident, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
            if ident != name:
                raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply non-concat target")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without thisArg")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    # thisArg: array literal or Array constructor call forms.
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        recv, j = _js_parse_array(text, j, depth, state)
    elif text.startswith("Array", j):
        recv, j = _js_parse_array_constructor(text, j, state)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply thisArg not an array")
    if not isinstance(recv, _JSArr):
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply thisArg not an array")
    if any(not isinstance(e, str) for e in recv.elements):
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply thisArg non-string element")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without argsArray")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply argsArray not an array")
    # Desugar: thisArg.concat(...argsArray) -- each argsArray element is
    # one concat argument (array args extended one level, engine-exact).
    elements = list(recv.elements)
    j = _js_extend_concat_apply_args(text, j, elements, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        # A third argument changes the call arity -- fails closed.
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply with extra args")
    return _JSArr(elements), j + 1


def _js_parse_fn_params(text, j):
    """Parse `(p1, p2, ...)` at text[j] (F519); returns (params, next).

    Each parameter must be an ASCII identifier; duplicates fail
    closed (conservative: node v24.20.0 raises SyntaxError for arrow
    duplicates and gives last-wins for sloppy function duplicates --
    outside the filed scope either way). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("F519 params without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    params = []
    if j < n and text[j] == ")":
        return params, j + 1
    while True:
        if j >= n or text[j] not in _JS_IDENT_START:
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 bad param")
        name, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        if name in params:
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 duplicate param")
        params.append(name)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == ",":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            continue
        if j < n and text[j] == ")":
            return params, j + 1
        raise _JSExprFail("F519 bad param list")


def _js_parse_fn_additive_body(text, j, params, state):
    """Parse `operand (+ operand)*` at text[j] (F519).

    Every operand must be a declared parameter name (whitespace
    tolerated); anything else (literals, member access, calls, `this`,
    nested functions, parenthesized sub-expressions) fails closed --
    the engine would evaluate arbitrary code, which the static
    decoder cannot. Returns (operand_indices, next_index) where each
    index is into *params*. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    idx = []
    while True:
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n or text[j] not in _JS_IDENT_START:
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 body operand not a param")
        name, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        if name not in params:
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 body references undeclared name")
        idx.append(params.index(name))
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == "+":
            j += 1
            continue
        return idx, j


def _js_match_decl_kw(text, j):
    """Match a `var`/`const`/`let` keyword at text[j] (F528/F529).

    Returns (keyword, next_index) with next_index just after the
    keyword, or (None, j) when no declaration keyword matches.
    The three keywords bind identically in the modeled positions
    (node v24.20.0 verified). Never evals.
    """
    for kw in ("var", "const", "let"):
        m = _js_match_kw(text, j, kw)
        if m is not None:
            return kw, m
    return None, j


def _js_parse_iife_block_body(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse `{ var|const|let f = FN; return f; }` at text[j] (F528).

    Returns (fn_params, body_operands, next_index) with next_index just
    after the closing brace -- the same triple as _js_parse_fn_target,
    for the function value the block evaluates to. Node-exact desugar
    (v24.20.0): the block declares exactly one binding initialized to
    the literal function value FN (a literal arrow or function
    expression, parsed by _js_parse_fn_target) and returns that
    binding, so the block evaluates to FN itself.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - more than the two statements, a declaration keyword other than
      var/const/let, a non-FN initializer, returning a different
      ident than the declared one, or any other block shape.
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "{")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    _kw, m = _js_match_decl_kw(text, j)
    if m is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F528 block not a var decl")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, m)
    fname, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "=")
    fn_params, body, j = _js_parse_fn_target(
        text, _js_skip_ws(text, j), depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ";")
    rk = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j), "return")
    if rk is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F528 block without return")
    rname, j = _js_parse_ident(text, _js_skip_ws(text, rk))
    if rname != fname:
        raise _JSExprFail("F528 block returns another ident")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == ";":
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "}")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the intermediate binding is obfuscation-shaped
    return fn_params, body, j


def _js_parse_iife_fn_inner(text, j, depth, state, _iife_ext_ok):
    """Parse `(IIFE)` (the IIFE without its invocation) at text[j].

    Returns (params, body_operands, next_index) with next_index just
    after the IIFE's closing paren -- the same triple as
    _js_parse_fn_target, for the function value the IIFE evaluates to.
    With _iife_ext_ok false this is exactly the F522 grammar; with true it
    additionally accepts the F528 forms:
    - a NESTED IIFE `((F522-IIFE))()` as the arrow body -- exactly one
      nesting level around the strict F522 shape (the nested attempt
      recurses with _iife_ext_ok=False, so deeper nesting fails closed);
    - a BLOCK body `{ var|const|let f = FN; return f; }` on the arrow
      form and on the function-expression form.
    Node-exact desugars (v24.20.0): the nested IIFE evaluates its
    inner F522 IIFE to FN, so the outer evaluates to FN; the block
    body binds FN to one name and returns it, so it evaluates to FN.
    Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if text.startswith("function", j):
        k = j + len("function")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F522 functionx IIFE")
        if k < n and text[k] == "*":
            raise _JSExprFail("F522 generator IIFE")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_START:
            # Optional name (engine-exact; ignored).
            _, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        iife_params, j = _js_parse_fn_params(text, j)
        if iife_params:
            raise _JSExprFail("F522 IIFE with params")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n or text[j] != "{":
            raise _JSExprFail("F522 IIFE without brace")
        if _iife_ext_ok:
            j2 = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
            is_decl = any(_js_match_kw(text, j2, kw) is not None
                          for kw in ("var", "const", "let"))
            if is_decl:
                # F528: (function(){ var|const|let f = FN; return f; }).
                fn_params, body, j = _js_parse_iife_block_body(
                    text, j, depth, state)
                j = _js_expect(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j), ")")
                return fn_params, body, j
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        rk = _js_match_kw(text, j, "return")
        if rk is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("F522 IIFE without return")
        fn_params, body, j = _js_parse_fn_target(
            text, _js_skip_ws(text, rk), depth, state)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == ";":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        j = _js_expect(text, j, "}")
    else:
        # Arrow IIFE.
        iife_params, j = _js_parse_fn_params(text, j)
        if iife_params:
            raise _JSExprFail("F522 IIFE with params")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if not text.startswith("=>", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("F522 IIFE arrow without =>")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 2)
        if _iife_ext_ok and j < n and text[j] == "{":
            # F528: (() => { var|const|let f = FN; return f; }).
            fn_params, body, j = _js_parse_iife_block_body(
                text, j, depth, state)
        elif _iife_ext_ok and j < n and text[j] == "(":
            # F528: one nesting level of parens around the F522 shape,
            # two placements (both node-exact, v24.20.0):
            #   (a) (() => ((F522-IIFE))()) -- parens around the
            #       uninvoked IIFE, invocation outside (filed shape);
            #   (b) (() => ((F522-IIFE)())) -- parens around the
            #       invoked IIFE.
            # The nested attempts parse the strict F522 shape
            # (_iife_ext_ok=False); on any mismatch they fall back to the
            # plain FN parse so previously-decoded inputs are
            # unaffected.
            try:
                k = j + 1
                fn_params, body, k = _js_parse_iife_fn_inner(
                    text, k, depth, state, False)
                k = _js_expect(text, _js_skip_ws(text, k), ")")
                k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                k = _js_expect(text, k, "(")
                k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                k = _js_expect(text, k, ")")
                state["ops"] += 1  # the nested layer is obfuscation-shaped
                j = k
            except _JSExprFail:
                try:
                    k = j + 1
                    fn_params, body, k = _js_parse_iife_fn_target(
                        text, k, depth, state, False)
                    k = _js_expect(text, _js_skip_ws(text, k), ")")
                    state["ops"] += 1  # the paren layer is obfuscation-shaped
                    j = k
                except _JSExprFail:
                    fn_params, body, j = _js_parse_fn_target(
                        text, j, depth, state)
        else:
            fn_params, body, j = _js_parse_fn_target(
                text, j, depth, state)
    # Close the IIFE paren (the invocation is parsed by the caller).
    j = _js_expect(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j), ")")
    return fn_params, body, j


def _js_parse_iife_fn_target(text, j, depth, state, _iife_ext_ok=True):
    """Parse an IIFE-evaluated function target `(IIFE)()` at text[j]
    (F522, F528).

    Returns (params, body_operands, next_index) -- the same triple as
    _js_parse_fn_target, for the function value the IIFE evaluates
    to. Node-exact desugars (v24.20.0): `(() => FN)()` and
    `(function(){return FN})()` evaluate to FN itself (F522); the F528
    extensions -- a nested IIFE `(() => ((F522-IIFE))())()` or
    `(() => ((F522-IIFE)()))()` (one paren level around the F522
    shape, either placement) and a
    block body `{ var|const|let f = FN; return f; }` on either IIFE
    form -- likewise evaluate to FN itself (the IIFE is immediately
    invoked with zero arguments and returns FN unchanged), so a
    Reflect.apply target in any of these shapes is engine-identical
    to the literal-FN target F519 models.

    The IIFE must be exactly one of the documented shapes, followed
    by the immediate zero-argument invocation `()`.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - non-empty IIFE params, invocation with arguments, nested IIFEs
      beyond the single F528 level (the nested attempt parses the
      strict F522 inner, so a deeper IIFE fails there), IIFEs
      returning non-functions, block bodies with more than the two
      modeled statements or returning another ident, async/generator
      IIFEs (the `async`/`function*` heads fail the subform parses),
      `new`-invoked or method-style IIFEs, and any other IIFE shape.
    Never evals.
    """
    fn_params, body, j = _js_parse_iife_fn_inner(
        text, j, depth, state, _iife_ext_ok)
    # The immediate zero-arg invocation.
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, "(")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    j = _js_expect(text, j, ")")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the IIFE layer is obfuscation-shaped
    return fn_params, body, j


def _js_parse_fn_target(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse `(p1, p2) => BODY` or `function(p1, p2){return BODY}` at
    text[j] (F519).

    Returns (params, body_operands, next_index). The arrow form
    requires the parenthesized parameter list (a bare `p => BODY`
    fails closed, documented); the function form allows an optional
    name and an optional trailing `;` (both engine-exact, node
    v24.20.0). `function*` (generator) and `async` fail closed -- the
    target peek never routes them here. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if j < n and text[j] == "(":
        params, j = _js_parse_fn_params(text, j)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if not text.startswith("=>", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 arrow without =>")
        body, j = _js_parse_fn_additive_body(text, j + 2, params, state)
        return params, body, j
    if text.startswith("function", j):
        k = j + len("function")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 functionx")
        if k < n and text[k] == "*":
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 generator target")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_START:
            # Optional name (engine-exact; ignored).
            _, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        params, j = _js_parse_fn_params(text, j)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n or text[j] != "{":
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 function without brace")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("return", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 function without return")
        k = j + len("return")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 returnx")
        body, j = _js_parse_fn_additive_body(text, k, params, state)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j < n and text[j] == ";":
            j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if j >= n or text[j] != "}":
            raise _JSExprFail("F519 function without close brace")
        return params, body, j + 1
    raise _JSExprFail("F519 target not a function")


def _js_parse_f544_spread_apply_args(text, j, state):
    """Parse `...['lit', ...]` as the SOLE element of a Reflect.apply
    argsArray at text[j] (F544); text[j] is the first dot of `...`.

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): in
    `Reflect.apply(FN, thisArg, [...['f1', 'f2']])` the spread expands
    into the argsArray, so the spread array's elements ARE the
    arguments -- an empty spread is exactly no arguments. The array
    is parsed with the same bounded string-literal-only grammar as
    literal args (`_js_parse_string_literal_list`; parts-budget
    charged per element, plus one part for the spread itself); the
    array is never evaluated. Mirrors `_js_parse_f531_spread_bound_args`
    (F531), which covers the .bind-arguments position; this covers the
    apply-argsArray position (a different position per the F544
    distinctness rationale).

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - the spread source must be an array literal; any other source
      (`...x`, `...{'0':'f1'}`, a spread of a spread) fails closed.
    - the spread must be the SOLE element of the argsArray: this
      function consumes the argsArray's closing `]` -- multiple
      spreads, mid-list spreads, and literal+spread mixes fail closed
      (node accepts them; the lane rejects rather than mis-decodes --
      documented scope boundary).
    Returns (elements, idx_after_close_bracket). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if not text.startswith("...", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("F544 spread without ellipsis")
    state["parts"] += 1  # the spread is obfuscation-shaped
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 3)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("F544 spread source not an array literal")
    elements, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j + 1, state, "]")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "]":
        raise _JSExprFail("F544 spread not the sole argsArray element")
    return elements, j + 1


def _js_parse_f519_apply_args_array(text, j, state):
    """Parse a Reflect.apply argsArray's element list at text[j] --
    text[j] is just after the argsArray's opening `[` (F519/F544).

    The literal list (`['f1', 'f2']`, F519) or a single spread of a
    string-literal-only array literal (`[...['f1', 'f2']]`, F544).
    Returns (elements, idx_after_close_bracket). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text.startswith("...", j):
        return _js_parse_f544_spread_apply_args(text, j, state)
    elements, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j, state, "]")
    return elements, j
def _js_parse_f546_aliasadd_body(text, j, alias, state):
    """Parse `return a[K](+ a[K])*;` at text[j] (F546).

    The bare-alias additive body over the arguments alias: each
    operand is exactly `ALIAS[INT]` with a canonical decimal-int
    literal (no leading zeros except "0", no sign); whitespace
    tolerated. Returns (operand_indices, next_index). The indices
    are bounds-checked against the argsArray length by the caller
    (node coerces out-of-range to `undefined` -- conservative
    fail-closed, the F519-arity-mismatch precedent). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    rk = _js_match_kw(text, j, "return")
    if rk is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 aliasadd without return")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, rk)
    ops = []
    while True:
        a2, k = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        if a2 != alias:
            raise _JSExprFail("F546 aliasadd operand not the alias")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        k = _js_expect(text, k, "[")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        d0 = k
        while k < n and text[k] in "0123456789":
            k += 1
        keytok = text[d0:k]
        if not keytok or (len(keytok) > 1 and keytok[0] == "0"):
            raise _JSExprFail("F546 aliasadd non-canonical index")
        ops.append(int(keytok))
        state["parts"] += 1
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        k = _js_expect(text, k, "]")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k < n and text[k] == "+":
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
            j = k
            continue
        if k < n and text[k] == ";":
            k += 1
        if _js_skip_ws(text, k) != len(text):
            raise _JSExprFail("F546 aliasadd trailing statements")
        return ops, k


def _js_parse_f568_entries_values_body(text, j, alias, state):
    """Parse `return Object.entries(a).map(e=>e[1]).join(SEP);` /
    `return Object.values(a)(.map(x=>x))?.join(SEP);` at text[j] (F568).

    The F546xF543 composition: an arguments-enumerating body in a
    function-valued Reflect.apply target using the entries/values
    idioms (F543's IIFE-dispatch grammar, reused verbatim). The
    entries pairs / values array of the arguments exotic are
    element-identical to the argument array in ascending index order
    (node v24.20.0 verified), so the result is ``SEP.join(elements)``
    with a node-exact string-literal separator. Returns (kind, sep,
    next_index) where kind is "entries" or "values". The caller
    applies the join over the argsArray elements. Never evals.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - entries without the `e[1]` pair projection, a values body
      without `.join`, a non-literal join separator, `.join()` with
      no separator (node inserts "," -- the lane models only the
      literal-sep form, documented), non-identity maps, and any
      other body shape.
    """
    n = len(text)
    rk = _js_match_kw(text, j, "return")
    if rk is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F568 body not return")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, rk)
    member, q = _js_parse_f543_entries_values_head(text, j, alias)
    q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
    if member == "entries":
        q = _js_expect(text, q, ".")
        mm = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, q), "map")
        if mm is None:
            raise _JSExprFail("F568 entries without map")
        q = _js_expect(text, _js_skip_ws(text, mm), "(")
        q = _js_parse_f543_pair_projection(text, q)
        q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
        q = _js_expect(text, q, ")")
    else:
        # Optional identity map over the values array (a no-op
        # desugar -- the values are already element-identical to
        # the argument array).
        qq = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
        if qq < n and text[qq] == ".":
            mm = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, qq + 1), "map")
            if mm is not None:
                mq = _js_expect(text, _js_skip_ws(text, mm), "(")
                mq = _js_parse_f543_identity_map(text, mq)
                mq = _js_skip_ws(text, mq)
                mq = _js_expect(text, mq, ")")
                q = mq
    q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
    q = _js_expect(text, q, ".")
    mm = _js_match_kw(text, _js_skip_ws(text, q), "join")
    if mm is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F568 idiom without join")
    q = _js_expect(text, _js_skip_ws(text, mm), "(")
    q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
    if q >= n or text[q] not in "'\"":
        raise _JSExprFail("F568 join separator not a literal")
    sep, q = _js_parse_string(text, q)
    q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
    q = _js_expect(text, q, ")")
    q = _js_skip_ws(text, q)
    if q < n and text[q] == ";":
        q += 1
    if _js_skip_ws(text, q) != len(text):
        raise _JSExprFail("F568 trailing statements")
    state["parts"] += 1  # the idiom body is obfuscation-shaped
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    return member, sep, q


def _js_parse_fn_enum_body_target(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse `function(){DECL a=arguments; BODY}` at text[j] (F546).

    A function-valued Reflect.apply target whose body enumerates
    the arguments exotic object (the F519 x F529/F533 composition):
    `var|const|let a=arguments;` followed by one of
    - the F533 for-of accumulation idiom
      (`var|let s='';for(var|let|const x of a){s+=x;}return s;`),
    - the F529 for-in accumulation idiom
      (`var s='';for(var k in a){s+=a[k];}return s;`),
    - the bare-alias additive body (`return a[0]+a[1];`), or
    - the F568 entries/values idioms
      (`return Object.entries(a).map(e=>e[1]).join(SEP);` /
      `return Object.values(a)(.map(x=>x))?.join(SEP);`).
    The target must be a `function` expression (not an arrow -- the
    arrow's body grammar is F519's additive chain, unchanged) with
    an EMPTY parameter list (declared params alongside the
    arguments-exotic enumeration change the engine binding --
    fail closed, documented), anonymous-or-named (the name is
    ignored). Returns (kind, operand_indices, next_index) where kind
    is "forof", "forin", "aliasadd", "entries", or "values"; payload is
    the index list for "aliasadd", the join separator string for
    "entries"/"values" (F568), and None for "forof"/"forin". Never
    evals.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - `function*` (generator), async, rebound aliases, bare
      `arguments` (not the alias) as the enumeration source,
      destructuring loop targets, non-empty accumulator
      initializers, `const` accumulators, loop bodies other than
      the exact idioms, and any other body shape.
    - a leading directive prologue (`"use strict";` / `'use
      strict';`, `;`- or ASI-terminated) is SKIPPED node-exactly
      (F570): the engine treats it as inert, so the decl match
      starts after it. Directives mid-body are never skipped.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if not text.startswith("function", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 target not a function")
    k = j + len("function")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 functionx")
    if k < n and text[k] == "*":
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 generator target")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_START:
        # Optional name (engine-exact; ignored).
        _, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    params, j = _js_parse_fn_params(text, j)
    if params:
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 target has declared params")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "{":
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 target without brace")
    inner_start, inner_end, j = _js_span_braced(text, j)
    stmts = text[inner_start:inner_end]
    # F570: a directive prologue ("use strict"; ...) may lead the
    # body -- the engine treats it as inert, so the skip is
    # node-exact; with no directives this is exactly
    # _js_skip_ws(stmts, 0) (the helper starts there), so the
    # no-prologue behavior is unchanged. Each skipped directive
    # charges one part inside the helper (F452 discipline).
    sj = _js_skip_ws(stmts, _js_skip_directive_prologue(stmts, state))
    _kw, m = _js_match_decl_kw(stmts, sj)
    if m is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 decl not var/const/let")
    sj = _js_skip_ws(stmts, m)
    alias, sj = _js_parse_ident(stmts, sj)
    if alias == "arguments":
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 alias named arguments")
    sj = _js_skip_ws(stmts, sj)
    sj = _js_expect(stmts, sj, "=")
    m = _js_match_kw(stmts, _js_skip_ws(stmts, sj), "arguments")
    if m is None:
        raise _JSExprFail("F546 source not arguments")
    sj = _js_skip_ws(stmts, m)
    sj = _js_expect(stmts, sj, ";")
    rest = stmts[sj:]
    rj = _js_skip_ws(rest, 0)
    rk = _js_match_kw(rest, rj, "return")
    if rk is not None:
        # F568: the entries/values idiom bodies (the F546xF543
        # composition). Tried before the aliasadd body: disjoint
        # (the idioms call Object.entries/values on the alias; the
        # aliasadd body indexes `a[K]` directly, so each rejects
        # the other's shape chargeless).
        try:
            member, sep, _ = _js_parse_f568_entries_values_body(
                rest, rj, alias, state)
            return member, sep, j
        except _JSExprFail:
            pass
        # The bare-alias additive body.
        ops, _ = _js_parse_f546_aliasadd_body(rest, rj, alias, state)
        return "aliasadd", ops, j
    # The accumulation idioms, reused verbatim from F533/F529.
    if _js_match_kw(rest, rj, "var") is not None or \
            _js_match_kw(rest, rj, "let") is not None:
        try:
            _js_parse_f533_forof_accum(rest, rj, alias)
            return "forof", None, j
        except _JSExprFail:
            pass
        try:
            _js_parse_f529_forin_accum(rest, rj, alias)
            return "forin", None, j
        except _JSExprFail:
            pass
    raise _JSExprFail("F546 body not an enumerating idiom")


def _js_parse_fn_destructure_body_target(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse `function(){DECL PAT=arguments;return BODY;}` at text[j] (F558).

    A function-valued Reflect.apply target whose body destructures
    the arguments exotic object (the F519 x F524/F545 composition):
    `var|const|let [a,b]=arguments` (array pattern, F524) or
    `var|const|let {K0:n0(,K1:n1)?}=arguments` (object pattern, F545)
    followed by a single `return EXPR;`. The target must be a
    `function` expression with an EMPTY parameter list (declared
    params alongside the arguments-exotic destructure change the
    engine binding -- fail closed, documented), anonymous-or-named
    (the name is ignored). Returns (names, keys, body_expr,
    next_index). The caller binds the names to the argsArray
    elements via `_js_bind_destructure_arguments` and parses the
    rewritten body with the additive grammar. Never evals.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded): the
    full `_js_split_destructure_var_decl` scope boundary applies
    verbatim (1-2 element patterns of plain identifiers, canonical
    decimal-int object keys, bare `arguments` source, no holes /
    defaults / rest / nested patterns / duplicate names or keys, no
    `arguments` as a bound name), plus `function*` generators,
    non-empty params, and any other body shape. The enum-body
    parser rejects these inputs chargeless (its decl parse fails on
    the pattern bracket), so the two target classes are disjoint.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if not text.startswith("function", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("F558 target not a function")
    k = j + len("function")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("F558 functionx")
    if k < n and text[k] == "*":
        raise _JSExprFail("F558 generator target")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_START:
        # Optional name (engine-exact; ignored).
        _, j = _js_parse_ident(text, j)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    params, j = _js_parse_fn_params(text, j)
    if params:
        raise _JSExprFail("F558 target has declared params")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "{":
        raise _JSExprFail("F558 target without brace")
    inner_start, inner_end, j = _js_span_braced(text, j)
    stmts = text[inner_start:inner_end]
    # Directive prologues are inert in the engine (F570 mechanism,
    # generalized here per the F560 precedent): skip before the
    # destructure declaration. With no directives this is exactly
    # _js_skip_ws(stmts, 0); each skipped directive charges one part
    # inside the helper (F452 discipline).
    sj = _js_skip_ws(stmts, _js_skip_directive_prologue(stmts, state))
    names, keys, body_expr = _js_split_destructure_var_decl(stmts[sj:])
    state["parts"] += 1  # the destructure body is obfuscation-shaped
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    return names, keys, body_expr, j


def _js_apply_enum_body_result(kind, payload, elements):
    """Desugar an F546/F568 enum-body target over *elements* (F546/F568/F569).

    The arguments exotic is the argsArray elements: for-of/for-in
    accumulation and the entries/values idioms (element-identical to
    the argument array, node v24.20.0) yield the joined elements;
    the bare-alias additive body yields the indexed elements in
    operand order (indices bounds-checked against the elements
    length -- node coerces out-of-range to `undefined`, conservative
    fail-closed, the F519-arity-mismatch precedent). Never evals.
    """
    if kind in ("forof", "forin"):
        return "".join(elements)
    if kind in ("entries", "values"):
        return payload.join(elements)
    for k in payload:
        if k >= len(elements):
            raise _JSExprFail("F546 aliasadd index out of range")
    return "".join(elements[k] for k in payload)


def _js_apply_destructure_body_result(names, keys, body_expr, elements,
                                      depth, state):
    """Desugar an F558 destructure-body target over *elements* (F558/F569).

    Each pattern key is bounds-checked against the elements length
    (node coerces out-of-range to `undefined` -- conservative
    fail-closed). The names are bound to the elements via
    `_js_bind_destructure_arguments` and the rewritten body is
    parsed by the additive grammar at depth+1, so every downstream
    decoder, bound, and fail-closed rule applies unchanged. The
    elements are re-quoted as single-quoted string literals for the
    substitution (`\\` and `'` escaped -- `_js_parse_string`
    decodes them back losslessly, node-exact round-trip). Never
    evals.
    """
    for k in keys:
        if k >= len(elements):
            raise _JSExprFail("F558 destructure key beyond argsArray")
    raw = ["'" + v.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("'", "\\'") + "'"
           for v in elements]
    rewritten = _js_bind_destructure_arguments(body_expr, raw, names, keys)
    if depth >= _JS_EXPR_MAX_DEPTH:
        raise _JSExprFail("too deep")
    val, k = _js_parse_additive(rewritten, 0, depth + 1, state)
    k = _js_skip_ws(rewritten, k)
    if k != len(rewritten):
        raise _JSExprFail("F558 destructure body trailing text")
    if not isinstance(val, str):
        raise _JSExprFail("F558 destructure body not a string")
    return val


def _js_f582_truncate_arity(elements, params, label):
    """Node-exact arity for F519-class additive targets (F582, v24.20.0).

    The engine fills params positionally and IGNORES extras; fewer args
    than params fail closed (node coerces missing params to undefined --
    conservative). Body operands are pure param indices, so extras can
    never enter the result. Returns elements truncated to the parameter
    count. Never evals.
    """
    if len(elements) < len(params):
        raise _JSExprFail(label)
    return elements[:len(params)]


def _js_parse_reflect_apply_fn(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse 'Reflect.apply(FN, thisArg, argsArray)' at text[i] (F519).

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): Reflect.apply invokes FN with
    thisArg and the argsArray elements as arguments. FN is a
    statically-known function value -- an arrow `(p1, p2) => BODY` or
    a function expression `function(p1, p2){return BODY}` -- whose
    body is exactly an additive chain over its declared parameters
    (F519); or a function expression with an arguments-enumerating
    body (F546: for-of/for-in/bare-alias idioms; F568 adds the
    entries/values idioms); or a function expression with a
    destructuring-pattern body over `arguments` (F558); or an
    IIFE-evaluated target (F522). With an all-string argsArray the
    result is the parameter-ordered concatenation (the `+` operands
    are all strings, so no coercion surprises). The member name accepts the
    dot form and the F504 bracket-string forms (routed here by
    _js_parse_reflect_call, which already verified the name;
    re-verified defense-in-depth).

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - thisArg is parsed and discarded: the modeled bodies reference
      only their parameters, so `this` is unobservable
      (node-verified) -- the parse keeps the grammar honest (F483
      precedent).
    - argsArray must be a string-literal-only list
      (_js_parse_string_literal_list), or a single spread of one
      (F544, `[...['f1','f2']]` -- node-exact, engine-identical to
      the literal form); extras beyond the parameter count are dropped
      engine-identically (F582); fewer args than params fail closed
      (node coerces missing params to undefined -- conservative
      fail-closed, documented).
    Returns (result_string, next_index) without the postfix chain
    (the caller applies it). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = i + len("Reflect")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflectx is not Reflect")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        _mname, j = _js_parse_reflect_member_key(text, j, depth, state)
        if _mname != "apply":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect bracket member not apply")
    else:
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without dot")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("apply", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without apply")
        k = j + len("apply")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("applyx is not apply")
        j = k
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    _target_mode = "additive"
    _enum_kind = None
    _enum_payload = None
    _dest = None
    try:
        params, body, j = _js_parse_iife_fn_target(text, j, depth, state)
    except _JSExprFail:
        # Not an IIFE-evaluated target (F522) -- fall through to the
        # literal arrow/fn-expr target (F519). The IIFE parse only
        # succeeds where the literal parse fails (it requires the
        # trailing `()` invocation), so no previously-decoded input
        # changes behavior.
        try:
            params, body, j = _js_parse_fn_target(text, j, depth, state)
        except _JSExprFail:
            # F546: a function-valued target whose body enumerates
            # the arguments exotic object (the F519 x F529/F533
            # composition). The F519 additive grammar rejects these
            # bodies chargeless (its operand parse fails on the
            # `var` keyword), so this path only accepts inputs that
            # previously failed closed -- no previously-decoded
            # input changes behavior.
            try:
                _enum_kind, _enum_payload, j = _js_parse_fn_enum_body_target(
                    text, j, depth, state)
                _target_mode = "enum"
                params = []
            except _JSExprFail:
                # F558: a function-valued target whose body
                # destructures the arguments exotic object (the F519
                # x F524/F545 composition). The enum-body parser
                # rejects these inputs chargeless (its decl parse
                # fails on the pattern bracket), so this path only
                # accepts inputs that previously failed closed -- no
                # previously-decoded input changes behavior.
                _dest_names, _dest_keys, _dest_body, j = \
                    _js_parse_fn_destructure_body_target(
                        text, j, depth, state)
                _dest = (_dest_names, _dest_keys, _dest_body)
                _target_mode = "destructure"
                params = []
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("F519 target without thisArg")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    _, j = _js_parse_ignored_value(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("F519 thisArg without argsArray")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("F519 argsArray not an array")
    # F544+F546 composition (wave-368 merge): the argsArray grammar
    # admits the F544 spread form; enum bodies (F546) read the expanded
    # elements.
    elements, j = _js_parse_f519_apply_args_array(text, j + 1, state)
    if _target_mode == "enum":
        # F546/F568: the arguments exotic is the argsArray elements.
        result = _js_apply_enum_body_result(_enum_kind, _enum_payload,
                                            elements)
    elif _target_mode == "destructure":
        # F558: the pattern names are bound to the argsArray elements.
        result = _js_apply_destructure_body_result(
            _dest[0], _dest[1], _dest[2], elements, depth, state)
    else:
        elements = _js_f582_truncate_arity(
            elements, params, "F519 fewer args than params")
        result = "".join(elements[idx] for idx in body)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("F519 with extra args")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the function-valued dispatch is obfuscation-shaped
    return result, j + 1


def _js_parse_f531_spread_bound_args(text, j, state):
    """Parse `...['lit', ...]` / `...'lit'` as the bound-arg list at text[j]
    (F531 / F559).

    text[j] is the first dot of the `...` spread. Node-exact desugar
    (v24.20.0): in `[].concat.bind(BINDTHIS, ...['f1', 'f2'])` the
    spread expands into bind's argument list, so the spread array's
    elements ARE the bound args -- an empty spread is exactly no bound
    args. The array is parsed with the same bounded string-literal-only
    grammar as literal bound args (`_js_parse_string_literal_list`;
    parts-budget charged per element, plus one part for the spread
    itself), so the desugar is faithful to the direct form. The array
    is never evaluated.

    F559: a string literal spread (`... 'f1'`) expands to the string's
    characters as the bound args (node-verified); the empty string
    binds nothing. Parts charged per character (F452 lesson).

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - the spread source must be an array literal (F531) or a string
      literal (F559 -- expanded to its characters); any other source
      (`...x`, `...{'0':'f1'}`, template/tagged forms) fails closed.
    - the spread must be the SOLE and FINAL bound arg: the caller has
      just seen `,` after BINDTHIS, and this function consumes `)` --
      multiple spreads, mid-list spreads, and literal+spread mixes
      fail closed (node accepts them; the lane rejects rather than
      mis-decodes -- documented scope boundary).
    Returns (elements, idx_after_close_paren). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if not text.startswith("...", j):
        raise _JSExprFail("F531 spread without ellipsis")
    state["parts"] += 1  # the spread is obfuscation-shaped
    if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
        raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 3)
    if j >= n:
        raise _JSExprFail("F531 spread without source")
    if text[j] == "[":
        elements, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j + 1, state,
                                                     "]")
    elif text[j] in "'\"":
        # F559: string-literal spread -- the engine expands the string
        # to its characters, which become the bound args
        # (node v24.20.0: [].concat.bind([],...'f1') binds 'f','1';
        # the empty string binds nothing). Backtick template sources
        # fail closed in _js_parse_string. The parts budget is charged
        # per character (F452 lesson): a huge literal cannot buy
        # unbounded downstream work -- the F531 array path charges
        # per element the same way.
        frag, j = _js_parse_string(text, j)
        state["parts"] += len(frag)
        if state["parts"] > _JS_EXPR_MAX_PARTS:
            raise _JSExprFail("too many parts")
        elements = list(frag)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("F559 spread source not an array/string "
                          "literal")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("F531 spread not the final bound arg")
    return elements, j + 1


def _js_parse_f521_bound_target(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse a `.bind`-bound concat function value at text[j] (F521).

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): `[].concat.bind(BINDTHIS, ...ARGS)`
    (or `Array.prototype.concat.bind(BINDTHIS, ...ARGS)` -- node:
    `[].concat === Array.prototype.concat`) is the concat function
    with its this-value fixed to BINDTHIS and ARGS pre-bound; invoking
    it with call-args C is exactly `BINDTHIS.concat(...ARGS, ...C)`.
    Returns (bind_this_elements, bound_args, next_index) where
    next_index is just after the bind call's closing paren. Never evals.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - the bound function must be a concat reference (`[].concat` or
      `Array.prototype.concat`, ws-tolerant, ident-boundary-checked);
      `.bind` on any other function fails closed -- this is the filed
      F521 scope (concat-family bound targets only). A bound JOIN
      target (`[].join.bind(['f1','f2'], '')`) node-evaluates to the
      credential through a different builtin and is filed separately
      as F530 (F500/F505 different-builtins precedent), not silently
      dropped.
    - the `.bind` member is the dot form, the F490 `?.` form, or the
      F482 bracket-string form (all engine-identical, node v24.20.0
      verified); the inner `concat` member is dot-only (F506 precedent).
    - BINDTHIS is an array literal or Array-constructor form with
      all-string elements (the F506 thisArg classes); anything else
      fails closed.
    - every bound ARG must be a string literal (F454 grammar);
      numeric, template, and additive args fail closed. The one
      exception is the spread form: a single spread of a literal
      array as the SOLE bound arg (`.bind(BINDTHIS, ...['f1'])`),
      modeled node-exactly by `_js_parse_f531_spread_bound_args`
      (wave-367 lane 1), extended by F559 (wave-369 lane 3) to a
      single spread of a string literal whose characters become the
      bound args (`.bind(BINDTHIS, ...'f1')`); multiple spreads,
      mid-list spreads, literal+spread mixes, and non-literal
      spread sources all fail closed.
    - a third `.bind` on the two-hop chain (three-hop chain) fails
      closed structurally -- the triple caller expects `,` after the
      bound target (documented residual).
    """
    n = len(text)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # F504-class receiver: `[].concat`.
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if k >= n or text[k] != "]":
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 bound target non-empty receiver")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 bound target without member dot")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if not text.startswith("concat", k):
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 bound target non-concat")
        k2 = k + len("concat")
        if k2 < n and text[k2] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 concatx is not concat")
        j = k2
    elif text.startswith("Array", j):
        # F469-class receiver: `Array.prototype.concat`.
        k = j + len("Array")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 Arrayx is not Array")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 Array without dot")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if not text.startswith("prototype", k):
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 Array non-prototype")
        k2 = k + len("prototype")
        if k2 < n and text[k2] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 prototypex")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k2)
        if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 prototype without member dot")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if not text.startswith("concat", k):
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 Array non-concat")
        k2 = k + len("concat")
        if k2 < n and text[k2] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 concatx is not concat")
        j = k2
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 bound target not a concat reference")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    _bd = _js_peek_dispatch_call(text, j)
    if _bd is None or _bd[0] != "bind":
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 target not .bind-bound")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, _bd[1] + 1)
    # BINDTHIS: array literal or Array-constructor, all-string
    # elements (the F506 thisArg classes, engine-identical).
    if k < n and text[k] == "[":
        bind_this, k = _js_parse_array(text, k, depth, state)
    elif text.startswith("Array", k):
        bind_this, k = _js_parse_array_constructor(text, k, state)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 bind this not an array")
    if not isinstance(bind_this, _JSArr):
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 bind this not an array")
    if any(not isinstance(e, str) for e in bind_this.elements):
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 bind this non-string element")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k < n and text[k] == ")":
        bound_args, k = [], k + 1
    else:
        if k >= n or text[k] != ",":
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 bind without args")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        # F531: a single spread of a literal array as the bound-arg
        # list, e.g. `[].concat.bind([], ...['f1'])`. The spread must
        # be the sole and final bound arg (parsed by
        # _js_parse_f531_spread_bound_args); anything else falls back
        # to the literal-arg grammar below.
        if k < n and text.startswith("...", k):
            bound_args, k = _js_parse_f531_spread_bound_args(text, k,
                                                             state)
        else:
            bound_args, k = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, k, state,
                                                          ")")
    # F526: exactly one additional `.bind` hop (a two-hop chain), e.g.
    # `[].concat.bind([],'f1').bind([])`. Node-exact (v24.20.0):
    # B2 = B1.bind(BINDTHIS2, ...ARGS2) where B1 is itself
    # .bind-bound -- B2's [[BoundThis]] is discarded when the call
    # reaches B1 (a bound function ignores its receiver), so the FIRST
    # hop's BINDTHIS wins; args accumulate hop1-then-hop2-then-call.
    # Node-verified: [].concat.bind(['T1'],'f1').bind(['T2']) invoked
    # => the 'T1' array receives the args. BINDTHIS2 is parsed with the
    # same bounded array grammar (fail closed) and its value discarded.
    # The hop member accepts the same dot / F490 `?.` / F482
    # bracket-string forms as hop one (via _js_peek_dispatch_call).
    # A third `.bind` hop fails closed structurally -- the triple
    # caller expects `,` after the bound target. Never evals.
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    _hop = _js_peek_dispatch_call(text, k)
    if _hop is not None and _hop[0] == "bind":
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, _hop[1] + 1)
        if k < n and text[k] == "[":
            _b2, k = _js_parse_array(text, k, depth, state)
        elif text.startswith("Array", k):
            _b2, k = _js_parse_array_constructor(text, k, state)
        else:
            raise _JSExprFail("F526 second bind this not an array")
        if not isinstance(_b2, _JSArr):
            raise _JSExprFail("F526 second bind this not an array")
        if any(not isinstance(e, str) for e in _b2.elements):
            raise _JSExprFail("F526 second bind this non-string element")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k < n and text[k] == ")":
            k = k + 1
        else:
            if k >= n or text[k] != ",":
                raise _JSExprFail("F526 second bind without args")
            _args2, k = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, k + 1, state,
                                                      ")")
            bound_args = bound_args + _args2
    return list(bind_this.elements), bound_args, k


def _js_parse_f521_bound_triple(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse the F521 `BOUND, thisArg, argsList` triple at text[j].

    BOUND is the `.bind`-bound concat function value
    (_js_parse_f521_bound_target); thisArg is parsed and discarded --
    the bound function's this is fixed by `.bind`, so Reflect.apply's
    thisArg is ignored by the engine (node v24.20.0 verified, F483
    precedent); argsList is a string-literal-only list (the F519
    grammar). Returns (elements, next_index) where elements is the
    desugared BINDTHIS.concat(...BOUNDARGS, ...ARGSLIST) element list
    and next_index is just after argsList (the caller checks the
    closing paren -- the _js_parse_f506_triple contract). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    bind_this, bound_args, j = _js_parse_f521_bound_target(text, j,
                                                           depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 bound target without thisArg")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    _, j = _js_parse_ignored_value(text, j, depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 thisArg without argsList")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "[":
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 argsList not an array")
    args, j = _js_parse_string_literal_list(text, j + 1, state, "]")
    return bind_this + bound_args + args, j


def _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse 'Reflect.apply(BOUND, thisArg, argsArray)' at text[i] (F521).

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): Reflect.apply invokes the
    `.bind`-bound concat function with thisArg and the argsArray
    elements as arguments; the bound this-value and pre-bound args
    win, so the result is the parameter-ordered concatenation
    BINDTHIS.concat(...BOUNDARGS, ...ARGSLIST) -- all elements are
    string literals, so no coercion surprises. The member name
    accepts the dot form and the F504 bracket-string forms (routed
    here by _js_parse_reflect_call, which already verified the name;
    re-verified defense-in-depth). Returns (value, next_index)
    without the postfix chain (the caller applies it). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = i + len("Reflect")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflectx is not Reflect")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        _mname, j = _js_parse_reflect_member_key(text, j, depth, state)
        if _mname != "apply":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect bracket member not apply")
    else:
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without dot")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("apply", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without apply")
        k = j + len("apply")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("applyx is not apply")
        j = k
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.apply without paren")
    elements, j = _js_parse_f521_bound_triple(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1),
                                              depth, state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 with extra args")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the bound-target dispatch is obfuscation-shaped
    return _JSArr(elements), j + 1


def _js_parse_reflect_get_target(text, j):
    """Parse the TARGET of `Reflect.get(TARGET, KEY)` (F491).

    Returns ((kind, tname), next_index) where kind is "proto" (with
    tname "array"/"string") or "ctor". Only the filed target classes
    are modeled: `Array.prototype` / `String.prototype` (textual,
    ws-tolerant, ident-boundary-checked) and the Array constructor
    reference (`Array` / `[].constructor`, via
    _js_parse_array_ctor_ref). Anything else -- `Math`, bracket
    targets (`Array['prototype']`), non-empty receivers -- fails
    closed (documented scope boundary). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    for tname, kind in (("Array", "array"), ("String", "string")):
        if text.startswith(tname, j):
            k = j + len(tname)
            if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                break  # Arrayx / Stringx -- not a target
            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
            if k < n and text[k] == ".":
                k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
                if text.startswith("prototype", k):
                    k2 = k + len("prototype")
                    if k2 >= n or text[k2] not in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
                        return ("proto", kind), k2
            break
    try:
        k = _js_parse_array_ctor_ref(text, j)
    except _JSExprFail:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.get target not modeled")
    return ("ctor", None), k


def _js_desugar_indirect_member(kind, tname, key, text, j, depth, state):
    """Desugar a resolved `TARGET.KEY` indirection (F491).

    *kind* is "proto" (tname "array"/"string") or "ctor"; *key* is the
    folded member name; text[j] is just past the indirection's closing
    paren (and `.value` for the descriptor form). The resolved
    reference is engine-identical to the direct member access
    (node-verified, v24.20.0): the parse continues as if the text
    were the canonical direct form, so every downstream grammar,
    bound, and fail-closed behavior is shared with it -- faithful by
    construction, no F481-class composition gap. Index mapping back
    to the original text is positional (the suffix is verbatim).

    Returns (value, next_index) with the postfix chain applied, or
    None when the text after the indirection is not a modeled call
    (the caller falls through; the bare indirection is not a
    decodable shape). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    state["ops"] += 1  # the indirection is obfuscation-shaped
    if kind == "ctor":
        if key != "of":
            # Engine-exact: `Reflect.get(Array,'from')` resolves, but
            # calling `from` with string args throws -- same boundary
            # as F476's direct form. Never mis-decoded.
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.get ctor key not of")
        canon = "Array.of"
        synth = canon + text[j:]
        sval, send = _js_parse_array_constructor(synth, 0, state)
        if send < len(canon):
            raise _JSExprFail("bad ctor desugar")
        return _js_parse_postfix(text, j + (send - len(canon)), sval,
                                 depth, state)
    table = _F478_ARRAY_METHODS if tname == "array" else _F478_STRING_METHODS
    if key not in table:
        # Engine-exact: the resolved reference is undefined
        # (`Array.prototype.replace`), so any call throws in node --
        # not a decodable shape. Same validation as
        # _js_parse_prototype_dispatch. Never mis-decoded.
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.get key not a modeled method")
    canon = ("Array.prototype." if tname == "array"
             else "String.prototype.") + key
    synth = canon + text[j:]
    r = _js_parse_prototype_dispatch(synth, 0, depth, state)
    if r is None:
        return None
    sval, send = r
    return sval, j + (send - len(canon))


def _js_parse_reflect_get(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `Reflect.get(TARGET, KEY)` / `Reflect['get'](TARGET, KEY)`
    at text[i] (F491).

    Desugars the indirection to the direct member reference:
    `Reflect.get(Array.prototype,'concat')` is engine-identical to
    `Array.prototype.concat`; `Reflect.get(Array,'of')` is
    engine-identical to `Array.of` (node v24.20.0 verified). KEY may
    be a string literal or a constant-folded computed key (the
    F491xF492 composition, via _js_fold_key_expr).

    Returns (value, next_index), or None when text[i] is not a
    `Reflect.get` shape (the caller falls through to
    _js_parse_reflect_call, which fails closed on it). Once the `get`
    member commits the parse, malformed shapes fail closed via
    _JSExprFail. Descriptor objects with getters/setters cannot
    arise: only the filed (TARGET, KEY) data-descriptor pairs are
    modeled. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if i > 0 and text[i - 1] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        return None
    j = i + len("Reflect")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        return None
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        try:
            mname, j = _js_parse_bracket_member(text, j)
        except _JSExprFail:
            return None
        if mname != "get":
            return None
    else:
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            return None
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("get", j):
            return None
        k = j + len("get")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            return None  # getx / getOwnPropertyDescriptor -- not get
        j = k
    # The `get` member commits the parse: malformed shapes fail closed.
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.get without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    try:
        (kind, tname), j = _js_parse_reflect_get_target(text, j)
    except _JSExprFail:
        # F540: `Reflect.get(OBJLIT, KEY)` -- object-literal target
        # desugared to the F535 member read. The `{` commits the
        # parse; anything else re-raises (fails closed as before).
        if j >= n or text[j] != "{":
            raise
        return _js_parse_f540_reflect_get_object(text, j, depth,
                                                 state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.get without key")
    key, j = _js_fold_key_expr(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1), depth,
                              state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect.get unterminated")
    return _js_desugar_indirect_member(kind, tname, key, text, j + 1,
                                      depth, state)


def _js_parse_gopd_value(text, i, depth, state):
    """Parse `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(TARGET, KEY).value` at
    text[i] (F491).

    Desugars the descriptor indirection: `.value` on the filed
    (TARGET, KEY) pairs is engine-identical to the direct member
    reference (node v24.20.0 verified), so the parse continues via
    _js_desugar_indirect_member exactly as `Reflect.get` does.

    Returns (value, next_index), or None when text[i] is not this
    shape (the caller falls through). Once the
    `getOwnPropertyDescriptor` member commits the parse, malformed
    shapes fail closed via _JSExprFail. Only `.value` (dot or
    bracket-string) is modeled -- other descriptor fields fail
    closed. Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    if i > 0 and text[i - 1] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        return None
    j = i + len("Object")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        return None
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
        return None
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if not text.startswith("getOwnPropertyDescriptor", j):
        return None
    k = j + len("getOwnPropertyDescriptor")
    if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        return None
    j = k
    # The member commits the parse: malformed shapes fail closed.
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("gopd without paren")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    (kind, tname), j = _js_parse_reflect_get_target(text, j)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("gopd without key")
    key, j = _js_fold_key_expr(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1), depth,
                              state)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("gopd unterminated")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        vname, j = _js_parse_bracket_member(text, j)
        if vname != "value":
            raise _JSExprFail("gopd field not value")
    elif j < n and text[j] == ".":
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if not text.startswith("value", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("gopd field not value")
        k = j + len("value")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("valuex is not value")
        j = k
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("gopd without .value")
    return _js_desugar_indirect_member(kind, tname, key, text, j,
                                      depth, state)


def _js_parse_reflect_call(text, i, depth, state):
    """Dispatch Reflect.construct (F468) vs Reflect.apply (F469) at text[i].

    Both share the F455-class ws-tolerant anchor; the method name after
    the dot selects the parser. Anything else fails closed -- never
    mis-decoded.

    The prefix is re-parsed here even though both callees re-parse it
    from i themselves (intentional defense-in-depth: the dispatch's
    startswith-based method selection cannot mis-route).
    """
    n = len(text)
    if i > 0 and text[i - 1] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        # Engine-exact: `fooReflect.apply` is member access on
        # `fooReflect` in node (ReferenceError for the bare global),
        # never the global Reflect -- the _JS_EXPR_START anchor has no
        # left boundary, so guard here. (Spec review, wave-354 lane 1.)
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflect is a member suffix")
    j = i + len("Reflect")
    if j < n and text[j] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("Reflectx is not Reflect")
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
    if j < n and text[j] == "?" and j + 1 < n and text[j + 1] == ".":
        # F490: Reflect?.apply -- `?.` adjacent (engine SyntaxError
        # otherwise); Reflect is provably non-null so ?. ≡ . (The
        # callees re-parse from i and normalize the same way.)
        j += 1
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # F480: Reflect['apply'](...) -- bracket-string member access,
        # node-identical to the dot form. F498: Reflect['construct']
        # is modeled identically to the dot form (F468): literal keys
        # via _js_parse_bracket_member, constant-folded keys via
        # _js_fold_bracket_key (F492); F504: template keys fold through
        # _js_fold_bracket_key and nested-bracket keys (`[['apply'][0]]`)
        # through _js_parse_nested_bracket_key -- all engine-identical
        # to the literal form (node v24.20.0 verified). Any other
        # member fails closed (documented scope boundary -- rejected,
        # never mis-decoded).
        _mname, j = _js_parse_reflect_member_key(text, j, depth, state)
        if _mname == "construct":
            return _js_parse_reflect_construct(text, i, depth, state)
        if _mname != "apply":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect bracket member not modeled")
    else:
        if j >= n or text[j] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without dot")
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if text.startswith("construct", j):
            return _js_parse_reflect_construct(text, i, depth, state)
        if not text.startswith("apply", j):
            raise _JSExprFail("Reflect without construct/apply")
        k = j + len("apply")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("applyx is not apply")
        j = k
    # Member is 'apply' (dot or bracket form): the TARGET selects the
    # desugar. Peek past the target's head: Array.prototype... is the
    # F469 concat-application path; the bare Array constructor is the
    # F480 constructor-dispatch path; a `(`/`function` head is the
    # F519 function-valued-target path; a `[].concat.bind(` /
    # `Array.prototype.concat.bind(` head is the F521 bound-target
    # path; a `[].join.bind(` / `Array.prototype.join.bind(` head is
    # the F530 bound-join-target path; a `[].slice.bind(` /
    # `Array.prototype.slice.bind(` head is the F534 bound-slice-target
    # path; a `[].filter.bind(` / `Array.prototype.filter.bind(` head
    # is the F537 bound-filter-target path; a `[].map.bind(` /
    # `Array.prototype.map.bind(` head is the F538 bound-map-target
    # path; a `[].reduce.bind(` / `Array.prototype.reduce.bind(` head
    # is the F553 bound-reduce-target path; a `[].flatMap.bind(` /
    # `Array.prototype.flatMap.bind(` head is the F554
    # bound-flatMap-target path; a `[].find.bind(` /
    # `Array.prototype.find.bind(` head is the F555 bound-find-target
    # path; a `(FN).bind(` head is the F556 bound-arrow-target path;
    # a `new Proxy(` head is the F542 Proxy apply-trap target
    # path; an `Object.entries` / `Object.values` head is the F581
    # enumeration-builtin dispatch path. The callees re-parse from i
    # (defense-in-depth); a mis-route here always fails closed inside
    # the callee.
    # F513: Reflect.apply.bind(Reflect)(T, thisArg, argsList) -- the
    # bound form. F521: Reflect.apply.call(Reflect, BOUND, thisArg,
    # argsList) -- the call-indirect form. The dispatch-call peek is
    # pure lookahead (dot, ?., or bracket-string member per
    # F482/F490): anything but `.bind(`/`.call(` falls through to
    # the target peek below.
    _bpeek = _js_peek_dispatch_call(text, j)
    if _bpeek is not None and _bpeek[0] == "bind":
        return _js_parse_f513_reflect_bind(text, _bpeek[1], depth, state)
    if _bpeek is not None and _bpeek[0] == "call":
        return _js_parse_f521_call_dispatch(text, _bpeek[1], depth, state)
    _ptarget = _js_peek_reflect_apply_target(text, j)
    if _ptarget == "concat":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_concat(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "bound":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "boundjoin":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound_join(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "boundslice":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound_slice(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "boundfilter":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound_filter(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "boundmap":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound_map(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "boundreduce":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound_reduce(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "boundflatmap":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound_flatmap(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "boundfind":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound_find(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "boundarrow":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_bound_arrow(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "proxyapply":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_proxy(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "enumbapply":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_enumb(text, i, depth, state)
    if _ptarget == "fn":
        return _js_parse_reflect_apply_fn(text, i, depth, state)
    return _js_parse_reflect_apply_ctor(text, i, depth, state)


def _js_parse_f513_reflect_bind(text, paren, depth, state):
    """Parse `Reflect.apply.bind(Reflect)(T, thisArg, argsList)` at the
    `.bind(` paren (F513).

    *paren* is the index of the `(` opening the bind call. Node-exact
    desugar (v24.20.0): `Reflect.apply.bind(Reflect)` is a bound callable whose
    invocation with `(T, thisArg, argsList)` is exactly
    `Reflect.apply(T, thisArg, argsList)` -- the engine ignores
    Reflect.apply's this-value, so only the literal `Reflect`
    namespace is modeled as the bind receiver (anything else fails
    closed, conservative). The triple is parsed by _js_parse_f506_triple
    (the F469/F504 target and argument classes, engine-identical).

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded): bind with
    bound arguments (`Reflect.apply.bind(Reflect, X)` -- the bound args
    shift the triple's argument positions, so the static triple grammar
    no longer applies; whether the engine throws or evaluates depends
    on the resulting arity -- conservative either way); a bound
    function with no immediate `(` call (not a decodable value, F483
    precedent). Returns (value, next_index) without the postfix chain
    (the caller applies it, same as _js_parse_reflect_apply_concat):
    _JSArr for the F506/F521 concat paths, plain str for the F530
    bound-join path (join returns a string directly). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, paren + 1)
    if not text.startswith("Reflect", k):
        raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind receiver not Reflect")
    k2 = k + len("Reflect")
    if k2 < n and text[k2] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
        raise _JSExprFail("F513 Reflectx is not Reflect")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k2)
    if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind with bound args")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    if k >= n or text[k] != "(":
        raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind without immediate call")
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
    try:
        elements, k = _js_parse_f506_triple(text, k, depth, state)
    except _JSExprFail:
        # F521: the triple's target is a .bind-bound concat function
        # (e.g. `[].concat.bind([], 'f1')`) -- outside the F469/F504
        # target classes, so the F506 triple cannot parse it; try the
        # F521 bound-target triple (fails closed on mismatch). The
        # F506 raise commits nothing, so the fallback is clean.
        try:
            elements, k = _js_parse_f521_bound_triple(text, k, depth,
                                                      state)
        except _JSExprFail:
            # F530: the triple's target is a .bind-bound JOIN function
            # (e.g. `[].join.bind(['f1','f2'], '')`) -- outside the
            # F521 concat-only target class, so the F521 triple cannot
            # parse it; try the F530 bound-join triple (fails closed on
            # mismatch). The F521 raise commits nothing on join input
            # (it rejects at the non-concat member check before any
            # value parse), so the fallback is clean. Returns the
            # joined string, not an element list.
            try:
                joined, k = _js_parse_f530_bound_join_triple(text, k, depth,
                                                             state)
                k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                    raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                return joined, k + 1
            except _JSExprFail:
                # F534: the triple's target is a .bind-bound SLICE
                # function (e.g. `[].slice.bind(['f1','f2'], 0, 2)`)
                # -- outside the F530 join-only target class (the
                # F530 raise commits nothing on slice input -- it
                # rejects at the non-join member check before any
                # value parse), so try the F534 bound-slice triple
                # (fails closed on mismatch). Returns a _JSArr (slice
                # yields an array), not an element list.
                try:
                    elements, k = _js_parse_f534_bound_slice_triple(text, k,
                                                                     depth,
                                                                     state)
                    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                    if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                        raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                    state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                    return _JSArr(elements), k + 1
                except _JSExprFail:
                    # F537: the triple's target is a .bind-bound
                    # FILTER function -- outside the F534
                    # slice-only target class (the F534 raise commits
                    # nothing on filter input -- it rejects at the
                    # non-slice member check before any value parse),
                    # so try the F537 bound-filter triple (fails
                    # closed on mismatch). Returns a _JSArr.
                    try:
                        elements, k = _js_parse_f537_bound_filter_triple(
                            text, k, depth, state)
                        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                        if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                            raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                        state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                        return _JSArr(elements), k + 1
                    except _JSExprFail:
                        # F538: the triple's target is a .bind-bound
                        # MAP function -- outside the F537
                        # filter-only target class (the F537 raise
                        # commits nothing on map input -- it rejects
                        # at the non-filter member check before any
                        # value parse), so try the F538 bound-map
                        # triple (fails closed on mismatch). Returns
                        # a _JSArr.
                        try:
                            elements, k = _js_parse_f538_bound_map_triple(
                                text, k, depth, state)
                            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                            if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                                raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                            state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                            return _JSArr(elements), k + 1
                        except _JSExprFail:
                            # F553: the triple's target is a .bind-bound
                            # REDUCE function -- outside the F538
                            # map-only target class (the F538 raise
                            # commits nothing on reduce input -- it
                            # rejects at the non-map member check
                            # before any value parse), so try the F553
                            # bound-reduce triple (fails closed on
                            # mismatch). Returns the folded string,
                            # not an element list.
                            try:
                                # F553 reduce: INIT resolution needs the
                                # call's argsList, so this parses the
                                # whole `BOUND, thisArg, argsList`
                                # shape (node-exact, v24.20.0).
                                result, k = _js_parse_bound_reduce_call(
                                    text, k, depth, state)
                                k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                                if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                                    raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                                state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                                return result, k + 1
                            except _JSExprFail:
                                # F554: the triple's target is a
                                # .bind-bound FLATMAP function --
                                # outside the F553 reduce-only target
                                # class (the F553 raise commits nothing
                                # on flatMap input -- it rejects at the
                                # non-reduce member check before any
                                # value parse), so try the F554
                                # bound-flatMap triple (fails closed on
                                # mismatch). Returns a _JSArr.
                                try:
                                    elements, k = _js_parse_f554_bound_flatmap_triple(
                                        text, k, depth, state)
                                    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                                    if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                                        raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                                    state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                                    return _JSArr(elements), k + 1
                                except _JSExprFail:
                                    # F555: the triple's target is a
                                    # .bind-bound FIND function --
                                    # outside the F554 flatMap-only
                                    # target class (the F554 raise
                                    # commits nothing on find input --
                                    # it rejects at the non-flatMap
                                    # member check before any value
                                    # parse), so try the F555 bound-find
                                    # triple (fails closed on mismatch).
                                    # Returns the found string, not an
                                    # element list.
                                    try:
                                        result, k = _js_parse_f555_bound_find_triple(
                                            text, k, depth, state)
                                        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                                        if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                                            raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                                        state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                                        return result, k + 1
                                    except _JSExprFail:
                                        # F542: the triple's target is a Proxy
                                        # apply-trap target -- outside the F555
                                        # find-only target class (the F555 raise
                                        # commits nothing on Proxy input -- it
                                        # rejects at the non-find receiver check
                                        # before any value parse), so try the
                                        # F542 proxy-apply triple (fails closed on
                                        # mismatch). Returns the delegated string.
                                        try:
                                            result, k = _js_parse_f542_proxy_apply_triple(
                                                text, k, depth, state)
                                            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                                            if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                                                raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                                            state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                                            return result, k + 1
                                        except _JSExprFail:
                                            # F556: the triple's target is a
                                            # .bind-bound ARROW/FUNCTION value
                                            # ((FN).bind(BT, ...)) -- outside
                                            # the F542 Proxy-only target class
                                            # (the F542 raise commits nothing
                                            # on arrow input -- it rejects at
                                            # the non-Proxy receiver check
                                            # before any value parse), so try
                                            # the F556 bound-arrow triple
                                            # (fails closed on mismatch).
                                            # Returns the applied string.
                                            result, k = _js_parse_f556_bound_arrow_triple(
                                                text, k, depth, state)
                                            k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
                                            if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
                                                raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
                                            state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
                                            return result, k + 1
    k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
    if k >= n or text[k] != ")":
        raise _JSExprFail("F513 bind call arity")
    state["ops"] += 1  # the bind indirection is obfuscation-shaped
    return _JSArr(elements), k + 1


def _js_parse_f521_call_dispatch(text, paren, depth, state):
    """Parse `Reflect.apply.call(Reflect, BOUND, thisArg, argsList)` at the
    `.call(` paren (F521).

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): `Reflect.apply.call(Reflect, T,
    thisArg, argsList)` is exactly `Reflect.apply(T, thisArg, argsList)`
    -- Function.prototype.call invokes Reflect.apply with Reflect as the
    receiver (ignored by the engine, so only the literal `Reflect`
    namespace is modeled; anything else fails closed, conservative)
    and (T, thisArg, argsList) as the arguments. The target must be
    the F521 `.bind`-bound concat class, the F530 `.bind`-bound join
    class, the F534 `.bind`-bound slice class, the F537 `.bind`-bound
    filter class, the F538 `.bind`-bound map class, the F553
    `.bind`-bound reduce class, the F554 `.bind`-bound flatMap class,
    the F555 `.bind`-bound find class, the F542 Proxy apply-trap
    target, the F556 `.bind`-bound arrow/function class, or a plain
    F519-class function value (F544 --
    additive body over declared params, literal-or-spread argsArray;
    F569 adds the F546 enumerating-body and F558 destructuring-body
    targets); any other target fails closed here -- call-indirection
    with other targets is a documented residual, not filed. F590:
    the call receiver is the literal `Reflect` (canonical) or the
    literal `null` -- Function.prototype.call's receiver is
    unobservable when invoking the built-in Reflect.apply (its this
    value cannot affect behavior), so `Reflect.apply.call(null, T,
    ...)` desugars node-exactly to the canonical form
    (v24.20.0). Any other receiver (`undefined`, `void 0`,
    arbitrary expressions) fails closed -- conservative,
    documented. The directive-prologue skip (F570) composes here
    through the shared `_js_parse_fn_enum_body_target` /
    `_js_parse_fn_destructure_body_target` parsers. Returns
    (value, next_index) without the postfix chain (the caller applies
    it). Never evals.
    """
    n = len(text)
    j = _js_skip_ws(text, paren + 1)
    if text.startswith("Reflect", j) and (
            j + 7 >= n or text[j + 7] not in _JS_IDENT_CONT):
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 7)
    elif text.startswith("null", j) and (
            j + 4 >= n or text[j + 4] not in _JS_IDENT_CONT):
        # F590: the null receiver desugars node-exactly to the
        # canonical Reflect receiver (the built-in ignores its
        # this). Anything else fails closed.
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 4)
    else:
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 call receiver not Reflect")
    if j >= n or text[j] != ",":
        raise _JSExprFail("F521 call dispatch without target")
    # The bound-target triple parsers, tried in order: F521 (concat),
    # F530 (join), F534 (slice), F537 (filter), F538 (map), F553
    # (reduce), F554 (flatMap), F555 (find), F542 (Proxy apply-trap).
    # Each raises _JSExprFail on mismatch -- and each rejects at its
    # member/receiver check before any value parse, so a raise commits
    # nothing the next parser needs -- making this loop exactly the old
    # nested-fallback chain, flattened (the nesting hit Python's static
    # block limit). kind is "arr" when the triple yields an element
    # list (wrapped in _JSArr) and "str" when it yields the scalar
    # directly. The arity check sits inside the try, as before: a
    # parsed-but-arity-bad triple falls through to the next parser,
    # and every later parser rejects the earlier target classes at
    # its member check, so no input changes verdict.
    # F553 reduce goes first with its dedicated call parser: INIT
    # resolution needs the call's argsList (bound INIT wins, else the
    # first call arg becomes INIT -- node v24.20.0), so it parses the
    # whole `BOUND, thisArg, argsList` shape itself. The raise commits
    # nothing on non-reduce input (it rejects at the non-reduce
    # member check before any value parse), so the fallthrough is
    # clean.
    try:
        _val, _nj = _js_parse_bound_reduce_call(
            text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1), depth, state)
        _nj = _js_skip_ws(text, _nj)
        if _nj >= n or text[_nj] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 call dispatch arity")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the call indirection is obfuscation-shaped
        return _val, _nj + 1
    except _JSExprFail:
        pass
    _bound_triples = (
        (_js_parse_f521_bound_triple, "arr"),
        (_js_parse_f530_bound_join_triple, "str"),
        (_js_parse_f534_bound_slice_triple, "arr"),
        (_js_parse_f537_bound_filter_triple, "arr"),
        (_js_parse_f538_bound_map_triple, "arr"),
        (_js_parse_f554_bound_flatmap_triple, "arr"),
        (_js_parse_f555_bound_find_triple, "str"),
        (_js_parse_f542_proxy_apply_triple, "str"),
    )
    for _tp, _kind in _bound_triples:
        try:
            _val, _nj = _tp(text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1), depth, state)
            _nj = _js_skip_ws(text, _nj)
            if _nj >= n or text[_nj] != ")":
                raise _JSExprFail("F521 call dispatch arity")
        except _JSExprFail:
            continue
        state["ops"] += 1  # the call indirection is obfuscation-shaped
        if _kind == "arr":
            return _JSArr(_val), _nj + 1
        return _val, _nj + 1
    # F544 (wave-368 lane 3): the target is a plain
    # F519-class function value (arrow or function-expr
    # with an additive body over declared params), not a
    # bound target. Desugars to the direct form:
    # Reflect.apply.call(Reflect, FN, thisArg, argsList)
    # is exactly Reflect.apply(FN, thisArg, argsList)
    # (node v24.20.0: Function.prototype.call invokes
    # Reflect.apply with (T, thisArg, argsList)). The
    # argsArray admits the F544 spread form. F569
    # (wave-369 lane 7): arguments-enumerating
    # (F546) and destructuring-pattern (F558)
    # bodies are also modeled here -- the
    # F544xF546 / F544xF558 compositions desugar
    # to the direct form (node v24.20.0).
    # Disjoint from the F537/F538/F542 bound/proxy
    # shapes above (those require .bind/Proxy
    # receivers), so no previously-decoded input
    # changes behavior.
    try:
        _tj = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        _c_mode = "additive"
        _c_enum_kind = None
        _c_enum_payload = None
        _c_dest = None
        try:
            params, body, _tj = _js_parse_iife_fn_target(
                text, _tj, depth, state)
        except _JSExprFail:
            # Not an IIFE-evaluated target (F522) -- fall
            # through to the literal arrow/fn-expr target
            # (F519). The IIFE parse only succeeds where the
            # literal parse fails (it requires the trailing
            # `()` invocation), so no previously-decoded
            # input changes behavior.
            try:
                params, body, _tj = _js_parse_fn_target(
                    text, _tj, depth, state)
            except _JSExprFail:
                # F569: the target is a function-valued
                # target with an arguments-enumerating
                # body (F546) or a destructuring-pattern
                # body (F558). Each class rejects the
                # others' shapes chargeless, so this path
                # only accepts inputs that previously
                # failed closed -- no previously-decoded
                # input changes behavior.
                try:
                    _c_enum_kind, _c_enum_payload, _tj = \
                        _js_parse_fn_enum_body_target(
                            text, _tj, depth, state)
                    _c_mode = "enum"
                    params = []
                except _JSExprFail:
                    _cd_names, _cd_keys, _cd_body, _tj = \
                        _js_parse_fn_destructure_body_target(
                            text, _tj, depth, state)
                    _c_dest = (_cd_names, _cd_keys, _cd_body)
                    _c_mode = "destructure"
                    params = []
        _tj = _js_skip_ws(text, _tj)
        if _tj >= n or text[_tj] != ",":
            raise _JSExprFail("F544 call dispatch without thisArg")
        _, _tj = _js_parse_ignored_value(
            text, _js_skip_ws(text, _tj + 1), depth, state)
        _tj = _js_skip_ws(text, _tj)
        if _tj >= n or text[_tj] != ",":
            raise _JSExprFail("F544 call dispatch without argsArray")
        _tj = _js_skip_ws(text, _tj + 1)
        if _tj >= n or text[_tj] != "[":
            raise _JSExprFail("F544 call dispatch argsArray not array")
        elements, _tj = _js_parse_f519_apply_args_array(
            text, _tj + 1, state)
        if _c_mode == "enum":
            result = _js_apply_enum_body_result(
                _c_enum_kind, _c_enum_payload, elements)
        elif _c_mode == "destructure":
            result = _js_apply_destructure_body_result(
                _c_dest[0], _c_dest[1], _c_dest[2],
                elements, depth, state)
        else:
            # F582: engine-identical to the direct F519 form.
            elements = _js_f582_truncate_arity(
                elements, params, "F544 call dispatch fewer args")
            result = "".join(elements[idx] for idx in body)
        _tj = _js_skip_ws(text, _tj)
        if _tj >= n or text[_tj] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("F544 call dispatch with extra args")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the call indirection is obfuscation-shaped
        return result, _tj + 1
    except _JSExprFail:
        # F556: the target is a .bind-bound ARROW/FUNCTION value
        # ((FN).bind(BT, ...)) -- outside the F544 plain-fn target
        # class (the F544 parse commits nothing on bound-arrow input
        # -- the fn-target parse fails at the `.bind` member before
        # any value parse), so try the F556 bound-arrow triple (fails
        # closed on mismatch). Returns the applied string.
        result, j = _js_parse_f556_bound_arrow_triple(
            text, _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1), depth, state)
        j = _js_skip_ws(text, j)
        if j >= n or text[j] != ")":
            raise _JSExprFail("F521 call dispatch arity")
        state["ops"] += 1  # the call indirection is obfuscation-shaped
        return result, j + 1


def _js_parse_f530_bound_join_target(text, j, depth, state):
    """Parse a `.bind`-bound join function value at text[j] (F530).

    Node-exact desugar (v24.20.0): `[].join.bind(BINDTHIS, SEP)` (or
    `Array.prototype.join.bind(BINDTHIS, SEP)` -- node: `[].join ===`
    `Array.prototype.join`) is the join function with its this-value
    fixed to BINDTHIS and the separator pre-bound; invoking it with
    call-args C is exactly `BINDTHIS.join(SEP)` -- the engine ignores
    every join argument after the first, and the first is already bound,
    so C is irrelevant (node v24.20.0 verified). Returns
    (bind_this_elements, separator, next_index) where next_index is
    just after the bind call's closing paren. Never evals.

    Bounds (all fail closed -- rejected, never mis-decoded):
    - the bound function must be a join reference (`[].join` or
      `Array.prototype.join`, ws-tolerant, ident-boundary-checked);
      `.bind` on any other function fails closed -- this is the filed
      F530 scope (join-family bound targets only; concat-family is
      F521, a different builtin per the F500/F505 precedent).
    - the `.bind` member is the dot form, the F490 `?.` form, or the
      F482 bracket-string form (all engine-identical, node v24.20.0
      verified); the inner `join` member is dot-only (F506 precedent).
    - BINDTHIS is an array literal or Array-constructor form with
      all-string elements (the F506 thisArg classes); anything else
      fails closed. Numeric elements decode node-exactly via the
      F475 _JSNumStr machinery (consistent with F521's isinstance
      check and `_js_apply_method_values`' join) -- they are not a
      fail-closed boundary, just never credential-shaped.
    - exactly ONE bound arg, the separator, must be a string literal
      (F454 grammar); zero bound args (separator-via-argsList), extra
      bound args, and non-literal separators fail closed (documented
      scope boundary -- the decoder never guesses the separator).
    """
    n = len(text)
    if j < n and text[j] == "[":
        # F504-class receiver: `[].join`.
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, j + 1)
        if k >= n or text[k] != "]":
            raise _JSExprFail("F530 bound target non-empty receiver")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("F530 bound target without member dot")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if not text.startswith("join", k):
            raise _JSExprFail("F530 bound target non-join")
        k2 = k + len("join")
        if k2 < n and text[k2] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F530 joinx is not join")
        j = k2
    elif text.startswith("Array", j):
        # F469-class receiver: `Array.prototype.join`.
        k = j + len("Array")
        if k < n and text[k] in _JS_IDENT_CONT:
            raise _JSExprFail("F530 Arrayx is not Array")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k)
        if k >= n or text[k] != ".":
            raise _JSExprFail("F530 Array without dot")
        k = _js_skip_ws(text, k + 1)
        if not text.startswith("prototype", k):
            raise _JSExprFail("F530 Array non-prototype")
        k2 = k + len("prototype")
